      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A YOUNG man at Niagara, having been crossed In love, walked out to the precipice, gave one lingering look at the gulf beneath him, and—went home. His body was found next morning—in bed. WIFE (who is always ailing) : "You will bury me by the side of my first husband, won't you, John I" Husband: "With pleasure, my dear."</p>
        <p>- -- • - - SPRATTS : "Miss Elder is much older than I thought." Hunker! "Impossible !" Spratts: "Well, I asked her if she had read Esop's Fables, and she said she read them when they first came out.,, FATHER "What would you advise me to do with my son? His pronunciation is perfectly terrible." Teacher: "Get him a position as porter at a railway station at once." A POVINCIAL journal inserts the following in. puff: "We have no pity for the man who blunders right into the arms of a fierce creditor simply because he is too mean to buy a pair of Highsite's glasses." DAUGHTER; "No, fate r, I cannot nh,rry that man. He has red hair.' Father: "But, my dear daughter' that objection doesn't amount to any- thing. Don't you notice that he is almost bald, and in a short time will not have a single zed hair on his head ?" .</p>
        <p>CITIZEN: "Why are you trying to shoot that dog?" Policeman: "He's mad." "How do you know he's mad ?" "He refused water." "Lon- don water r " Yes." ".Bah ! That's no sign." DooTou : Well, my fine little fellow, you have got quite well again I was sure that the pills left for you would cure you. How did you take them, in water or in cake ?'' "Oh, I used them in my pop-gun." GIRLS, be very shy about marrying a man who can suppress a full-grown sneeze in church, lt indicates a bull headed amount of willpower which will interfere with your happiness some day. "I TELL you, Susan, that I will commit suloide if you don't have me." "Well, Thomas, as soon as you have given me that proof of your affection I will believe that you love me." " WEIY, Tom, my dear boy, how much older you look !" "All right ! The fact is, I never was so old in all my life." HOSTESS "Miss Telfer has no partner for this 'waltz; you will not mind dancing with her instead of with me?" He (anxious to be very "On the contrary, I shall be delighted." COUNT DE COSMOPOLIS : "Zen mademoiselle do not feel ze indifference for me?" Miss Wealthful: " No ; I've been awfully interested in you since papa told me you were an adventurer. It's so jolly! Tell me about your escapes and all that." AT A SCHOOL EXAM.—lnspector "What do you see above your head when you are in the open air ?" Scholar: "The sky." Inspector : "And what do you see when the sky is covered with clouds I" Scholar: "My umbrella."</p>
        <p>"TEAT'S a very lovely baby of yours, Lawson. I wonder what he will develop into?" "Well, if we can judge of the future by the present, I think he will be a town crier when he grows up." "RicnEs take unto themselves wings, and fly away," said the teacher, "What kind of riches is meant ?" And the smart boy at the foot of the class said: "They must be ostriches." Surroß : "Sir, you are undoubtedly aware of the object of my visit ?" Father: "I believe you desire to make my daughter happy. Do you really mean it'!" Suitor: " Unquestionably." Father: "Well, don't marry her, then." "NOT To - DAY, THINK You!" Servant: "Please, ma'am, there's a poor man at the door with wooden legs." Young Housewife: "Why, Babette, what can we do with wooden legs? Tell him we don't want any." MAGISTRATE (sternly): "This is your sixth appearaece here." Impudent Prisoner: "Quite right, guy'nor, go up top !" MRS. NICKER (whispering to her husband) : "Oh, John, I believe there's a burglar in the room routing about your clothes Mr. N. : "Well, it doesn't matter. You've been through my pockets already, haven't you." HE : "And did you see Monte Carlo while you were at Nice ?' She: "No; papa called on him, I believe, but from his disappointed appearance when he returned to the hotel, I think Mr. Carlo must have been out." MRS. STRONGMIND : "Why don't you go to -work?" Tramp: " Pease, mum, I made .a solemn vow twenty years ago, that I'd never do another stroke of work till women was paid th' same wages as men." Gets a trifle. Miss HAYDON "Miss Palmer, I mast ask you to explain yourself. I understand that you told Mrs. Palmer that my brother George was the black sheep of the family." Miss Palmer : "My dear Miss Hayden, it was merely a slip of the tongue, I assure you. I meant to say white sheep.'" A ScarcitsiAsi waited upon one of the most celebrated teachers of the German flute, desiring to know on what terms he would give him a few lessons. The flute player informA him that he charged two guineas for the first month and one guinea for the second. "Then," replied the Scot, "I'll begin the second month !" "I HAVEN'T seen your wife out lately, Mr. •Goodheart." "No, she keeps at home these days." "Is she ailing?" "No. The fact of the matter is, a week ago I took home two of the handsomest bonnets I could rind in town, and told her she might have her choice between them. She has been busy day and night ever since trying to make up her mind, and was as undecided as ever when I came away this morning." "You ought to help her out of the dilemma." "How can I?" "Why, take one of them and carry it back to the shop. That will be the one she'll want." DURING an equestrian performance a number of ladies in the fi-ont stood up, thus obstructing the view of those persons who were seated. In vain were they collectively requested to sit down, till at last a happy thought occurred to one of the sufferers. He called out in measured tones, " Will the pretty lady in front kindly sit down ?" whereupon about fifty old women briskly seated themselves. "You don't call on Miss Cutting any more, I hear, Blobber." "No.' "Did she reject you ?" "Not exactly, but when I first began calling there was a mat at the door with the word Welcome' woven in it, and a motto on the wall that read, 'Let Us Love One Another.' Later I noticed that the doormat was changed for one that said 'Wipe Your Feet,' and a motto declaring that Early to Bed and Early to Rise Make You Healthy Wealthy, and Wise,' had the place of the other one." A Lirri.E incident of a late occurrence at a Lon- don hotel is only more amusing than many others of the sort in that we can vouch for its truth. An American visitor, ruffled at being pressed for settlement of his bill, threatened to leave. "You don't stir from this hotel until you have paid up," protested the manager. "Just put that in writ- ing and I'll stay here for the remainder of my days," was the retort. The visitor is still at the hotel. JEWELLER: "Yes, sir; I will engrave anything you wish on this ring without extra charge." Young Man: "Inscribe on it From George to Alice.'" Jeweller: "inn. The lady is your sister, maybe?" Young Man: "The fact is, this is an engagement rin Jeweller: " Ah, my young friend, I have had considerable experience in engagement rings, and I would suggest that the inscription be simply, From George.' Then it will do for another time, you know." IT is dangerous to make a confession unless one really means it, and we may add that it is some- times dangerous to take a confession as honestly meant. A husband and wife, between whom a little unpleasant passage had occurred, had made the matter up, and the wile said, as if to clear her conscience :—" Oh, well, I suppose that I have my faults.' "Yes, my dear," said the husband. " What ? " "I simply said 'yes.'" "That I have my faults'" exclaimed the wife, indignantly. "What are they. I should like to know?" "Well, to begin with —" "No. I don't want to hear." EPISTOLARY PERPLEXITIES. —"ln short, X. is a great personage. Suppose I address him: My honoured master '?' " Nonsense ! a scamp like that l" "Say, what would you put?" "I should simply write: "My dear colleague.'</p>
      </div>
