      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>" SWEE'PNESS and Light"—A sponge-cake. SPIRITUAL Fire—An alcohol lamp. THE Woman in White—Lot's wife. MORE Dead Than Alive—Heroes. A Goon Dressing Down—Swan's. UNDER a Cloud—Umbrellas. TRIAL Balances—The scales of justic Ws well enough to aim high, but you don't want to forget to take into consideration the range of your gun. WORLDLY Wise—A geographer. A RECEIVING Teller—The gossip. LIFE is full of trials, but the lawyer confronts them witn the smile of a hero. MAKES Haste Slowly—The messenger boy. REPUTATION may be a bubble ; but it takes a long time to blow it. People talk a great deal about the lost art of conversation. ABOUT the most aggravating thing that can happen to a man is to go contrary to his wife's wishes and then fail. "I can't imagine why they have female inspectors of customs." "To get all the things back into women's trunks, of course." NOTHING disgusts a man so at lack of punctual- ity as to arrive at a meeting-place fifteen minutes late and find his friend not there yet. ITS DISADVANTAGES. Boning: Knight become so narrow-minded?" - "By minding his own business.'' hAD BEEN THROUGH IT.—Teacher: "What little boy can tell me the name of the worst nation on earth? Bobby?" Bobby: "Vaccination." A USEFUL MEMBER.—" Mamma: "Have I an eye-tooth?" "Yes, Johnny. Why 2" "Why, because if I have I can't see anything with it." LITTLE Boy (with swollen face) : "Oh, dear! I wish I was grandpa, or else the baby." Mother: "Why?" Little Boy: " Giandpa's teeth are all gone, and baby's hasn't come yet." MODEL BOOKKEEPING.—PrincipaI: "You had a deficit in the cash account yesterday. Has the error been discovered?" Clerk : "The error, yes ; but—not the cash " SOME EXCUSE.— Purchaser : "You said this horse was afraid of nothing. He has just shied at a girl on a bicycle. Dealer: Oh, well, perhaps she had on a divided skirt." SURE TO PLEASE HlM.—Wife: "Let's go to the Sunday concert." Husband: "Urn—l'm afraid I won't like it." Wife : "Yes, you will. The music isn't religious at all; it's quite wicked." POETRY, a waxed moustache, mystery, long hair, and a sweet tenor voice will often make a woman feel as if there was only a sheet of tissue paper between heaven and herself; but it is the man with the wart on his nose and six figures in his bank balance who scoops her in and makes her happy ever afterwards. You can't have your penny and your cake; but, if you are sufficiently able and unscrupulous, you can, as a rule, have your penny and someone else's cake. HAPPINESS AT SMALL COST. Home-seeker: "What miserably imperfect glass you have in these windows! Every object is horribly dis- torted." Agent: "Yes, ma am ; that's so that you may feel better satisfied with yourself when you look through those windows at your neigh- bours." A DIFFICULT QUESTION.—" They say a giraffe sells for five thousand dollars." "That's a good deal of money for a giraffe. How much is that a yard on the average ? " MARRIAGE WOULD CURE HIM.—Mr. Gumpps : "That boy will never be good for anything until he marries." Mrs. Gumpps : "I suppose not." Mr. Gumpps : "No. He must get over the habit of hanging around the house." WELL DlVlDED.—Mistress: "I suppose you had a fine time at the wedding, Mary Ann?" Mary Ann: " Indade an" we had, mum. We danced 'til the brith ov loife wasn't lift in us; an' at sup- per the broide's cake was broke over her head an' into a Iffindhred halves." A LITTLE GIRL who had been very observant of her parents' mode of exhibiting their charity, when asked what generosity was, answered : "It is giving to the poor all the old stuff you don't want yourself." MAKE A BAD MATTER WORSE. —Mrs. De Bride was entertaining callers. After they left she remarked to her husband: "I hope they didn't see my walking shoes lying there. They would think me very untidy if they did." "Oh, if they saw them, they probably thought they were mine," answered her husband, in a consoling tone. And she hasn't spoken to him since. NOT SUFFICIENTLY DEFINITE. Mother : "I told you to give your sister half of that apple, and you have eaten it all yourself." Small Son: "I offered it to her, and she sucked it a little, and then pushed it away," "She says you didn't." "Oh, you mean that sister? I thought you meant the baby." COULDN'T STAND IT.—COaChMan : "I am going to leave, sir. I can't stand the missus." Em- ployer : "Too strict, is she ? " Coachman : "Yes, sir. She keeps forgetting that I can leave any time, and bullies me just as if I was you." BURGLAR PTOOF.—An old maid is so much afraid of robbers that she stews pepper under her bed every time she retires for the night. When ques- tioned on the subject, she said : "Any scamp attempting to hide there will betray himself through having to sneeze." A BAD QUESTION.—Mrs, Hobson de Clarke (who flatters herself on her youthful appearance) : "You would scarcely think, Mr. Darnley, that the stalwart young fellow by the piano is my son, would you?" Dumley (en:tautly) : "No, indeed, Mrs. Clarke; it seems absolutely impossible. .Ah —er—is he your eldest son ? " OLD GENTLEMAN (benevolently) : "Let me see. I believe you are the boy I bought a paper of yesterday, when I didn't have change. I owe you a halfpenny. Here it is." Newsboy (who isn't the boy): "Never mind, mister. Keep it for yen honesty f" AT a duel the combatants discharged their pistols without effect, whereupon one of the seconds interfered, and proposed that the duellists should shake hands. To this the other second objected as unnecessary. "Their hands," said he, "have been shaking this half-hour." SPINSTER: "You want a pretty good price for that parrot." Dealer : " Yes ; but then the bird has had such an aristocratic training." Spinster : "How do you know?" Dealer : "Because he always talks when anyone begins to sing." BEGGING OFF. —Grandpa : "What Don't like fat? If you don't eat fat, you will grow up as thin as a rail." Little Grandson.: "When I grow up, I want to earn lots of money, oas to take care of papa and mamma and you and grandma." "Grandpa's own grandson, so he is! But what has that to do with fat ?" "I can get an awful big ,salary as a living skeleton, you know, grand- His Sinat-REsrEcT.—Tramp : "All my troubles come from card-playin', mum. I lost me self- respect, an' then I didn't care what became of me." Housekeeper (sympathetically) : "Poor man ! I should think you wo,i'd have lost your self- respect." Tramp : "Yes, mum. A man can have no self-respect w'en he always loses." A SERIOUS Loss.—Mistress " "What did you do with that old brown dress that hung in my closet?" Domestic : "You told me to get rid of all the rags, ma'am, and so I gave it to the ragman. Mistress: "Goodnees me birsw do you suppose I am ever to get any new cheerios if I haven't an old dress to put on when my husband comes home?" BLACK PILLS.—" My dear," said Mr. Finnicky, "I don't think those pills I have been taking have done me much good." "Why, you haven't taken any for three weeks !" "Yes, I have; I've swallowed one three times a day, as directed." "You have? Then why is it that there are as many left as there were three weeks ago? What box have you been taking them from?" "This one—marked for me." "Dear me, John! That is my shoe-button box !" ETHEL : "Papa, did you notice that gentleman pass us, with the sad, dejected expression on his face ? " Papa : "Yes, dear—an interesting face, truly." Etbufil : "I wonder what misfortune could have befallen'him to cause that sad, pitiful expres- sion, those deep lines of care, and that far-away look in his eyes. Do you suppose that death has robbed him of some loved one ? " Papa "No, little one; he is the editor of a column of 'wit and humour' in a local paper."</p>
