      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, J UNE 1. 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,— There is not a great deal of difference between this and last year's coats. The chief feature of those for spring and early summer wear is the abbreviated basque. The new basque is quite fluted at the back by reason of the excessive slope at each seam from the waist downwards, but the front lies flat and close to the figure. With open coats, where there are wide revers and lace jabots in the Louis Quinze style, the basque sometimes drops a trifle longer in front, but more usually it is of the same depth all round. Fancy collars will be worn on coats, bodices, and blouses, and the more convenient way to make these is to mount the edge on tape drawn a trifle tighter than the collar, to enable it to be tacked on to the garment. Some delightful costumes are being made in heliotrope. A charming one in heliotrope poil de chevre had the collar and loose fronts of velvet. There was a justancorps ala Henri 11. in fawn cloth, embroidered with white satin flowers, gold thread, and sequins. A jabot of fine lace was worn. The sleeves were very full, and were turned back at the cuffs with the fawn cloth. The parasol was of light coloured shot silk. There was also a pretty square crowned toque, with black lace brim, jetted wings, and roses.</p>
        <p>A hat which might easily be ....nsferred into a bonnet is made of jet. The design is quite novel, the front brim being turned up in a square-looking way. This only needs the addition of strings to make a delightful and most Decoming capote for a young married woman. The material is jet. At each side of the back is arranged ecru-tinted lace in aigrette form, with a fan-like arrangement of the lace drooping over the hair, the rosette being caught in the centre with a paste brooch. Talking of rosettes—though these, of course, are of lace— reminds me that the rosette of to-day must be of gigantic proportions. A circle of silk or velvet, some nine or 10 inches in diameter, is necessary to the formation of what are termed "cabbage" rosettes, and anything smaller than this is con- sidered insignificant, and to savour of yesteryear. Not the least pretty capote I have seen was made of voluminous yellow and violet pansies. It was headed with a marabout aigrette, and* the pansies rested on a wreath of green leaves.</p>
        <p>Possibly you may have some of last year's bodices which are worth while adapting to this year's fashions. There are plenty of ways of doing them up. Suppose, for example, the gown we have to deal with is one of the fine black crepons in vogue last summer, and that it is made with Eton fronts, and a vest of gold-coloured Pongee, the skirt being fastened over the bodice just a shade below the waist-line: it would make it look almost like another dress if the fronts were rounded away into a very short zouave, and edged with jet passe- menterie, and the vest replaced by one of cream lace, lined with pale green satin, the lace being plain where it is put into the side-seams, but pleated in front and overhanging the belt a little, two bands of folded black satin ribbon crossing the pleats between the bust and collar. Then the skirt should be made to hook well up round the waist under a belt of satin ribbon, with a bow at the back, and the collar should be finished with a band of ribbon and a bow. The sleeves, which will probably be fairly full, should be taken out, inter- lined with stiff muslin, and put in again with two or three rows of gathering at the top. These alterations need not be expensive, as coloured lining satin can be bought for a shilling a yard, and the cost of the lace and passementerie need not amount to more than five shillings at the outside. The following is a pretty gown. It is made of string-coloured canvas, with very full skirt. The bodice is of pink-mauve silk, striped with deep mauve satin. There is a V-shaped band of white satin studded 'WI gold seo”:- Bretelles, collar.</p>
        <p>and cuffs arc of pale fawn chiffon; and the sash and ends in front of the same. Large black fancy straw hat may be worn with this gown, covered with chiffon, and trimmed with black plumes and paradise feathers. A band of passementerie passes round the crown. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>BE WISE IN</p>
        <p>Sufferers from Grsvel, Lumbago. Piles, Pains in the Back, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint. Diseases of Kidneys, Bladder Stone. Sciatica, Rheumatism amq Gout will find a positive Cure in Holrovd's Gravel Pills. Try a small box and It not satisfied your money will be returned. Price is. of all Chemists, or post free for 12 stamps, from HOLROYD'S Medical Hall, Checkheaton, Yorks. Dol't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor and a box will be sent re't ros.,—Agr.</p>
