      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>An excellent quality of illumination has been made from peat. The early Egyptian lamps were of granite, alabaster and terracotta IA ton of diamonds is worth about seven million pounds. The United States uses nearly oae-half of the quinine used the world. Almost every day the Queen of Italy has a spin on her bics cle. Diaper was first made at Ypres, in Flanders, it is be- lieved before 1,200. Many coloured ribbons were worn on the hair of ladies from 1426 to 1500. The chance of two finger-prints being alike is not one in 64,000,000,000. In 1812 there were 39,765 persons in Great Britain paying taxes on incomes greater than £2OO ; now the number is 210,430. Tumblers of nearly the same shape and dimensions as those employed to-day have been found in great num- bers in Pompeii. They were of gold, silver, glass,agate, marble and other semi-precious stone.</p>
        <p>Improving the Complexion. It is said that in Paris every lady who is anxious to improve her complexion drinks goat's milk during the months of May and June. Vendors go through the streets with nanny in leading strings, and the women who improve their complexions every year are served at the doorsteps. Scciety women diet themselves on goat' s milk at the end of the social season instead of drugging themselves. This animal food is a source of nourishment, and is held to be one of the best reme- dies for a muddy and unwholesome-looking complexion.</p>
        <p>" Is Marriage a Failure ?" The following extraordinary event has recently been chronicled :—William Cox, of Monticello, attended a dinner at which bat three of his living wives, and he is only 38 years of age. His first wife was Miss Minerva Smith, by whom he had nine children, and was soon afterwards divorced. He then married Miss Julia Stephens, and was soon divorced from her. He then wedded his present wife, seven children blessing their home. His first two wives have married again, and there is now a feeling of perfect harmony and goodwill existing between them. Mr. Cox sometimes invires his two former helpmeets tc dine with him, and they res- pond, and the time passes without dissatisfaction or unpleasantness. Mr. Cox was married at the early age of 14.</p>
        <p>Why Londoners are Weary. We notice, with extreme regret, says "Science Sift- ings," the increasing height, notwithstaniing putalic protest, of London buildings. They shut out fresh air and sunlight. The necessity of these to healtn and happiness seems to be escaping modern notice, as do other hygienic considerations. Nature has furnished life-giving and health-producing agents in abundance, and placed them at our hand. But through ignorance of the laws which govern life and health, we reject these agents. We shut ourselves up in close roomstand poison the whole system by breathing a vitiated atmosphere. If the apartment is cold we simply close the door cx window. Would it not be more sensible to build a fire, and then let the fresh air come in? This would give the desired warmth, and at the same time afford a good papply of oxygen for the lungs. It is next thing to a stupid, and certainly very bad economy, to exclude these life-giving agents from our homes, and to take into our lungs, instead, the poisonous exhalations that are thrown off:tram our own bodies. Akin to the folly referred to is that of shutting out sunlight. Ignorance, toe, is at the bottom of this, for nothing is so essential to life and health, unless it be pure air, as sunlight. Without it vegetable life, as well as animal, must wither and die. S 3 far as health is concerned is is a sort of suicide to exclude the sunlight ; and no amount of faded carpets and tapestries can be weighed in the balance against lost health. Better fade the carpet than the ruddy cheek. We know these house- plants at sight ; pale, bloodless, with a sort of wheyev or buttermilk complexion. Their " cure " is to throw open their windows,dash back the curtain,and let in the cheerful rays of light.</p>
        <p>At the Old Bailey on Mowfay, Baron Pollock sentenced a man named Richards, convicted on his own codession of wounding a girl who had refused to "keep company ' with him, to fourteen years' penal servitude. Thejudge said it was a most unfortunate thing that while otlitr crimes seemed to be becoming more rare, these offences of young men attempting to kill or wound women in consequence of some love affair were becoming extremely common.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>LONGING TO DIE.</head>
        <p>LONGING TO DIE.</p>
        <p>James Canning, formerly a soldier, pleaded guilty at the Central Criminal Court on Monday, to the murder of his sweetheart, Jane Youell, at Bermondsey. Baron Pollock said that after reading the depositions and the statement the prisoner made before the magistrate he was quite prepared to accept the plea. Nobody could be surprised and nobody could blame the prisoner for the frank avowal that he had made of his guilt. After he had been sentenced to death, Canning, addressing the Court in a firm voice said : "My sentence is just, my Lord, and I long to die; I long to die !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WHY MIL GLADSTONE DOES NOT GROW OLD.</head>
        <p>WHY MIL GLADSTONE DOES NOT GROW OLD.</p>
        <p>There are two parts of the body which, if wisely used, largely escape senile failure. These are the brain and the heart. We have all often wondered why great brain workers and statesmen like Mr. Gladstone, are able to labour with almost unimpaired mental activity and energy up to a period when, with the generality of men, most of the organs and functions of the body are in de- cay. The reason is simple. The brain remains vigor- ous to the last because its nutrition is especially pro- vided for. About middle life or a little later, the general arteries of the body begin to lose their elasticity and to slowly but surely dilate and are much less efficient car- riers of the blood to the different organs of the body. This is not the case,however, with the internal carotids, which supply the small vessels of the brain. They con- tinue to retain their elasticity,so that the blood-pressure remains higher than within the capillary area of any other organ of the body. The cerebral blood-paths tieing thus kept open,the brain tissue is kept better nourished than the other tissues. Thus they who are growing old, if they have not abused their nervous arrangements,may then have hope that their intellectual faculties will con- tinue active to the close of life. Instances in which this has been the case are not rare. They will no doubt in the future, when the art of living is more highly deve- loped, be far more common. The reason for Mr. Glad- stone's wonderful physical strength is not so easily ar- rived at. Doubtless, however, it may be accredited to the healthy methods of living which he has always pur- sued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WE PARTED AT THE ALTAR;</head>
        <p>WE PARTED AT THE ALTAR;</p>
        <p>BY LAURA JEAN LIBBEY,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Mad:Betrothal," "lone," " Parte,d by Fate," "Floribel's Lover," etc., etc.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXXI. A STRANGE REVELATION. GREAT was Mrs. Lancaster's surprise to receive a hurriedly-written note from Doris the next morn- ing stating she had been called suddenly away, and would write to her when she reached her destination. "I cannot understand this strange freak of Doris," exclaimed Mrs. Lancaster, in amazement, as she passed the note over to her husband. "Where do you suppose Doris has gone t" she cried. "It seems almost impossible to believe. Left Newport—and the season at its height— without one word as to why or where she was going. I really cannot comprehend it ; can you ?" "I never attempt to comprehend a woman's motives," returned her husband, laconically. "It would be a useless want of time and thought." Vivian Carsdale heard of Doris's departure with a strange smile on her lips. She, and she alone, understood her sudden flight. She did not make known poor Doris's story as she had threatened to do. She had merely meant to frighten we girl into acknowledging her identity. Now that her rival was vanquished, she could afford to hold her peace. There was great disappointment among the young ladies over Doris's sudden departure' for she was a general favourite, and this piqued Vivian exceedingly. Very adroitly she set herself the task of un- ravelling what seemed to her a wonderful mystery —as to how poor, dependent Doris had become a great heiress, and was known as Miss Field- ing. Little by little she drew from Mrs. Lan- caster the story of her son Karl's adventure and his romantic meeting with Doris, and their discovery subsequently that she was the long- lost heiress whom they had been seeking. She had been identified, beyond a doubt, as the? daughter of Hulbert Brandon Fielding. "But her past life ?" said Vivian, eagerly. "Did she ever—" "She told me very little of her past life," inter- rupted Mrs. Lancaster. "Doris did not like to talk about it." Vivian knew, when she heard that, that Doris had never revealed, then, that strange chapter in her past life. "It is a wonder that Mr. Thornton, the banker's son, who left the hotel recently, and Miss Fielding did not make a match," laughed Vivian. "I hear that he was so much in love with her." "Doris took a strange dislike to him," responded Mrs. Lancaster. "He was all that was gallant and handsome, and seemed to worship her. Yet she avoided him whenever it was possible." "I should not have thought that would have pleased your son, Mrs. Lancaster," laughed Vivian roguishly ; "for, if report speak truly, he has hopes in that direction. ' "Doris always declares she will never marry, so all hope would be useless in that direction," said Karl's mother. And at that moment the entrance of Karl hint- self put a stop to all further conversation on that</p>
        <p>topic. Newport, even in all its gaiety, would not have attracted Vivian thither had it not been for the hope of seeing Frederick Thornton again, and the wild delusion of fanning the old love into flame. The shock of coming face to face with Doris, whom she believed to be dead, had been a great and ter- rible blow to her, and she had realised, with this living barrier between them, that Frederick was indeed lost to her for ever. Yet it was with a feeling of intense satisfaction she had realised that, owing to the great and sur- prising change in Doris, he had failed to recognise her. It was no wonder. The Doris he had known in the past was but a timid slip of a school-girl ; the Miss Fielding he had met in later years was a society belle and a beauty. What strange fate had brought them together again, and parted them more widely than before? "This is the second time that girl has come between me and Frederick Thornton's love 1" groaned Vivian, desperately, as she paced the floor of her room excitedly. Meanwhile the days dragged slowly by; the weeks lengthened into a month, and as yet not a line had been received from Doris. Karl Lancaster and his mother were growing uneasy over the matter, while the old doctor declared that his capricious young ward was abun- dantly able to take care of herself. No need of anxiety; Doris must return soon, for her supply of pocket money, which she had taken with her, would not last for ever. Buoyed up with this hope, they waited patiently; but not so much as a line, revealing Doris's whereabouts, reached them. But let us follow Doris's letter written that night to Frederick Thornton, and see what became of it. Travelling about from place to place as he was, it was a difficult matter for letters to reach him. It was quite six weeks ere Doris's letter fell into his ihands. He looked at the square white envelope which, by its many crossed-out directions, seemed to have ft 1, followed him about so persistently, with curious eyes, wondering who his new correspondent could •'Lbe. " The best way to find out would be to open and Yee," he thought, suiting the action to the word. He saw it was a long, closely-written letter blotted by tears. "it looks like Miss Fichl ing's writing," he cried, quickly turning over the page. "Why, it is from Miss Fielding." km muttered, catching his breath hard. Like a man in a dazed, bewildered dream, Fre- derick Thornton read the startling letter through from beginning to end. What was it this letter told him? Was he mad or dreaming? Doris his neglected bride, whom he had mourned as dead, and Miss Fielding were one and the same! Oh, impossible! There was some terrible mis- take ! Again and again he read the letter through. Yes, here it was, in black and white, as plain as writ- ten words could make it, every incident brought up and lucidly explained. Great drops of per- spiration stoAl out on his face, and his hands trembled, strong man though he was. But one thought filled heart and b: am. He could claim Doris Fielding; she was his wife. Now he realised why he had been so strangely attracted toward her from the first. Had he been blind that he had not noticed her great resemblance to the young bride from whom fate had parted him so strangely hi that bitter past? The revelation was wonderful to him. He would lose no time in hurrying back to New- port to see Doris, and explain to her that which she had not given him time to explain on that fatal night—the innocent cause that had parted them, wrecking two lives. How he would kneel at her feet, and tell her how he had mourned the young bride whom he had lost so cruelly—mourning for her, refusing to be comforted; and how the first sight of her face, which had reminded him so strangely of Doris, had thrilled anew the heart he had believed dead. Despite her coldness and aversion (and Heaven knows, under the cruel mistake she was labouring under, he well knew she had reason to abhor him, innocent though he had been of any thought of deserting his poor little bride), he believed she still loved him. Through his brain flashed the desperate cry that had fallen from her lips when they had faced death together in the surf : 'Save yourself, Frederick Never mind me 1 You must not give up your life uselessly for me! Oh, save yourself for my sake—because I love you !" Six long weeks the letter—this precious letter— had been following him about. What must Doris think because she had received no reply? Should he telegraph her that it had only been received within the hour? No, no! It would be better to go on and see her in person. No telegram could tell one-half of what he had to say to her. But one eastward-bound express stopped at the little village where he was staying, and that was at six in the evening. It was not yet noon. How should he pass the intervening hours? Frederick Thornton asked himself. One heart in that quiet village was fiery and restless enough as the hours rolled by. One man paced its streets with impatience, because the warm day would not go more quickly. He counted the hours. There were still two to pass; then the train would arrive. It seemed to him the sun was slow in setting; h. N:,41,c , 1.113 tra,in roust ..a</p>
        <p>an Initir- late:H inust- do something to while away the hours until then. He could no nothing. It was impossible to detach his mind from her. He could think of nothing but the strange revela- tion contained in Doris's letter.</p>
        <p>Like one dazed, he paced up and down the streets. The sun or moon might be shining; the earth be green or brown benea h his feet; the trees be bare or full of leaf. He knew nothing, saw no- thing, understood nothing but this: He was going to see Doris again —his beautiful, peerless Doris, that had blossomed from such a frail bud into such a magnificent flower—and claim her as his wife. Men and women and children looked curiously at him as he passed along with his handsome, absorbed face. They were nothing to him—the whole world was nothing—for he was going back to claim his beautiful young bride. The two hours must be lived through somehow. At last the longed-for whistle of the incoming train from afar off fell upon his ear. "Thank Heaven!" muttered Frederick. "This has been the longest day I have ever experienced in my life." How slow the train seemed to drag along. It almost seemed to Frederick Thornton that he could walk as swiftly. The night waned at last, and the golden sun rose on a new-born day. It was fully nine in the morning ere the train steamed into Newport station. Frederick took a cab, and lost no time in reach- ing the Ocean Beach Hotel. "Were the:Lancasters here yet, and their ward ?" he asked eagerly at the hotel. Karl Lancaster, who had entered the office un- perceived, stepped forward in person to answer his queries. "We—that is, my father and mother and I—are still here; but Miss Fielding has gone," he said, slowly. "Gone !" echoed Frederick; and he gasped out the word as though it was the last he should ever speak. "Where has she gone, Karl ?" A strange flush passed over Karl Lancaster's face. "You must tell me !" Frederick cried. "I must see her 1" Karl Lancaster looked inte his friend's pale, excited face without replying. "I have the right to know, Karl," he went on, huskily. "I may as well tell you what all the world must soon know. Doris is my wife !" And, in a few brief words, he told Karl all, finishing by putting Doris's letter in his hand. Karl Lancaster's amazement can better be imag- ined than described. "Now you will tell me where Doris has gone, Karl," he cried, hoarsely. "I cannot rest night or day until I have seen her." Heaven help you to bear what I must tell you, Frederick," said Karl; "but you must bear it bravely like a man. "Doris has gone," he went on. "We do not know where to. She went away suddenly six weeks ago without saying where she was going, leaving no address behind her. She left a note, merely saying, when ,he arrived at her destination she would write. We have not heard from her since. We have written to all her friends, but we could not trace her whereabouts." A cry that was full of anguish fell from Frederick Thornton's lips. "Gone !" he repeated. "Gone I Oh, Heaven I" How dark the sky looked. How the light seemed to fade from the sun. He stood before Karl dismayed. Something like the truth flashed over Frederick Thornton. After writing that letter, which she knew full well would bring him at once to New- port, she had gone hurriedly away to avoid him, leaving no address behind her by which she could be traced. "How she must abhor me to have done that," he thought, bitterly. The blow that Karl Lancaster had received in the knowledge that Doris belonged to another, was a heavy and sore one; but he bravely put all thought of self from him and strove to comfort his friend. "The season here is drawing to a close. We would have been away two weeks ago, had it not been for the vain hope that Doris might re- turn," said Ka.rl. "We leave here for New York to-day. Will you not come with us ?" . Most of the summer guests had gone; there was nothing to stay for, and Frederick sorrowfully con- sented. Great was the consternation of both Dr. Lancas- ter and his wife when they heard Frederick Thorn- ton's strange story—more pitiful than the most pathetic romance could have been. He told them everything—omitting no detail— of how he had first met Doris by accident in the seminary grounds; how their acquaintance had ripened into friendship in the days that followed, and through pity for the lobely life of the beauti- ful, desolate child (for she was little more than that), he had invited her to the grand balL He described the sternness of Madame Delmar toward her, and Doris's fear lest madame would refuse to allow her to go. "'Then do not ask her,' I rejoined," continued Frederick, "I said, Why refuse yourself two hours of pleasure? You, who have had so little enjoyment in your life? Come, it would be a decided lark to cheat madame.' I counselled with all a young fellow's impulsive thoughtlessness, and poor Doris listened. "'The gates of the seminary close at ten; could I get back by that time, do you think, Mr. Frederick?' questioned Doris dubiously, yet with all a girl's eagerness and love of mischief, raising her laughing blue eyes to mine. "I assured her, on my honour as a gentleman, she should be back by ten. The gates should never be closed against her; I would hold myself responsible for that," continued Frederick, brash- ing his hand over his damp forehead. Well, the upshot of the matter was, that Doris and I went that night to the grand ball, Mrs. Lancaster. It was her first ball, and you know with what keen zest a young girl always enjoys her first ball. It seemed like a glimpse of Heaven to her. • "The hours flew by unheeded, and to make a long story short, we returned to the seminary just in time to see the heavy gates closed in our faces. "I shall never forget the grief of poor little Doris," said Frederick, huskily • "how she wept, and wringing her hands, declaring that madame would turn her away from the in- stitute on the morrow for what she had done— jwit as sure as fate. And, oh, how piteously the poor child cried out that she could never—never face the great, cold, cruel world, and she wished she had died before she ever consented to go to the ball. "I said, Doris, it was all my fault. With me rests the responsibility of the affair. You shall not be thrown on the mercy of the cold world. There is one way of preventing it, and that is to marry me, Doris." "'Are you really in earnest, Mr. Frederick ?" she gasped, looking up in dismay at me through her tears. "'Of course I am, Doris,' I replied. You shall be my bride within a few hours, if you "There had been no wooing, no courtship. I never kissed the pretty white face until I stood beside Doris that night at the altar, and she was pronounced my bride. "We took the midnight train for Baltimore, arriving there late the next forenoon. And now I am coming to the most pitiful part of my story, Mrs. Lancaster. When you know it all it will be easier for you to try to reconcile Doris and me— when she is found—if you will undertake so difficult a task." "Of courPe I will," she replied, heartily; "you may be quite sure of that." Now that she knew that Doris was lost to her own son Karl for ever, her next thought was to see her husband's ward happy; for she loved Doris with a true, motherly affection. " I had reached the climax of my story when I told you that on the wedding trip Doris and I went to Baltimore' arriving there at about noon.. "Leaving her by herself to rest before luncheon, I left her, strolling out into the street for the pur- pose of smoking a cigar, smiling as I recalled poor little Doris's words : "'Don't be quite an hour, please, Mr. Frederick, for I shall be so lonely until you return.' "It sounded to me more like a chil(ra speech than a woman's; but, then, my little bride was but a child, after all—she was only seventeen. "I had not walked far—l was just crossing the road—when suddenly I felt a terrible blow from behind on my head. And I knew no more. "When I regained consciousness I found myself lying upon a cot bed in hospital. "With consciousness—it was found my memory was completely shattered—all remembrance of Doris, the bride who must have waited for me in such terror and agony, was completely obliterated from my mind. I had forgotten all about my sud- den marriage—forgotten Doris completely," 'To be continued), e'</p>
      </div>
