        <p>Lord -oil --- a 00„ . osebery introduced a bill on Monday after- kit', In the Lords to strengthen the Judicial Com- " 8,8 of the Privy Council by the addition of a atiVoer or members specially versed in colonial iae"„,ll,B. His speech drew to the 'Upper House many laa,;'uere of the Commons' House, who went to see +lna the Prime Minister really was. His speech 100,. PPearance were all that could be desired. He bei's ruddy and sunburnt, and hardly, if at all, h."? is former standard of health and vigour. bee: BPeech was short and clear, and delivered in his and most deliberate style. The appeals from cora appear to be falling off and those from the onies to have doubled, so that the proposal is aecessary, as well as a fresh link to the Empire.</p>
        <p>It is now becoming more certain than ever that tin and greatest danger which the Government have to face after Whitsuntide is the Welsh QinllendrnentB to the ninth clause of the Welsh tmitireh Bill. Mr. Idloyd-George and his friends are to INZ up a very threatening position with regard tk. th„ "IS clause; end, as the Tories an; supporting WIZ, for quite different reasons, the situation may la „,c3ne of grave danger unless some arrangement 'made.</p>
        <p>04.riere is at present an unusual spectacle, for no fee is charged, but which the inhabitants tb.Plath-st., Park-lane, London, have, by virtue of Th'lr Position, unrivalled opportsnities of seeing. bo°' retinue of the Afglsan Prince resort at stated .4Ts to open thegarden of Dorchester House, and, With th eir faces turned towards Mecca, prostrate tu:selves on their carpets in prayers. The unac- tio„,;°rned spectacle not unnaturally tills the win- baa's of the houses in South-st. which closely coro- lla:I tile gardens. From one of these houses on teeetY afternoon somebody emerged, and pro- In in the most unconcealed manner to "Kodak ' kneeling Orientals. 4</p>
