      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SIR J. T. BRUNNER, M.P.</head>
        <p>SIR J. T. BRUNNER, M.P.</p>
        <p>General satisfaction has been expressed at the coafer- merit of a baronety upon Mr. John Tomlinson Brunner M.P., for the Northwich division, and it is regarded as a well-des rved recognition not only of his useful services as a keen and earnest politician, but also of the zeal and princely liberality which have characterized ins untir- ing efforts in the promotion of the intellectual and material prosperity of the people amongst whom he has for so many years lived and laboured. Many Runcorn friends despatched messages of congratulation to Sir John and Lady Brunner, and one was also sent from the Runcorn Liberal Club, in which the members signified their pleasure that such a birthday honour had been be. stowed upon him. At this point it may not be inappropriate to give a short sketch of the career of the new baronet. Sir J. T. Brnnner M.P., is the second son ot the Rev. John Brunner, a native of Zurich, who, settling in this country, was long the principal of a flourishing school for boys in the then fashionable suburb of Everton, Liverpool. He received his education from his father, and entered business as a chemist. He owes his success to the discovery, in con- junction with Mr. Mond, of the new process in the manufacture of alkalis, which have made the business of Brunner, Mond, and Co., Limited, one of the most phenomenally lucrative in commerce. The extensive works of the firm, of which Sir John Brunner is chair- man, are situate at Northwich, in the division which the hon. baronet has represented continuously since 1887. He was first elected for the division in 1885, and repre- sented it until the dissolution in the fol. owing year, when he did not succeed in securing re-election. He was re-elected, however, in August, 1887. after a memorable contest, in which part was taken against him by Mr- A. J. Balfour. There is no doubt that Sir John Brunner owes much of his popularity to his great generosity. He has endowed the Grammar Schools at Witton and Farnworth with scholarships for the benefit of boys from elementary schools, and provided a similar endowment at the Northwich Hili School for Girls. Besides this, he has liberally endowed the Nantwich, Winsford, and Middlewich Free Public Libraries. In 1885 he presented to the town of Northwich a free public library at the cost of £7,000, and in addition to contributing handsomely to the Technical Institute at Runcorn, he has provided a Trades Ball for the use of Trades and Friently Societies in the town. His interest in education has been turther manifested iii gifts to University College, Liverpool, of which he is a member of the council, since among other gifts he endowed the chair of economic science with £lO,OOO. In politics the hon. baronet has always been a staunch Liberal, and in the House of Commons has done good service on com- mittees. He has also sat on Royal Commissions. He has been twice married, first in 1864 to Miss Salome Davies, daughter of Mr. James Davies, of Liverpool; and secondly, to Miss Wyman, daughter of the late Dr. Wyman, of Kettering, who still survives. Sir John Brunner is an active member of the Liverpool Reform Club, and also of the Reform, National Liberal, and Devonshire Clubs, London. His seat is Winnington Old Hall,Cheshire, but he has residsnces at Druid's Cross, Wavertree, Liverpool; and Ennismore-gardens, Lon- don, S.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>13IPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>13IPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday evening Mr. A. MOMEY, answering a question, said an arrangement had been made with the City of Dublin Steam Packet Company the effect of which would be to secure an ac- celeration of half an hour in the sea passage of the Irish mails. The subsidy would be £IOJ,OOO a year, and the agreement was for a term of twenty years. Mr. BRYCE informed Mr. VINCENT that in his opinion to subject foreign commercial travellers in this country to special taxation would be injurious to British inter- ests.</p>
        <p>A bill was brought in by Mr. CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN to amend the law in regard to the volunteer force and active service. _ . .</p>
        <p>The House again went into Committee on the Welsh Church Bill, and various amendments were defeated. In the House of Lords Lord ROSEBERY introduced a bill to amend the constitution of the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council. He said that under the order constituting the Judicial Committee two places were allotted to Indian and colonial judges. At the present moment there was only one judge having colonial I experience, and only one having Indian experience on the Judicial Committee, while the number of appeals, especially from the colonies, was constantly increasing. The bill proposed that any pesron holding judicial office in the Supreme Court of the Dominion of Canada or the Australian colonies should be sworn a member of the I Privy Council, and should also be a member, without salary, of the Judicial Committee. He believed that if I the bill became law two or three of our great self- governing colonies would take advantage of its .provi- sions. The bill was read a first time. TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening Mr. MACLIIRE moved that the House should not sit on Wed- nesday (Derby-day). Mr. KENYON seconded the motion not, however, he said, because he wished to go to the Derby, but because a number of meetings of importance Ito Wales were to be held and he did not know how he could be present at them il the House sat. After some discussion the motion was rejected by 221 to 174. The House again went into Committee on the Welsh Disestablishment Bill. On clause 5 Mr. BARTLEY moved an amendment to provide that " private benefactions " should include "grants from Parliament." It was strongly opposed on behalf of the Government, and was defeated ty 16 to 11.1,</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords Viscount HALIFAX moved the second reading of a Bill to amend the Matrimonial Causes Act, 1857, and the law relating to the marriage of divorced persons. The operative clause of the measure, be explained, would free the minister of any church or chapel of the Church of England from liability to any suit, penalty, or censure for refusing to permit the mar- riage of any person whose former marriage shall have been lissolved on the ground of his or her misconduct to be solemnised in such church or chapel. The Archbishop of CANTERBURY and the Marquis of SALISBURY supported the Bill. The Lone CHANCELLOR agreed with the Mar- quis ot SALISBURY that the Bishop of the diocese.and not the incumbent of a church, should determine what mar- riages of divorced persons should take place in church. It was, he added, somewhat remarkable that a scandal so widely felt as this had been described to be should have been permitted to go on uninterruptedly for over thirty years. The Bill was afterwards read a second tine. WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Dr. Fox mos,cd the second reading of the Irish County Councils Bill. He said the Irish members did not admit that this bill would be anything but a mere instalment of self- government; it would net in the least obviate the necessity for Home Rule. Mr. T. M. BELLY, in second- ing the motion, said the Tory party, although they de- clared their anxiety to extend local government to Ire- land, quarrelled with every effort in that direction. As to the Liberal party, he did not expect trorn themn any measure of county government for Ireland, because they would lay themselves open to the charge that they had abandoned Home Rule; all he asked from them in reference to this measure was that they would adopt a benevolent attitude. After some further discussion the amendment was by leave withdrawn, and the bill was read a second time. .Dr. Fox then movezl, that.the bill be committed to the Grand Committee on Law. Mr. BALFOUR opposed the motion and Mr. SHAW-LEFEVRE said the bill was not one which should be sent to that committee. The motion was under discussion when the time arrived for the adjournment of debate. Mr. LAMBERT moved that the Land Tenure Bill should be referred to the Standing Committee on Trade, but objection twine taken the motion had to be postponed.</p>
        <p>What becomes of pain when successfully treated? We say, it vanishes like smoke. Thor- ough treatment does away with italtogether. If you want a relief from pains in the back, side, chest or limbs, use an</p>
        <p>ALLCOCK'S r.l PLASTER.</p>
        <p>Of all Chemists from Ls. *id. ke.to 225. 6d. each.</p>
        <p>BOUND UP TOGETHER.</p>
        <p>In some way, too mysteri• us for us te) understand, the mind is bound up with the body ; woven into ii, so to speak, like the pictures into a piece of tapestry. And that is why Mr. Edward James Grant felt so low-spirited and miserable he didn't know what to do. His doctor said he had congestion of the liver, and it looks as though the doctor was right. The trouble began away ba.?,k in December, 1887. Before that Mr. Grant enjoyed as good health as anybody. Then he became suddenly aware that his energy was all ebbing out of him ; he felt tired, relaxed, unstrung, and drowsy. Holing out his tongue in front of the glass he noted that it was brown and thickly coated. Food in plenty was on the table at meal times, but he didn't want it. Appetite, that king of sauces, was lacking. When he did force down some. thing on the principle that one must eat to live, the re- sult wasn't satisfactory. After every effort of that kind he felt a weight and pain in the chest and sides, and what he speaks of as " a kind of lump "between the chest and throat.</p>
        <p>Ile often spat up a fluid bitter as gall and yellow as a guinea. As time went on he had frequent attacks of dicaioness. Sometimes they would take him in the street and he feared he should fall. As he went about his work his head would swim round,and he wetild have these attacks two or three times a day. " I felt so low-spirited and miserable," he says, " that I didn't know what to do," 'which We can easily be- lieve. " I consulted a doctor," he tells us, " who said I had congestion of the liver, and he gave me a medicine that helped me for a time. I also took liver pills, but felt none the better for it.</p>
        <p>" In this state I continued for four years, trying for wards and backwards after some medicine adapted to my case, but without coining upon it. In January of this year (1892) it was that I first heard of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and began to use it. After I had taken two bottles, the bad symptoms abated, my appetite began to come back, the dizzy spells returned no more, and now I can eat anything, and am well as ever. You may make my letter public if you think it would do good, and I will gladly :answer inquiries. Yours truly (Signed) EDWARD JAMES GRANT (milk dealer), 43, Vestry-road, Camberwell, London, S.E., December 6th, 1892."</p>
        <p>The trouble began in this way: The stomach being first overloaded and overworked, shirked part of its busines and sent a lot of half-digested stuff on to the liver. The latter organ resented this, and refused to handle it, for you must remember that all food most irst pass through the liver before it can reach any point beyond it. So the liver, having taken a " day off," as we say, stopped gathering bile from the blood to help the bowels. The bile, consequently, stayed in the blood, and set on foot all the mischief which made our good friend so miserable he didn't know what to do.</p>
        <p>Luckily for him, and for all of us,Mother Seigel knew what to do, and put her knowledge in the form of the wonderful remedy which has immortalised her name. He beard of it, used it, and was cured of his dyspepsia and his liver complaint at the same time. And inso- much as most of our unhappiness arises from nnhealth- Mess, Mr. Grant's spirits soon became what every well man's spirits ought to be—light and buoyant. On this result he will please accept our sincere congratulations.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S PER MANENT BENEFIT BUILDING SOCIETY. ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual meeting of the above society was held in the Golborne-st. schoolroom under the chairmanship of Mr. R. Isherwood, who was elected to that position at the commencement of the business. There were also present—Messrs. J. Hatton, J. Long. and, P. Dalton (secretary), J. Harding. E. Wallington, E. Fenton, J. Baxter, J. Ashley,F. Br3wn,T. Brown, W. Brown, W. W Baker, and Mr. G. Armstrong.</p>
