      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE l, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE l, 1895.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD 01_i' GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Board was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday. Mr. William Houghton presided, aud there were also present Mrs. Clay, the Revs. T. R. Matthews, J. H. Riddette ; Messrs. G. Thompson, S. Davies, T. Mather, J. Johnson, T. B. Malkin, G. Pilling, A. J. Jolley, J. Clarke, J. Ball, J. W. Knowles, T. Halton, A. Jolley, J. Houghton, E. L. Bent, J. Billings, J. Dignum ;J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (deputy clerk), H. M. Mines (superintendent;, C. H. Ward (master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>REPORT AND BALANCE SHEET.</head>
        <p>REPORT AND BALANCE SHEET.</p>
        <p>Mr. DALTON in reading the twenty-fifth annual report, for the year ending 30th April, 1895, said " the managers have pleasure in laying the twenty-fifth annual statement of accounts before the shareholders, as shown in the annexed statement. The sum of £1,494 9s. 7d. has been received as subscriptions during the year, and £2,951 54. 6d. for repayments, making the total receipts £4,445 15s. id. The withdrawals dur- ing the year amount to £1,512 4s. 3d. The sum of £2,860 12s. 6d. has been sold to tnembers during the year. There are 212 subscribers holding 289 shares, and 37 borrowers holding 173 i shares, making 249 members holding 462 t shares. The profits for the year, including 4s. 2,1. from last account, after deducting working ex- penses, amount to £403 15s. 5d., out of which the managers recommend a dividend of 4 per cent., which will take £356 14s. 2d. leaving a balance of £47 is. 3d., which they direct to be dealt with as follows, viz. £25 to be placed to the contingent fund, and the balance of £22 is. 3d., to be carried forward to next year's account. The reserve fund amounts to £soo,and the contingent fund to £145 Bs. 2d. The whole of the manage.is retire. Messrs. Harding, Wallington, Fenton, Isherwood, Bouchier and Baxter are eligible and offer themselves for re-election. Six additional managers will be taken from the register in rotation.—James Harding, chairman."</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said seeing that they had such a balance sheet before them, and that that was their 25th annual report, he did not know that he had ever more pleasure in moving the adoption of the balance sheet, than in the present instance. He then made several comparisons and said there was an increase over the re- ceipts of 1894 of a large amount. This showed that they were not decreasing but increasing and that people had more confidence in the Society. Their withdrawals had not all been withdrawals in consequence of leaving the Society as a great many had been due to depression in trade. The balance sheets had worked out very fairly for a long time and every investor whn had £lOO in the Society that night. was worth £lO6 14s. This was very satisfactory considering the state of trade and he had great pleasure in proposing that the report and balance sheet be adopted. Mr. FENTON seconded and said it was a pleasure to , know that the Society was now in a flourishing condition after all the depression of trade which had existed for sometime, but which he now hoped was partially gone, if not altogether. Mr. GREALY : Has the membership increased during the year ? The CHAIRMAN Yes, by Six. The report and balance sheet were then passed as read.</p>
        <p>THE DIVIDEND.</p>
        <p>Mr. GIMALY moved that a dividend of 4 per cent, be paid to the investors on their deposits. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. LONGLAND seconded the motion which was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. LONOLAND, referring to a remark of Mr. Urea- ley's about the members bringing Rthers, said they did not only want investing members ; they wanted borrow- ing members. At different periods during the year the managers had had money in the bank which they would have liked to put it out and if all the members interested in the Society would mection to their friends what a sat- isfactory way it worked out,he thought they would have a great many more borrowers than during the past year. The CHAIRMAN said they were six more in number but several of the old members had paid off their amounts and he was not sure whether they had re- entered or not. It was quite possible that there might be considerably more than six, which was the number made by comparing the balance sheets of this and the past year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. GREALY moved that Messrs. Harding, Wallington. Fenton, Isherwood, Boucher and Bearer be re-elected managers for the ensuing year.—Mr. Holt seconded and the motion was carried unanimously.--Mr. Isherwood responded on behalf of the managers.—Messrs. J. E. Wright and William Bodle, jun., were elected surveyors for the year, and Mr. S. Albinson and Mr. E. Bradshaw as auditors.</p>
        <p>ALLOWANCE TO MANAGERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. GREALY moved that an allowance of Is. be paid to each manager for his attendance at each meeting dur- ing the year.—This was seconded by Mr. W. H. Walling- ton and unanimously agreed to. A vote of thanks was then passed to the Chairman of the meeting on the motion of Mr. Fenton seconded by Mr. F. Brown.—Mr. Isherwood briefly responded and the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LYiIM.</head>
        <p>LYiIM.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—At the Lamm Police Court, on Tuesday, before Mr. James Gregson, Patrick Quinn was charged with having been drunk on the previous day.—P.C. Breeze proved the case.—Defendant, who ex- pressed sorrow, and said the offence would not be re- peated, was fined 55.,0r in detault seven days' imprison- ment with hard labour. AN ANTI-YACCINATIONIST.—At the Sale Petty Sessions, on Monday, Alexander Edwin Davies, of Lymm, was summoned for neglecting to have his child vaccinated. The child was stated to have been born on the 11th July, 1894.—A Mr. Hough, of Salford, said to be con- nected with the Manchester and Salford Anti-vaccination Society, said he appeared in behalf of the defendant,but it was ruled that be could not appear. The offence was then gone into,and evidence was gives to the effect that defendant had bees notified that he was evading the law, but that he had said that he did not intend to have his child vaccinated. He admitted that he was a mem- ber of the Anti-vaccination Society.—A fine of 10s. was imposed. Mr. Hough paid the money and asked for a receipt from the assistant clerk, but this was refused him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>MASTER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MASTER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The MASTER having reported the receipt of a number of books, periodicals, &amp;c., said thdt the police the other day brought to the workhouse an infant two years of age without any particulars, except that the mother had been committed to prison for fourteen days with hard labout. The child was taken in by him, and the mother was now in the workhouse.—The CHAIRMAN asked if the mother was a tramp.—Mr. WARD: She has come out of gaol into the workhouse, but I can now get any further particulars that may be necessary.—The CHAIRMAN : When did the police meet with her?—Mr. WARD: The mother only came into the house last night from gaol. SCHCOLS COMMITTEE. —THE DUTIES OF THE BUILDINGS COMMITTEE.—PADOATE SCHOOL MANAGEMENT: A PROTEST.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the above committee, held on the 28th May, the Building Committee made their report, which was allowed to lie over until after Walking Day. The Chairman, Vice-Chairman, 'Mr. William Houghton, and Mr. Mines were appointed to see the Gas Works Committee in respect of the meter rent. It was pro- posed by Mr. Knowles, seconded by Mr. W. Houghton, that the men employed in the workshops work from half-past one till half-past five in the afternoon. An amendment was proposed by Mr. J. Houghton that it be five o'clock, but it was lost. It was unanimously re- solved that new band suits be provided. It was pro- posed and seconded that temporary assistance be engaged for two weeks to make up articles of clothing. The confirmation of these minutes was moved by Mr. DAVIES, seconded by Mr. PILLING.-Mr. HALTON wished the Chairman to define the duties of the Buildings and Repairs Committee.—The CLERK said that the commit- tee was appointed to give advice with regard to the al- terations to the buildings of the institution, and also to examine bills and such similar work.—Mr. HALTON said that he asked that question because he did not wish for a repetition of the proceedings of the previous day. He had received a post card from Mr. Mines, asking him as a member of the Buildings Committee, to attend the meeting of the Schools Committee at Padgate the pre- vious day. When he got to Padgate he was called upon to look over alterations inside as well as out of doors. For this he was made a laughing stock of. Mr. Thomp- son was not present, but on behalf of the committee, he would say that it was not right that they should waste their valuable time in that way, and he brought the imatter up so that he could have the duties of the Build- ngs Committee properly defined, and not be made a aughing stock of by the Schools Committee at Padgate. Be had been asked to go through the premises by Mr. Mines.—The CLERK said that it was the demeanour cf the Schools Committee which was at fault.—Mr. DAVIES said that he was not aware that the Buildings Commit- tee was to tell the Schools Committee as to whether they were to furnish a room or any work of that class. He thought the Schools Committee were competent to know that themselves.—The CLERK said that the committee was net appointed to decide as to indoor repairs or fur- nishing, but were to decide as to whether the charges, repairs, and outdoor alterations were exces- sive.—Mr. HALTON said that the committee had been called upon to look at the inside work as well as outside, by Mr. Mines. It was a rather unpleasant posi- tion to be in and he was very pleased to know that when they went to Padgate again that they were to look after outside work an 3 not inside. Mr. THOMPSON said he would have gone but he had been prevented. He was glad that he had thus been saved the treatment that Mr. Halton had got. (Laughter.) Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said that he did not agree with the recommendation that temporary assistance should be engaged for two weeks previous to Walking Day to make up articles of clothing. He could not let that pass, without entering a protest at the manner in which things were managed at Padgate. He thought that if the work was properly managed and methodically carried out, there would be no need of application of extenal assisance. There were plenty of officers and big girls about that place to do all the work. He thought that it was simply a disgrace to the management that help should be asked for periodically in that manner. He would move an amendment that this recommenda- tion be not adopted.—Mr. CLARKE seconded the amend- ment.—Mr. KNowt,Es as a member of the committee said he would have liked the work to have been done without outside assistance, but they had found that if the assistance was not obtained some of the children would be without new dresses on Walking Day, as there was not time to make them. The Committee were satis- fied that it was necessary that they should go to some expenditure. They would endeavour to obtain the materials necessary in sufficient time in future so that the garments could be made ready for Walking Day.—Mr. DAVIES : This will not occur again.— Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : That has been said every year.—The CHAIRMAN said that he was very</p>
        <p>much against it, but he thought it was necessary this year.—Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said that he was deter- mined to protest against this thing, and he would not withdraw his amendment.—The amendment was then put to the vote but was defeated by ten votes to six. The minutes were then passed unanimously.</p>
        <p>TRE LADIES' COMMITTEE AND PADGATE 9CIIOOLS..</p>
        <p>The CLERK read the following letter dated May 14th, relating to a visit paid by the Ladies' Committee to Pad. gate Schools :—" The committee are very pleased to re- port that they have found everything in a satisfactory condition on their recent visits to Padgate. It is to be regretted that the making of the new frocks for Walking Day should have been left until so near the time when they are required. The Ladies' Committee would venture to suggest that,in the event of any alteration in the staff which might make such an arrangement possible, a competent needlewoman can be employed to make the new clothes for the girls, as the mending in the homes is very heavy work. At the least, 250 articles a week re- quire mending in each of the girls' homes, not counting bedlinen, towels, &amp;c., and the knitting of stockings for themselves and a boys' home." Mr. DAVIES said that there was a little more light thrown upon the condition of affairs at Padgate.—Mr. Mum said that he never knew In his 25 years experience of a time when there were so few big girls at the schools. The CLERK said that made a material difference with regard to the question of assistance for the schools.</p>
        <p>UNIFORM TREATISIENT OF CASUALS.</p>
        <p>An invitation was given t; the Board by the Poor Law Union to send a member to join a deputation to meet the President of the Local Government Board in June,to discuss the question of the uniform treatment of casuals. The CLERK said that the Chairman and himself had at- tended the Central Poor Law Gonference in February, audit would be as well that they go with the deputation. Ordinarily a deputation to the Local Government Board consisted of three members and the clerk, but as there would be so many on the present occasion, the Local Government Board had written requesting that only one member besides the Clerk should attend. The mat- ter was of much importance, and especially so to War- rington, where they had almost the highest number of casuals proportionately to the population in Lancashire. It was of great importance that compulsory rules for the treatment of casuals should be brought up.</p>
        <p>WALKING DAY._</p>
        <p>The CRAIRMAN said that there had been a little friction with regard to the order of the children on Walking Day, as to whether the children at Padgate schools or the chil- dren in the House should follow the band. It would be better to avoid this friction—Mr. KNowLEs said that he thought the elder children should come first and the little children in lorries at the last,and he proposed that this be the order of walking.—Mr. E. L. BENT seconded and this was passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>ANNUAL PICNIC.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL PICNIC.</p>
        <p>_ The CalanmeN moved that the officers be allowed leave of absence on the date of the annual excursion, which this year would be to the Dukeries. The date of of picnic had not yet been arranged,but would probably be in June.—Mr. DAVIES seconded, and this was passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>VALUE R'S DUTIES.</head>
        <p>THE VALUE R'S DUTIES.</p>
        <p>- Mr. BENT asked what were the duties of the valuer.— The CLERK said he had to value property of the Union for the Assessment Committee.—Mr. BENT : He spends no special time for the purpose of the Committee.—The CLERK : No, he does not. Pie does all the work he is required to do, and he has a salary of £2OO per annum. —Mr. BENT said that he would give notice of motion that the subject be considered at the next monthly meeting.—The CLERK said that the salary had been con- firmed for the present year, and the notices would be out of order. It would be perfectly in order to discuss it next year.—Mr. BENT: Mr. Owen ought to be paid for the work he does.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED ACiENDA PAPERS. Mr. KNOWLES said that if it would not be inconvenient to the Clerk, he would like to propose that agenda papers be printed similar to those cf Corporations. This was done by many public bodies, but he was not certain whether any Boards of Guardians had adopted this method.—The CLERIC said that nearly all his correspond- ence came in the week of the meeting, and lettere from the Local Government Board frequently came on the morning of the meeting. It would therefore be impossi- ble to give the members any idea of the business which was to be transacted at the meeting. Otherwise he would have been pleased to have adopted Mr. Knowles suggestion.—Mr. KNOWLES said that under these circum- stances he would withdraw his motion. This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>A large proportion of Scott's Emulsion is taken by babies and growing children. If you have a thin, weak baby, or a child that shows emaciation, narrow chest and general weak- ness, you ought to give this great preparation a trial. You will surely see quick results. (11.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Bramwell Booth.chief, of the staff of the Salvation Army, giviag evidence on Tuesday before the Select Committee on the Unemployed, said that owing to want of money and ether causes. the project of emigrating the unemployed to an oversea colony bad not yet been carried out. No fewer than eight per cent. of the cri- minals received into the Salvation Army homes had be- longed to the professional clasaos—lawyere, doctors ke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>PEOPLE WHO IMITATE.</head>
        <p>PEOPLE WHO IMITATE.</p>
        <p>Where imitation or mimicry goes no further than fair play, there is something to be said in its defence. It argues a certain humility of mind that a person should acknowledge himself incapable of originality, and it is better to follow humbly a beaten track than to do nothing. In some other ways, however, imitation is an unpardonable crime. , Where some great discovery has benefited thousands of people, and is being sought after by all on the account, it is a cruel swindle for anyon- ignorant of the true discovery to foist upon unsuspecting people something resembling it in name or Outward I show, but worthless in comparison with the true inven- tion. Of course the worse example of fraudulent imita tion in this sense is the fabricating of pretended med;- nines, useless in themselves, on the strength of a super- ficial resemblance to a known remedy. Such imitators literally juggle with lifennd death. This journal has, in common with all the newspapers of this part of the country, published during the last two years a number of remarkable cases, in which apparently dying persons have been cured by the dis- covery published under the name of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and a large number of persons suffering from various disorders have sought the same remedy. Advantage has, unfortunately, been taken of this demand to play upon the innocence of the sufferers, by offering a substitute for Dr.Williams' Pink Pills. It is important that the futility of suck imitatioas should be publicly known. Any retailer or detailer who pretends to have "the same thing" as Dr. Williams' Pink Pills is trying to cheat the customer. Anyone who tries to sell Dr. Williams' Pink Pills loose or in bulk is trying to sell a worthless thing under false pre- tences. The genuine Pills are never sold in that way. Anyone who says he can prepare the formula himself says what is not true: no one can possibly know what is in Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, or divine the new curative principle that they contain. Not long since a so-called analysis of Dr. Williams' was published—it was an ab- solute failure, and entirely failed to show the composi- tion of this important remedy. Another point is that " cheaper" substitutes for Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are a self-confessed fraud. There Is so subsitute at any price ; but in no event could Dr. Williams' formula be sold .heap. The composition does not admit of it. But the price at which Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are sold, and their extraordinary curative effects, make the cost very low indeed compared with other remedies or medical treatment.</p>
        <p>As a final word the public should be warned against the statement sometimes made that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are identical with the well-known French Pharma- lcopcsia remedy, Blaud's Pills. Anyone who says so manifests the most ludicrous ignorance of the very ele- ments of pharmacy, and the public will be well-advised not to run the risk of buying medicine in any form from a self-confessed incompetent. When any difficulty is experienced in obtaining Dr.Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, in wooden tube, with pink wrapper and the fun name printed in red ink outside and in ; or wherever doubt is felt as to the genuineness of the goods supplied, the public are invited to apply direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, E.C., for supplies, which will be sent post free at 2s. 9d. a box,or six boxes for 13s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday afternoon. The chair was occupied by the Rev. G. F. Roberts, and there were also present Messrs. W. Houghton, J. Ball, T. Halton, J. W. Knowles, T. Mather, ,J. Dignum, J. Houghton, J. Warburton, J. Johnson, S. Sutton (clerk), W. Owen (surveyor), F. Kerfoot (nuisance inspector).</p>
        <p>BALANCE IN HAND.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that the balance in the treasurer's hands on the sonitary works account was £l5l ss. ;on the Burtonwood sewerage account, £2,100 15s. ; and on the Rixton Cemetery Loan account, £BO 7s. Bd.—The accounts were passed on the motion of Mr. T.Houghton, seconded by Mr. Dignum.</p>
        <p>THE CHESHIRE LINES AND THE PADGATE WATER SUPPLT.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. Meldrum in which he re- plied to the complaint that the Cheshire Lines Commit- tee were not carrying out their engagement to get all their water for the engines at Padgate from the mains at Padgate. He said that the Company were doing all that they had engaged to do. A long discussion arose upon the matter, Mr. J. Houghton averring that it was on the understanding that the whole of the water from the station, houses, and engines would be obtained from the Padgate water mains, that the mains had been laid on the terms agreed upon. It was ultimately decided on the mstion of Mr. W. Houghton, that the Chairman and Clerk form a deputation to wait unon the engineer of the Cheshire Lines Committe to endeavour to come to some arrangement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>INSPECTOR OF NUISANCES.</head>
        <p>THE INSPECTOR OF NUISANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. DmNum gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that Mr. F. Kerfoot be re-appointed inspec- tor of nuisances for the district. Mr. Kerfoot having applied for an increase of salary, Mr. Dignum said that he would move that Mr. Berfoot's salary be increased from £lOO to £llO. He said that Mr. Kerfoot's services had been very great to the Council,and it was only right that they should be acknowledged.</p>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICER.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM HOUGHTON gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that Dr. Sephton be re-appointed medical officer of health for the district at the same terms as previouslV.</p>
        <p>STREET PAVING IN LITTLE SANKEY.</p>
        <p>The CLERK announced that £1,352 16s. sd. was still owing to the Council by property owners for street pav- ing at Little Sankey. It was resolved on the motion of Mr. Dignum, seconded by Mr. W. Houghton that immediate payment be required of all outstanding liabil- ities of the persons in respect of street making at Little Sankey, and in case of failure of such payment, that proceedings be taken for the recovery of sums unpaid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. SEPIITON, the medical officer, reported that during the month, there had been 28 births, and 17 deaths. The death rate was 14.8 per 1.000, against 15.8, the average for the last three months, and 8.8. the corresponding month of last year. The deaths from zymotic diseases were equal to .8 per I.ooo,against I.2.the average of the last three months, and nil, the corresponding month of last year. The one death from zymotic disease was from membraneous croup. The following cases of infec- tious diseases had been reported :—Scarlet fever, two cases ; diphtheria, one case ; membraneous croup, two cases.—The report was adopted en the motion of Mr. W. Houghton, seconded by Mr. Halton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SANITARY ARRANGEMENTS AT GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>SANITARY ARRANGEMENTS AT GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Mr. HALTON said that he thought something should be done with regard to the sanitary arrangements of Great Sankey. Something similar to that of Little Sankey and Penketh might be adapted. Eventually there would be a great nuisance in Great Sankey if some system were not adopted. At present the people had to deposit their refuse, etc., on to the gardens or wherever they could, and as the population was increasing this could not be carried on long. The Parish Council had desired him to bring the matter forward.—The CLERK said that the matter could be delegated to the Parish Council to deal with it as they thought proper.—Mr. HALTON thereupon moved, and Mr. Dignum seoonded a resolution to this effect, and the motion was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>SURVEYOR'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>SURVEYOR'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The SURVEYOR Vei;M:i-e-cit'h;i-i-cin-14-na the month, in- spections had been made on the houses of Mr. Hindley and Mr.Ezard,at Padgate,and the houses of Mr. Hughes, Penketh, and Mr. Warburton, Great Sankey. Plans for the erection of a stall at Great Sankey by Mr. J. Warburton were also passed. The contractor was pro- ceeding satisfactorily with the Burtonwood drainage. Most of the owners had intimated their willingness to obey the Council's instructions and connect with the new sewer, many of them had entrusted the work to the contractor. He had issued a certificate of £2OO on account which made a total payment of £Boo.—The re- port was unanimously adopted.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON' FIELD CLUB.</p>
        <p>- ---- ----- The first walk of the season in connection with the above club took place on Saturday, the leaders being Mr. William Webster and Mr. L. Greening. About 20 members assembled outside the " Horse and Jockey," at 3 p.m. The walk was through the groves and grounds of Winwick Hall, Mr. W. G. Wallace having kindly given the club permission to ramble over the estate. Soon after arriving there it commenced to rain, which greatly interfered with specimen collecting. Notwithstanding this, however, specimens were collected of ants, viz., Myrmica, Leptothorax, Acervorum, Myrmica Scab- rinodin, Formica Nigra, &amp;c., also various spiders, Julidae, &amp;c., and Mr. Webster also took a fine ants' nest complete. Mr. Collins obtained specimens of Lepocoptera Larva. Botanicarspecimens were col- lected by some of the members. It was originally intended to go through the woods in the occupation of Mr. fierfoot, permission from which was obtained by the leader, but this was put off owing to the rain. On the return the members visited Winwick Church,where the caretaker very obligingly pointed out all the objects of interest, descriptions of which were very minutely followed by Mr. May. The re- turn home was made about 6 o'clock, the members having greatly enjoyed the walk, notwithstanding the unpropititious weather, and the first muster on Saturday augurs well for a very successful season for the club.</p>
        <p>"KINGS OF MEN."</p>
        <p>LECTURE BY THE REV. J. BOND.</p>
        <p>A lecture upon the above subject was given in connec- tion with the Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday School anni- versary at the Bold-street Wesleyan Church on Monday evening by the Rev. John Bond, of London. Mr. J. Hartley took the chair, and there was a fair attend- ance.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that the Sunday School was the best institution in the world. He had been connected with the Bold-street Sunday school for upwards of 28 years, and his labour among the young had been the joy of his life.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOHN Bonn said that his lecture was about the kings of men. Queens there certainly had besn. There had been queens political in this land of ours, from good Queen Bess who said that though she had but the body of a frail feeble woman, she had the heart and stomach of a king, and a king of England, too, down to the present time when they were still favoured with a queen, than whom no king had ever reigned more nobly in the hearts of the English people. (Ap- plause.) Domestic queens were in their hearts and homes, and to the young persons present he would ad- vise them that they should be such queens. (Laughter.) He had,however,just now to do with kings. He thought it would be admitted that people were not born equal. He was not going to deal with the precocious creatures who had learned to call their fathers the "old governor," and their mothers " the old judy," and the little brothers and sisters" the kids." He bad nothing to do with those wonderful people. Kings of men were men with a noble purpose, and who refused to let any difficulty come in their way to stop them. They also required to have an imperial will. A will of their own was very necessary whether they were kings or no. The will should be guided by sagacious counsellors, but when it acted it should act with all its force. Where there was a will there was a way. The great difference between one man and another was the will; no talent,no oppor- tunity would make a two-legged creature into a man much less a king without will. Another aid to the making of kings was faith in one's future. As instances of this he gave the names of Beaconsfield and Robes- pierre. Faith might make a man a fanatic, an unmiti- gated bore, or an ass—(laughter)—but no one could be a drone and yet have faith. Another quality was that of industry. Many of the sons of industry had been exalted to the most important places in the Church and State. He gave a most interesting instance of this by giving an account of Peter the Great of Russia. The next quality was perseverance. There had been many instances of this. One was that of Sir Robert Peel, who could by training himself, give an opponent's speech verbatim. Then there came Columbus Sir Robert Bruce and George Stephenson. He advised his audience never to give up. A great conquering force was the force of preservance. Another quality was that of courage. The Anglo-Saxon race was foremost in this. Another kind of courage was moral courage of which he was afraid was not common enough at the present time. The courage to do right though the clouds might fall, was the courage which they required at the present time. Luther was an instance of this kind of courage. Dare to be singular for the truth. Dare to stand up for oonviction. Dare to cross the Rubicon in defiance of that which was wrong.and in support of that which was right. Then there was the quality of goodness which was the kingliest of all the qualities and which helped to make every other quality victorious. It bound every other quality and made it strong as a rock. lie did not plead for an ostentatious goodness but for real unostentatious goodness. Goodness thew round a man an armour which it was impossible for the shafts of evil to enter. It became increasingly the king of the eartb. There was an aristrocracy of soul which was far superior than aristrocracy of blood. There was a king in mind and soul who was equal to anyone who ever ruled in England in the person of Oliver Cromwell. (Applause.) The lecturer caused much amuse- ment by his decription of "ape among men." To make one's self a king of men was an object worthy of everyone, but they must gain it by their own exer- tions. They must begin early, and day by day persist in their task. Set their mark high, and presently there would be given them the highest crown of all, the crown which never faded.</p>
        <p>At the close of the lecture, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer on the motion of Mr. W. Si!cock, seconded by the Rev. Owen Watkins and the meeting was brought to a termination by the pronounc- ing of the benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>MISHAPS ON THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, there was towed km the Ship Canal to Weston Point the brig ih'inefred, with a cargo of bone ash from Hamburg, in a damaged condition. On the previous day, when attempting to enter Eastham locks in the wake of the steam tug Accumulator, the steamer came in contact with the dolphins and smashed several of them, and then dashed violently against the " pier- head" and put her anchors through her bows. The vessel was promptly beached on the river side, where the anchor was extracted and repairs executed sufficient to enable her to reach her destination.—The Weston lock of the Ship Canal, which is the communication between the Mersey and the Weston Point Dock, has been blocked fcr three days by a hopper, which is sunk at the entrance. Divers are at work patching the damage, so that th, hopper may be raised and removed into the river, whither she was being towed when she sank. The hopper had previously been lying some months at the bottom of the Ship Canal. MANCEIESTER AND SALFORD LAND LORDS AND THE RATES.</p>
        <p>The heavy expenditure by the municipalities of Man chester and Salford upon the Ship Canal and other large objects appears to be producing the effect that might have been expected in the corresponding augmentation of the rates. This in its turn affects property owners, with the consequence that it is found necessary to raise rents. The following is a copy of a circular that has been sent out to tenants by one large property owner : —"ln consequence of the great increase of is. 9d. in the pound in the rates in Salford, Broughton, and Pendleton this year, caused by the expenditure of large sums of money by the local authorities, over whom I have no control, I regret being obliged to raise your rent to— from Monday next to meet such increased demands." LOCK-OUT AT THE SALFORD DOCKS.</p>
        <p>For some time past there has not been wanting evid- ence that eventually there would be a great deal of trouble caused owing to various reasons in connection with the Dock Labourers on the Ship Canal, and the dis- pute which occurred the other day has now indirectly caused a lock-out of a large number of labourers. Shortly after the opening of the canal a branch of the National Union of Dock Labourers was opened in Manchester, and the labourers having responded to the advances maJe towards them, have now placed them- selves in a fairly strong condition, and negotiations have lately taken place asking the Canal Company to recognize the union. This the Canal Company refuse to do, and whilst quite willing to accept the services of the union men are equally willing to accept the services of non-un- ionists,provided they are proficient in the work required of them. It was then decided by the unionists that they should wear a badge when at work in the docks to dis- tinguish them from the non-unionists ; but on this becoming known to the authorities an intimation was made that it would not be allowed. Nothing furttier was then beard of the matter till Monday morning,when about 75 per cent, of the labourers presented themselves at the dock gates for work wearing a metal token as a sign of their membership of the union. These men were, however, not allowed inside the gates, and according to a Canal official sufficient men were found to handle the various cargoes at present in the docks. The locked-out men preserved an orderly demeanour, and congregated in front of the gates. At a meeting held near Trafford Bridge the unemployed labourers expressed their deter- mination to make the Canal Company recognize the union, and allow them to wear the badge. Expressions were made that the Canal Company wanted to crush tha union, and a telegram offering support was received from the Dockers' Conference.</p>
        <p>The Bank of England has a reserve of only about £35,000,000 in gold ; but this country, though havirg the largest trade and interests to defend from one end ot the world to the other, has admittedly no need to make any preparation BO far as cash is concerned, as she is able to raise the very largest sums of money ‘t a moment's notice.</p>
