        <p>forward to with interest, and now that it has ....bawl made it may safely be said that it will not ,thsappoint the expectations that were formed. Mr. MORLEY.defendi the policy and the methods of the Government with a whole heart and with convincinu arguments. He unreservedly admits at at the present moment they are very hard 're•ssed. They are struggling with many difficul- ties, some of which, as he points out, are imposed bY those who ought to be their supporters. ut then the whole business of government is '0 surniountin.g of difficulties, and it is evident from Mr. MORLEY'S address that he, at all events, nws not been affected with that helpless despair bleb Opposition writers declare has seized the Whole of the Cabinet. He scarcely alludes to the kibject of dissolution. On the other hand, evi- dently speaking with a full knowledge of the net Minds of his colleagues, he declares that 8° long as the House of Commons continues its teort to us we shall persist and persevere." The ingv:lrent are sometimes taunted with " plough- sands of the seashore," that is to say,with tersisting in the performance of work which can a_ave no useful results, but Mr. MoriLEy is not RS- 44nled of the legislation which bas been carried out a comparatively hrief tenure of office,or of that "jell is still in hand. Large and useful measures • reform have been added to the statute-book, .anclit is probahle that before the end ef the year Other reforms v, ffl have been effected. The mea- -1 tea to which lie chiefly refers as still to be carried °ugh Parliament, are the Factory Bill and the • 'al, Veto Bill, and his allusions to the two Bills Q°lllcl be satisfactory toboth labour men and tern- ance advocates. Mr. Moat,av has been twitted • fa;with being under the influence of " Whiggism "so as labour legislation is concerned,but he repudi- this charge in an effective fashion. " Fen- t „ Socialism," which he has condemned, and ...e'°rY Acts are, he contends, two very different 'lags. As to the beneficence of the Factory A.cts holds the same opinions now that he did when wrote about COBDEN many years ago, and hat he Wrote then was to the effect that if there •dpbeen no factory legislation the ingenious, the and the industrious working people of this rltrY would have been used up, and that the , • .;,%ation was wise, far-sighted, humane and o.7bseuociiii n ietnety Forom this it will be seen that there 0 ground for the injurious insinua- :that Mr. MORLEY has been lukewarm in his „„ ;71't of the HOME SECRETARY'S Factory Bill. 18' as a matter of fact, a warm and convinced , • i'Porter of that Bill and one of its most eloquent 'Bl°Pions on the platform. Mr. MORLEY is as Phatic, or even more emphatic, with 814 to the Local Option Bill, and he a verY distinct pledge as to the course the ei.rnrnent intend to pursue. "The Govern- . 0,44' he says, "are in earnest in this matter. flies' InaY rest assured we shall not draw back in ar,,gt:eat cause, but shall press oar proposals for- , lor the deliberate consideration and judg- t tof the House of Commons and the country." • Bee °ls clear from this that the Government -tvle definitely decided to stand or fall by the of temperance reform; they will, as Mr. ,IttaY finely puts it, quoting the words of ANTI, used in another connection, "raise a .4-"ard to which the wise and the honest can .cessf`i, Whether that standard be carried sue- there-,Y through the present Parliament or not - 'l to be no going back on temperance reform. at t . work has been definitely taken in hand by the b , _eta,' party as the great "instrument of pro-, 7. in this country, and there can in these days nO question that it is a work which will be suc- fully accomplished sooner or later. le his latest speech Lord Salisbury was guilty of ,ttolet4ilig the old and discredited dodge of trying otill_ke political opponents responsible for depres- the uf trade. Just look, he said,on one band at oney overflowing the coffers of capitalists ittli linkers, and, on the c.ther hand, at" the sullen *eh Bof the half-starved labourers," and then he 004 on to create the impression that the present 11,ernitent had caused "a black impassable strea hoistrust 5&gt; between the capitalist and the t oritirer: In his speech on Wednesday, Mr. John the? very properly described language via's: Sort when used by a leading statesman as us and wicked claptrap." He went further .4 that f or he went on to show that the depres- ta; °,silY commenced under:Lord Salisbury's own clinlstration. The great collapse of financial • tacit W4B in 1890 when Lord Salisbury was at the po of affairs, and in 1891 there was a great fall in 14 :18 of British and Irish products. There b 11 all, been a total decline of about forty-five th°" in exports and no less that thirty millions e decline took place before the Tories went out I Ofaee. tagn, qto;atsbire has long been the great centre of the 1 e rade of the world, and until the last few era b h shad no serious rival. Of late, however, i.Cer 'lintries have commenced to push forward in 3 tlpert''istry, and we have had many alarmist 8 about China and Japan, which countries, we b tetv Y pessimists will shortly capture the ler , heti, Place in the textile industries of the world. er this be the case or not, it is a matter of ' concern to Lancashire, especially now that still another competitor in the field. This 4lnscovite empire. Up to the present time th e Whole of the cotton goods in Russia were ••• doiae' by England, but the Russian Government all it can to encourage the erectionof q %ea ctories chiefly in the neighbourhood of ii,'t. When we consider this fact, and also ' competn in the iron trade, it is very t 'bike that England's supremacy in the world of ,ht 'ee is being seriously menaced,and it behoves lish People to prepare for this.</p>
        <p>Of e • clOblirBe, some people declare that England has •r,el,-,,'er Prime and that trade isUrad daily leaving All': "lie is overstating the case. The English trid."re still considered to be the best in the Nam,l,ttlel with such burdens as royalties, and tok.-;b'e rates of carriage removed, she could still -eltiir-e7 foreigners a clean pair of heels. What is 'to i 8 eema good social legislation which will thee i"a root of things. ' The nation is at present 4 K ,4 .4ethar,-.:_it:. and the danger is that it will not until it is too late. tlt Th Warrington Town Hall, Park and eooiCOramittee are at present engaged tiis i_verY useful work at the Park. ue remembered that during the late eation-r"tt an attempt was made to flood the re- rcnilld so as to provide skating for inhabi- t the town without their having to go so Willt,'3'• The attempt proved futile, however, va'd 41 the water escaping. The Committee alt.,"tkilied to be prepred for the frost next as re-g`i at the present time are having the end of 'ik'illlieedreati°rl ground near to Bewsey railway box 'utaillos,lll3. A splendid piece of ice will be thus --"3 if there is sufficient frost next year, and to this the appearance of the plot of Much improved. The space round the also being covered with asphalt, and On of the park will be much</p>
        <p>if 044 4'n t b to_ Stud</p>
        <p>Pnrti,</p>
        <p>" Paddy " has a great deal saddled upon him which he does not deserve, and it has become the universal custom to describe any bull as an hiber nianism. Irishmen do not, however, monopolise all the talent for perpetrating amusing incongruities of expression. At an inquest held in Grappenhall last week a witness—an Englishwoman—was asked whether the deceased person attended to his work, and with a gravity befitting the solemnity of the occasion she replied, that" he always was at work except when he, was away." Needless to say this self evident statement was received with much laughter.</p>
        <p>At the same inquest some light was thrown upon the question as to who is responsible for the inter- ment of a :dead body,—the Jparish where it was found, or that in which the inquest was held,if held in a different parish. It appears from Mr. Be Prez, relieving officer for the Runcorn Guardians, that it is theduty of the overseers of the parish in which the body is found who are responsible, and if they refuse to inter the body, they are liable to a penalty of £5. If Mr. Be Prez is correct, and there is no reason for believing that he is not, the question ought to be raised at various other townships which we could name, where inquests are held upon bodies found,in neighbouring townships.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. G. Bennett, who, it is interesting to note, is a native of Warrington, gave some figures. at a lecture at the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church, which show that for wandering about, the work of a connexional evangelist would be hard to beat. His peregrinations since September 1893 have reached between 13,000 and 14,000 miles, and he has been even further thin from Lands End to John o' Groats, as he has been to the Shetland Isles This is a wandering life indeed, and there will not be many people who know as much about England, Scotland, and Wales, as the Wesleyan connexional evangelist. Warringtonians will he glad to know that Mr. Bennett is very successful in his work. In fact it is just the kind of work that suits him, and he has entered into it with heartiness and enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>Mtn; 4118t1</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>Hearty congratulations to our new baronet. The birthday honour which has been conferred upon the member for the division—now Sir J. T. Brun- ner, Bart.,—has been generally considered to be a most fitting and well deserved one. Apart from Mr. Brunner's most valuable service to the Liberal party to which be has always been true, he has dis- played a generosity to numerous institutions both within and without his own constituency, almost unique. Educational institutions in particular,have benefited by his practical benevolence and in many instances by his personal assistance in the manage- ment of them. Runcorn has many times received of his liberality. Quite recently he provided a home for the Trades and Friendly Societies of the town, by presenting to them a Trade Hall, and in the course of a few weeks he will probably open the Runcorn Technical Institute—an institution he has taken a deep interest in, and generously subscribed to. Nor have Runcorn people forgotten his munific- ence during the recent distress when he placed £5O weekly at the disposal of the Relief Committee. The many messages of congratulations which Sir John and Lady Brunner have received testify the ap- proval of the public of the honour the Queen has conferred upon them. There is no doubt that Sir John Brunner enjoys the confidence of his con- stituents ; and he, at all events. may view with , equanimity the prospects of a dissolution of Parlia- ment.</p>
        <p>The election of a representative on the Runcorn Council for Newtown Ward has this week assumed a more definite form. On Wednesday—the last day for receiving nominations, it was found that two candidates had entered the field, and unless one of them retires by next Tuesday a poll will of neces- sity take place. Mr. Jones, of the London and North-Western Hotel, was the last to hand in his nomination paper, and this was done so late as Wednesday afternoon, that it was thought up to almost the last moment that Mr. Reynolds would have a " walk-over." This, however, is very im- probable now, and as both candidates reside in the ward, and are well known, a close contest may be expected.</p>
        <p>. -- Runcorn possesses a good reputation for several things, but as a town for bringing up good Rugby footballers it seems to stand pre-eminent. Those at all interested in Rugby football in the town should be pleased with the very satisfactory state- ments which were made at the annual meeting of the members of the Runcorn club on Monday. The first team results were in every way a credit to the club and players, and on compari- son they will be found to be far and away better than the season's results of many clubs in the neighbourhood. The second team report was even better than that of the first, as may be noted from the fact that only once dur- ing the season did they retire from the field defeated; and this is a good augury for the future of the Runcorn Club. Another gratifying feature was the announcement that Crompton, an old Run- corn player had been appointed captain of the Bradford team. This statement was greeted with applause and a letter of congratulation was ordered to be sent to him. With regard to the financial position of the club there is one black mark and that is the adverse balance, but this has been con- siderably reduced during the year. Favoured with a good season in 1895-6, this debit balance should be completely wiped out and the club thus be placed on a sounder footing.</p>
        <p>At the meeting ot the Runcorn Board of Guar- dians on Tuesday Mr. DePrez one of the relieving officers called the attention of the members to the fact that be had paid a sum of over .£2 as funeral expenses of a man who was found drowned in the Ship Canal. He stated that he only paid the money to prevent a scandal. It was clearly pointed out at the meeting that it was no duty of Mr DePrez's to bury the body, but that it was incum- bent upon the overseers of the township where the body was found to do so. In the case in question one of those little legal points presents itself for solution', for it appears that the body was first sighted in the township of Latchford but recovered from the water at Grappenhall. However, the Runcorn Guardians have written for the recovery of the amount paid to the Latchford overseers,who in turn must recoup themselves from the proper authorities. The case will no doubt tend to clear the issues and prevent a similar occurrence in future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>PENKETII.</head>
        <p>PENKETII.</p>
        <p>THE NEW SEXTON.—The selection of a suitable person to officiate as sexton and clerk, to St. Mary's Church, in place of Mr. R. Boardman (deceased) leas resulted in the appointment of Mr. J. Wheeler to the vacant position. 7be appointment should have boen made at the Easter vestry meeting, but it was left over for the Vicar and the churchwardens to select a suitable man. SANKEY SCHOOL BOARD,—The monthly meeting or the above Board was held in the Board-room, Great Sankiv, on Tuesday afternoon, when there were present—Mr. W. P. Skelton (chairman), the Rev. E. S. Jackson (vice- chairman), Mr. Henry Bate, Mr. W. Airelough, Mr. Joseph Wright, Mr. William Jeans, jun. (clerk), and Mr. George Clarke (head master.)—The Clerk having read the minutes of the lass meeting, he produced an account which had been sent in by Mr. Sutton for 56 days' salary amounting to £3 is. 6d. Some discussion took place as to the date when Mr. Sutton actually ceased to be clerk, and as to the amount of remuneration to which he is entitled, and it was ultimately decided, on the motion of the Rev. E. S. Jackson, "That Mr. Sutton be paid up to May 14, the day he received notification of Mr. Jeans' appointment."—Mr. Jeans said it would be necessary, for his appointment to be legal, for the Board to pass a formal resolution that day approving of his appointment. —The Rev. E. S. Jackson thereupon proposed the con- firmation of the appointment of Mr. William Jeans,itin., as clerk, the appointment to date from May 14th.—The motion was seconded by Mr. Fairclough and carried,and Mr. Jeans promised to do his best to conduct the business of the Board as it should be conducted.—The Clerk reported a balance in hand in favour of the Board of £49 7s. sd.—Mr. Clarke reported as follows :—May 4, on books 350, present 329, percentage 275.5, money received 14s. 4d. ; May U. 355, 336, 305.5, 14s. 103. ; May 18. 358, 336, 307.9, 139. 10d. • May 25, 361, 341, 312'5, 14s ; total money received. £2 17.4).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATUROAY, JUNE 1 1895</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATUROAY, JUNE 1 1895</p>
        <p>THUNDERSTORM AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On 'I hursday evening a thunderstorm of great severity visited Runcorn and neighbourhood. The weather had been very oppressive during the day ; but at about five o'clock rain commenced to fall. It was accompanied by loud thunderclaps and vivid flashes of lightning. The storm continued until about seven o'clock. There was, however, another thunderstorm soon after mid-night which lasted for several hours. THE SHIP CANAL WORKS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>During the week a number of men have been engaged in the coastruction of the conveniences for the repair of vessels at the Old Quay which are being oarried out by the Manchester Ship Canal Co., under the supervision of Mr. Wiswall. Piles have been driven at the entrance to the basin and excavations are being made.</p>
        <p>r, ANOTHER RUNCORN BANKRUPT. Tuesday night's " Gazette " announced that a receiv- ing order has been made against James Jolley, tinsmith. Runcorn.</p>
        <p>CLIFTON PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the Parish Council of Clifton was held on 'Thursday evening in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, Cholmondeley-road. There were present : Messrs. W. Fletcher, P. Massey, J. Stokes, W. Wilde and J. Wilson. Mr. Stokes was unanimously appointed chairman on the motion of Mr. Wilson, seconded by Mr. Massey. Mr. J. B. Holland was appointed treasurer of the Council, subject to his consent. A precept was signed requiring the overseers to pay over to the Council the sum of £lO. The returning officer presented an account for the cost of the Parish meeting and poll amounting to £2 13s. Id. This was passed for payment. rhe Clerk (Mr. John Rowland) was authorised to pur- chase the necessary books and Bt4tlooery for the use of the Council. It was unanimously resolved that the meetings oi the Council be held in the Primitive Method- Ft sch,ii am subject to a satisfactory arrangement being made.</p>
        <p>THE VACANCY IN NEWTOWN WARD. Wednesday afternoon was the latest time fixed by Mr. F. A. Lake' law clerk, for the receipt of nominations of candidates for the vacancy on the Runcorn Urban Coun- cil. Two candidates were nominated, and they both reside in Newtown ward where the vacancy occurs. Mr. Thomas Reynolds, general dealer, Shaw-street, is proposed by Thomas Riley, Old Soapery, seconded by William Rushton, 74, Greenway-road ; and Mr. T. Jones, of the London and North Western Hotel, Lowland-road, has for his proposer, Alfred Ashbrook, 16, Greenway- road, and is seconded by Fred Yarwood, 40,A1 bert-street. Four o'clock on Tuesday next has been fixed as the last hour for withdrawals. The election will take place on June 15th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN ANT) WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN ANT) WIDNES.</p>
        <p>MR. BRUNNER'S BARONETCY. At a meeting of the Runcorn Nonconformist Minis- ters' Association on Monday, the secretary was in- structed to forward a letter of congratulation to Sir John Tomlinson Brunner, M.P.,upon the recent honour which has been conferred upon him. ST. PAUL'S BIBLE CLASS. RUNCORN. Mr. B. Kniveton presided at the weekly meeting of this bible class on Sunday. There was a very good at- tesdance. Solos were well rendered by Mr. C. Atherton and Mr.B.Crosby. Mr. William Cooke was the organist, and contributed an organ solo.</p>
        <p>THE DISTRICT NURSE'S WORE. The Runcorn district nurse reports that there are 26 eases on the books. Four patients have died, one has left the town, and eight are convalescent. The number of visits paid to patients was 222, and 72 occasional visits were also made. THE ARMENIAN ATROCITIES. Lord Rosebery and Lord Kimberley have sent letters to the Rev. J. Simpson, pastor of Bethesda Congrega- tional Church, Runcorn, acknowledging the receipt of the resolution passed by the congregation on Sunday week with:regard to the atrocities which have been per- petrated in Armenia. THE TRADE OF RUNCORN. The chemical industry shows signs of increased activity, and work is now a little brisker than has been the case for some time past at the Runcorn works. The Whitsuntide stoppage will be for a few days only, an during this period some repairs will be carried out. With regard to the reported introduction of anew industry into the neighbourhood, no definite decision has been arrived at. ANNIVERSARY SERVICES AT SUTTON WEAVER. On Sunday anniversary sPrvi Gee -were held in tile Wesleyan Chapel, Sutton VS'eaver, afternoon and even- sng. The sermons were preached by the Rev. G. M. Chesters,of Didsbury. In addition to the ordinary hymns a nice selection of anniversary pieces was given by the members of the choir in an effective manner. The offer- tories realised £6 123. Li.</p>
        <p>BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNI-</p>
        <p>VERSARY.</p>
        <p>The anniversary was held on Sunday when the preacher was the Rev. H. Williams, of Sale. Tnere were good congregations both morning and evening. Special hymns were sung by the scholers and elviir. Mr. C. Le Couteur presided at the organ with his usual ability. Collections were taken on behalf of the Sunday school funds with fairly good results. RUNCORN AND TNN ALKALI Co. Mr. Eustace Carey ot the United Alkali Co., has for- warded a letter to Mr. E. Gatley, secretary to the Run- corn Trades' and Labour Council in response to their memorial to the Alkali Co. He states that he has noth- ing further to add to the communication already sent to the Runcorn Urban Council, a copy of which is sub- mitted, and which appeareci in the " Examiner " of last _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN PARISH CHLTRCH. -THE NEW CURATE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PARISH CHLTRCH. -THE NEW CURATE.</p>
        <p>The Vicar in his monthly letter appearing in the " Runcorn Parish Magazine " for June, states that Mr. H. C. Moyle, 8.A., of Lincoln College, Oxford,who hopes to be admitted to the order of deacons on Trinity Sun- day,will take up his work amongst them on Monday, June 10th, in succession to Mr. Holden. Mr. Moyle will have the oversight of the district in which Mr. Holden used to visit, and will also have the care of the temper- ance work and Band of Hope. The Vicar adds that he feels sure they will extend to him a very cordial wel- come.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MEMORIAL SERVICE AT WIDNES..</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE AT WIDNES..</p>
        <p>At the United Methodist Free Church, Widnes, on ISunday, the Rev. John Stafford, of Runcorn, made 'special reference to the recent death of Mr. E. Bate, of West Bank. He based his remarks on the words in Revelation xiv. 13. In the course of his address he re- ferred to the good Christian work done by Mr. Bate,who died at the early age of 32 years. He spoke of Mr.Bate's services in connection with the Sunday school, Band of Hope, and an active tract distributor. DISOBEYING A MAINTENANCE ORDER AT RUNCORN. At Runc.,rn Police Court, on Saturday morning, be- fore Messrs. T. Handley and E. Imison, William Horatio Fletcher, Vicar-st., Runcorn, was charged under a war- rant issued at the instance of his wife, Elizabeth Flet- cher, for disobeying a maintenance order made against him on April 22nd. He deserted her and she obtained an order for 7s. 6d. per week, the man undertaking to take charge of the children. He had not paid her any- thing, and he now owed £2 is. 61-11 e was :committed to gaol for 14 days. A DISGUSTING WOMAN AT RUNCORN. , At Runcorn Police Court, on Tuesday morning, before Mr. F, J. Norman and Mr. E. I.nison, a woman well knoWn to the police, named Ann Corcoran, of Mersey- St., was charged with having on the 18th inst. used pro- fane language i Mersey-st., P',d also with being drunk in Court on the previous mowing. The woman came to the Court on Monday to answer the summons of us- ing profane language, but she was in such a state of intoxication that Inspector Hicks found it necessary to place her in the cells.—She pl aded guilty to both charges and was fined 10s. and costs on the first charge, and ss. and costs on the second. THE QUEEN'S BIRTHDAY CELEBRATION AT RUNCORN. In honour of the 76th anniversary of the Queen's birthday, flags floated from the public offices and other places in Runcorn, and peals were rung on the bells of the Parish Church. In the evening there was a muster of the members of the local companies of Volunteers. They assembled at the Drill Hall, whence, in review oter, headed by the band, they marched to a field on Picow Farm. Here they formed up in line, and fired a feu de joie, the band playing the National Anthem bet- ween each round. They then went through the ceremony of the Royal salute,the band still performing "God save the Queen." After this there was an ordinary drill, which completed,the members returned to the Drill Hall, where they were dismissed. They were under the com- mand of Captain George F. Ashton, who was assisted by Lieutenant Brundrit. There was on parade 10 ser- geants and 70 rank and file, including the band, THE I.L.P. CONFERENCE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At the Trades Hall, Runcorn, on Saturday afternoon, a conference of delegates from the branches of the In- dependent Labour Party in the Lancashire and Cheshire federation was held. All the branches in the federation were represented,there being about 40 delegates present on behalf of over 1,000 members. The proceedings commenced with tea, after which a meeting was held, presided over,by Mr.Godhead,of Droylsden.Business hav- ing reference to the programme and management of the party was transacted. It was decided to fall in with the recommendation of the Executive Council regarding the holding of a conference for the purpose of consider- ing the party's action at the forthcoming general elec- tion. On the following day, Sunday, Mr. Grady, of Walkden, delivered a lecture in the open air at Weston, on " The General Programme of the Independent Labour Party." In the evening he addressed a meeting in Devonshire Square, Runcorn, basing his remarks on the words "By their fruits ye shall know them." Both meetings were well attended. CHURCH CYCLE PARADE FROM RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BetweWKETai-ici-siTrti cyclists belonging to Runcorn, Lostock Gralam,and also members of the Wigan Wednes- day club participated in a church cycle parade on Sun- I day. They started from Devonshire-square, Runcorn, for Antrobus, proceeding through Great Budworth where a break in the journey was made, and an excel- ' lent tea was partaken of. Antrobus was reached soon ' after six o'clock. The cyclists were granted permission to place their machines on the Vicarage lawn, and they the a repaired to the church where a special cyclists' service was held, conducted by the Rev. J.Holden,vicar. The service was of about forty minutes duration, and an address suitable to the occasion was delivered by the Vicar. Mr. H. Clare presided at the organ. The return journey was afterwards commenced, Runcorn being reached about nine o'clock.—On Wednesday afternoon, the club run was to Newton-le-Willows when a good number turned out. RUNCORN INDUSTRIAL BUILDING SOCIETY. On Wednesnay evening the tenth annual meeting of the above-mentioned society was held at the office, High-street, on Wednesday evening.. Mr. James Goodwell presided. The report and balance sheet were submitted, and showed that the society had a balance in hand of £43 23. Bd., and this was partly due to the di- rectors and secretary accepting very small remuneration for their services, the working expenses for the year be- ing Emly £29 19s. id. There was a prospective profit : from premiums of £4lO ; the property upon which ' £2,765 had been advanced was in a satisfactory condi- tion ; and no member was in arear with his repayments. The balance in the bank towards a further appropriation was £lBl 63. 6d. The report was adopted on the motion of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. John Poole. The retiring directors, Messrs. John Poole, James GAodwell, Edward Ridge, and Edward Newton, were re-elected. Mr. Joseph Pritchard was re-ap-- ' pointed amiitor;and Mr.Williain Handley was appointed public auditor. A vote of thanks was accorded Mr. J. IPritchard and Mr.E. Gregory for auditing the account, on the motion of Mr. J. E. Meredith. seconded by Mr.E. Ridge.—ln replying Mr. Pritchard drew attention to the premiums on the sale of appropriations which could only be considered as prospective profits, but they would begin to be realised after the principal advanced had been repaid.—The Chairman, in responding to a vote of thanks to the directors which was passed on the motion of Mr. J. Pritchard, seconded by Mr. G. H. S. Mason, said he was surprised that some of the members who had withdrawn had not seen their way to stop in the society. He knew of no institution of the kind which offered more advantage to its members, especially to workingmen. Mr. John Poole endorsed the chairman's remarks and' said he did not know of any society which could show a greater exercise of economy in regard to the working expenses. The secretary, Mr. J. E. Meredith was thanked for his ser- vices on the motion of Mr. Poole, seconded by Mr. James Goodwell. In reply, he said he felt the society had done good in the past, and would continue to do so io the future if the members would only hold together. A ballet for an appropriation was then taken and resulted in:number 2 being draw:4,7l)kb entitled Miss Eve Annie Savage, of High-street, to an advance of £2OO free of interest, to be repaid at the: rate of 8 per cent, per annum. A.- note of thanks havlng been passed to the cheirman the procredints term'ts.tr d..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WHITSUNTIDE ARRANGEMENTS.</head>
        <p>WHITSUNTIDE ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>Whitsuntide has always its yearly attractions at Run- corn. On Whit-Monday with bands playing and banners floating the various friendly societies parade the town, followed later on in the day by a procession of Sunday school children, which is one of the sights of the year. Should the weather be favourable, the children will pro- ceed to fields in the neighbourhood and engage in vari- ous sports; tea being provided for them. In Runcorn there is no mistaking when Whitsuntide is drawing near. For some weeks prior to the popular holiday a general cleaning and decorating of the houses is commenced; and tradesmen in wearing apparel become busy. This year there has been a depression of trade in the town and many will, in consequence, have to curtail expendi- ture. The works in the town will be closed from Satur- day until Wednesday, but the school children are to enjoy a week's holiday. Cheap excursions have been arranged by the Railway Company to numerous places, and on Tuesday a sail down the Mersey to Liverpool and New Brighton has been arranged. As will be seen from an advertisement in another column, Mr. Shaw, of the Mersey Mission to Seamen has again, this year, made arrangements for a steamer to leave the Pier Head at Runcorn for Liverpool and New Brighton on Tuesday morning. The prices are moderate, and favoured with fine weather, the trip should prove a most enjoyable One.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WESiON.</head>
        <p>WESiON.</p>
        <p>--- WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.—OH Sunday the anniversary ser vices in connection with this school were held in the Wesleyan Chapel, Weston Point,vvhen the Rev. Edward Thomson, of Didsnury College, was the preacher. There were large congregations morning and evening who greatly appreciated the discourses. In the afternoon the sacred edifice was well filled to listen to a service of song, entitled " Little Minnie." The choir and scholars gave an excellent interpretation of the work, under the conductorship of Mr. Stubbs. Miss Berrington presided at the organ. The chair was occu- pied by Mr. James Taylor, and the connective readings were given by Mr. J. M.Frith. The collections realised £B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A SEXTON.—The death is announced of Mr. William Lowe, of Frodsham, who has held the position of sexton at the Iron Church, Frodsharn, for a consider- able time,and has earned the respect and goodwill of all. On Sunday he attended to his several duties at the church, but feeling unwell during the evening service he returned home. It was ascertained subsequently that he was suffering from acute pneumonia, and notwithstand- ing every endeavour to arrest the attack, he died on Thursday early in the afternoon. PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE—The monthly meeting of the Frodsham Parochial Committee was held on Friday evening in the Courtroom, Frodsham, when Mr. H. E. Eleyw@od presided. The Inspector submitted a long re- port as to the service of notices with regard to water connections, from which it appeared that some of the owners of property had already connected with the water mains; others had not, however, done what was needed. After considering the various cases the Inspec- tor was directed to obtain tenders for carrying out the requisite works forthwith. The Inspector also submitted the County Analyst's report upon four samples of ,water which he had examined--Mr.l. Edwards' pump well,Mr. R. Tickle's pump well, Mr. A. Bate's well, and a flow of water near to Mill-bank. In the two first cases these were pronounced as not fit for domestic use, and in the two latter cases they were pronounced as good waters and fit for domestic use. The necessary orders were then Issued with respect thereto. The minutes were confirmed at the meeting of the Runcorn Rural Council on Tues- day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PAWNBROKERS' AND TEE HIRE SYSTEM._</head>
        <p>PAWNBROKERS' AND TEE HIRE SYSTEM._</p>
        <p>The House of Lords on Thursday overruled a decision of the Appeal Court, and held that a pawnbroker who has accapted a piano which had been purchased on the hire sydem and only partially paid for was responsible to the vendor for the piano. Their lordships gave judg- ment accordinglytand ordered,the pawnbroker to pay the appellant all his costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MUSIC AS -MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>MUSIC AS -MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>" Science Siftings" tells us that Professor Tarchanoff, of St. Petersburg, made use of a measuring instrument of precision styled the " erg,og,raph." He found that if the fingers were completely fatigued,either by voluntary efforts or by electric excitation, to the point of being incapable of making any mark except a straight line on the registering cylinder, music had the power of making the fatigue disappear, and the finger placed in the ergograph again commenced to mark lines of different heights according to the amount of excitation. It was also found that music of a sad and lugubrious character had the opposite effect, and could che3k or entirely inhibit the contraitions. Professor Tarchanoff does not profess to give any •positive explanation of these facts, hut he inclines to the view that " the voluntary muscles, being furnished with excito- ' motor and depressent fibres, act in relation to the music similarly to the heart—that is to say, that joyful music resounds along the excito-motor fibres and sad music along the depressentor inhibitory fibres." Experiments on dogs showed that music was capable of increasing the elimination of carbonic acid by 16.7 per cent., and of increasing the consumption of oxygen by 20•1 per cent. It was also found that music increased the func- tional activity of the skin. Professor Tarchanoff claims as the result of these experiments that music may fairly be regarded as a serious therapeutic agent, and that it exercises a genuine and considerable influence cver the functions of the body.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MA RKETS.</head>
        <p>MA RKETS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MARKET.—WEDNESDAY, May 297 a FRUIT. VRIaItTABLEI, POUGTRY. BUTCHRILS'AND</p>
        <p>FISHMONGER TABLE.</p>
