      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. J. Mc Intyre, J.P., occupied the chair,and there were also pre- sent ;—Admiral Jones Parry Jones, Messrs. J. Knowles, J. Weir, T. Lewis, J. Stokes, T. Wright, J. W. Baguley, G. Priestner, E. Bratherton, J. Spruce, W. Pickering, J. M. Williams, H. E. Heywood, T. Darlington, J. L.Jenes, S. Woodward, J. B. Chadwick, J. Brandreth, J. Darby- shire, W. Turner, C. Roylance, A. Greenway, Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk), Mr. Swinton (surveyor), and Mr. Farrington (nuisance in- spector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER.</head>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICER.</p>
        <p>A telegram was read from Dr. Adams in which he r. ttted that owing to ill-health he was unable to be pi ent. He was at the time in the Isle of Man.—Mr. D,rhyshire wished to know who was attending to Dr. A fams':duties in his absence.—Mr. Heywood explained ~at Dr. Curtis was performing the duties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LATE ED- DISBURY 'HIGHWAY BOARD.,</head>
        <p>THE LATE ED- DISBURY 'HIGHWAY BOARD.,</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd wrote forwarding a statement of balances Paid to the treasurer of the Council by the late West Eddisbury Highway Board. The enclosure was as fol- lows :—Alvaniey £l3 ss. 3d.. Frodsham Lordship £23 18s. lid., Frodsham £3l ss. 7d., Helsby £l7 15s. Si.. Kingsley £33 10s. 7d., Kingswood £lO 45., Manley £l4 ss. 9d., Newton-by-Frodstiam £8 7s. 5d., and Norley £lO is. 81., total £l5O Os. 10d.</p>
        <p>REPORT OF THE HIGHWAY _COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brandreth read the report of the Highway Com- mittee which stated that it be an instruction to the In- spector of Nuisance and the Surveyors that all nuisances on highways be dealt with, and that all sewers and out- falls be under the supervision of the surveyors acting as inspectors of nuisances and that all other matters be attended to by the Inspector of Nuisances, and also that it be understood that each of the officers notify to the other any matter coming under his observation as might require the attention of such officer. Further that farmers be paid for carting stone at a rate not exceeding 83. per ton delivered on the highway where the surveyors re- quired them, the Surveyors to get them broken before being so delivered ; and that the price of cartage be at the rate of 12s. per day for two horses and a man, and 7s. per day for one horse and a man, and if paid for by weight, at a rate not exceeding Is. per ton for the first mile and 9d. per ton for each mile afterwards. Be moved the adoption of the report of the committee. Mr. Woodward seconded the motion. A discussion en- sued as to the way stones had been delivered f ormerly under the Highway 'Boards, and Mr. Roylance stated that from his own observations be could say that many tons of stones had found their way into the canal in the course of unloading the canal boats.—Mr. Stokes said he was in a position to confirm the statement.—The report was ultimately adopted, and it was also resolved that instructions be given to the surveyors to occasionally take the weight of the stones delivered.—Mr. Heywood made a recommendation that their surveyors do not use any more salt scale on the roads until the present ex- periment had been completed ; and also that the County Council be asked to cease using such rubbish. It was not the usual material to use for the purpose.—Mr. Wright seconded, and said there had been numerous complaints made by the residents at Halton. The stuff was carried into the houses and it was very objectionable.—Mr. Chadwick stated that he had been away from the works at which he was manager for about two weeks, but on his return he found that tons of salt scale had been taken away. This was not suitable for roads, pan scale could be more advantageously used. The former part of the resolution was carried, but it was decided not to communicate with the County Council. THE NUISANCE INSPECTOR'S REPORT. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Farrington, nuisance inspector. ;..dt,nitted his report stating that an analysis of the watf‘,- from Helsby directed to be taken from the pump of M. Brandreth, had been made. It had been certined as satisfactoiy. An analysis of water from a well at the farm occupied by Mr. Brocklehurst was also returned as good water. He also reported a house without water at Moore belong- ing to the Joint Railway Company and he was instructed to serve a notice for a supply to be furnished. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the fortnightly meeting of this Board was held in Dutton Workhouse under the presid- ency of Mr. Joseph Knowles. In addition to the members of the Rural Council being present there were :—Rev.Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. T. Handley, J.P., T. Lea and S. Yarwood ' • Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. G. Ashton (deputy clerk) and Mr. Cunnah (workhouse master). THE TIME OF MEETING.</p>
        <p>A letter from the Local Government Board was read with reference to the proposal of the Runcorn Union and of the Rural Djstrict Council to alter the hour of their ordinary meetings. It stated that it appeared to the Board that in the absence of any further order made by them in the matter it was competent to the Guardians and the Rural District Council to alter the day and hour of their meetings without any sanction on the part of the Board. They, however, requested that any alteration of the day or hour of the meetings might be reported to them as soon as practicable after it had been determined upon.—The Clerk stated that the alteration in the time of meeting of the two bodies would take effect a fort- night hence. THE REGISTRATION BOHNOARY.</p>
        <p>A communication from the Registrar-General was read, in which he stated that with the sanction of the Local Government Board,he had made an order affecting, on the Ist July next, certain changes in the boundaries of Warrington and Runcorn Registration districts. A copy of the notice of the order was enclosed,from which it appeared that Latchford Without, Thelwall,and Grap- penhall will be transferred from Latchford sub-district of Warrington Registration district to Budworth sub- district of Runcorn Registration district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>APPOINTMENT OF NURSES.</head>
        <p>THE APPOINTMENT OF NURSES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Yarwocd reported that seven applications were received in answer to the advertisements for two nurses at a salary of £25 per year with uniform, rations, and £4 ;n lieu of beer. The committee appointed to consider the applications had asked four to attend that Board in order that the Guardians might decide. The candidates who appeared before the Board were :—Mrs. Key, 36, Andrew-st., Walton, Liverpool ; Mrs. Rebecca Ryan, 56, Boundary-at., Liverpool ; Miss Annie Hughes, Portma- doc ; Mrs. Fielding, Rose Cottage, New Moston, near Manchester.—The result was that Miss Hughes and Mrs. Fielding were appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Heywood submitted tie Finance Committee's re- port which stated that the amount paid in out-relief was about the same as usual, that in Frodsham district being a little lower. Accounts to the amount of £298 were recommended for payment. The balance in hand was £2,225 6s. 6d. The precept from the County Coun- cil was received for £4,175 7s. 4d., made up as follows: —County rate. £3.155 ss. ; constabulary general expen- diture, £262 17s. ed. ; constabulary local expenditure, £757 4s. 10d. The general county rate was 3d. in the ; the general expenditure for the county constabulary force was fd. in the £, and that the local expenditure of the county constabulary force was in the £. The balance in hand would thus be soon wiped out. He would remind those Guardians who were overseers to have their rates paid in at the earliest possible date. The call of the County Council for the last half year was for £2,943, and for the corresponding peri last year £3,759. This did not include the three townships of Latchford Without, Grappenhall, and Thelwall, which had been added to the Union. The net increase was £125.</p>
        <p>THE PREVENTION OF A BURIAL SCANDAL NEAR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. De Prez trformed the Foard that he had paid £2 4s. as funeral expenses of a man who was found drowned. The body was discovered at Latchford but It was towed up to the locks which were in Grappenhall. He understood that it was the duty of the overseers of the township where the body was found to bury the per- son ;he only did it to prevent a scandal.—The Clerk stated that where a body was found on the 9bore of any tidal or navigable water it was the duty of the overseers, to bury it and recover the money from the county treasury.—Mr. De Prez said he knew he had done at illegal act in paying the money, and he thought in would be advisable to write to rho Local Government Board so that they might settle the matter.—Mr.Wright said they had a similar case at Halton some time ago.— It was ultimately decided that the Clerk write to the overseers of Latchford bringing under their notice the fact that they had neglected to bury the body, and Ito ask for the amount to be repaid, pointing out that they had been liable to a penalty of £5 ; and also that he write to the Local Government Board on the subject. This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>ALARMING COLLIERY ACCIDENT AT MANCHESTER.</head>
        <p>ALARMING COLLIERY ACCIDENT AT MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>THREE HUNDRED AND EIGHTY MINERS IMPRISONED.</p>
        <p>An accident, whicti--;;;To-rt.u—nraely unattended with fatal results, but which caused a painful sensation in the district, occurred on Wednesday at the Bradford Colliery, Manchester. At half-past one o'clock a cage, which was on its way up to the main shaft, got jammed among the guiding timbers, and so upset the gearing that it was;found impossible either to remove it or be get the other cage working in the same shaft in proper order. The result of this was that some 380 miners who were at work in the pit at the time were put to considerable inconvenience. They were, per- force, detained in the workings for many hours ' beyond the time when they were due at the pit bank. In order to release them from their enforced imprison- ment the upset shaft was utiliz,d. A bucket arrange- ment was got to work, hut owing to the bottom of the shaft being difficult of access from the place where the men were at work, the task of bringing them to the sur• face was long and tedious. It was not expected late on Wednesday night that the last of the miners kept below would reach home until an early hour on Thursday morning.lt is fortunate that the accident occurred when it did, for an hour later it would have been the time to I wind up the shaft, and as over a dozen men are lifted in the cage at once,fatal results might have been expected.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE RUNCORN</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C., presided at the annual meeting or the Runcorn Football Club on Monday even- ing which was held in the large room adjoining Mr. Riley's café, Bridge-et. Amongst those also present there were :—Dr. McKenzie, Messrs. W. Carroll, W. Jones, A. Lightburn, S. Abrams, E. Key, T. Robinson, W. Wright, A. Riley, J. F. Moulton, H. Hughes, S. Houghton, W. Nieid, W. Faulkner, W. H. Cawley, J. Stubbs and T. Warder, Mr. Pownall (treasurer), Mr. Langshaw (secre- tary), and Mr. Ellison (assistant secretary). The Secretary read his annual report which was pub- lished in last week's " Examiner." He thought it was highly satisfactory, and he was sure they ought to be very pleased with both teams in doing so well as they had done. (Applause.) THE TREASURER'S STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Pownall, treasurer, gave a statement of the re- ceipts and expenditure during 1894-5. The cash ac- counts showed that the season commenced with a balance ut 14s. 4d. ; the subscriptions from 33 players realised £6 16s. 6d. ; 166 honorary members, £6O 25.6 d.; 43 stand members, £ll 163. 6d. ; 9 season ticket holders £1 14s. 61. ; advertisement, 103. ; gate receipts for 32 matches, £458 43. 5.1.; stand receipts' £2l 17s. Cl.; balance Runcorn Cricket Club, season 1894, £4 163. id.; amount returned by county for fares and from insurance company, £l6 183. 5.1. ; Parr's Banking Company, over- draft, £169 16s. 2J. ; total, £753 63. lid. The payments were : By Parr's Banking Company, overdraft, season 1893.4, £l7O 143. 9.1. ; insurance of players, £3l ss. ; hire of conveyances, £4 19.3. Id. ; advanced cricket sec- tion for season 1895, £4 163. id. ; subscriptions, R.F.U., C.F.U., and L.D.C., £1 16s. eel. ; sundries, S.l. lls. 4.1. ; bank interest and commission,£7 9s. 8.1. ; field expenses, £3O 13s. sd. ; rents, rates and taxes, £57 7s. 103. ; printing, stationery, and advertising, £6l 143. 2.1. ; gatemen and police, £2B 2s. 6d. ; referees, £lB 53. 4.1. ; hotel and travelling expenses, £164 es. 10d. ; refreshments, £44 4s. 7d. ; postages, telegrams, and secretary's sundries, £l3 193. 2d. ; footballs, pants, jerseys, boots, embrocation, and doctor's fees, £9O 193. 81. ; salaries, £2O 193. ; total, £753 6i. lid. The balance account stood :-To Parr's Banking Company, overdraft, £169 163. 2d. ; to balance in favour of club, £BO 43. ; total, £250 Os. 2.1. By cash in secretary's hands, £25 ; by loan to cricket section, £25 ; by stand (less 5 per cent. depreciation), £ll5 Os. 10.1.; by pants, jerseys, boots, bags, bath fittings. &amp;c., estimated value, £42 173. 6.1. ; total, £250 Os. 2d. The accounts had been audited and found correct by Messrs. E. Lunt and T. C. Cooper.-The Chairman formally moved the adop- tion of the report and balance sheet,which was seconded by Mr. Nield, and unanimously carried. Mr. P. Speakman said they had just closed a season, the result of which must be gratifying Nall members and supportors of the club. The good football for which Runcorn had for years been famous, had been fully maintained. The past season had been one of very severe weather, the ground for weeks having been frost bound, and this together with the depression in trade in the neighbourhood had interfered very much with the receipts of the club. But notwithstanding these draw- backs they would be pleased to hear that the adverse balance of last year had been reduced by about £9.), showing that whilst the players had done their utmost on the field to maintain the fame of the club the commit- tee had by careful management strengthened the finan- cial position of the club. (Applause.) It was a grati- fication to him as it was to them, to find that their men had done right nobly. Be did not know a season when the players seemed to pull themselves together as they had done during the past season. It was not only a gratification to the gentlemen present but to the town generally, to have Runcorn Football Club known far and wide. He was very glad indeed that they had appointed a most efficient management and also a financial committee. They had done magnificent work and had given long considera- tion to the items to be liquidated. Last year they had accounts unpaid, but the working expenses of the present year had not only been met but their debts had been. reduced. The balance owing to the bank was partly due to the purchase of a grand stand. He was proud of the fact that they possessed such a field and stand, so that in wet weather visitors could go under cover. (Applause.) Ile could not say too much in praise of their secretaries and treasurer. They bad per- formed their duties well. It was highly satisfactory to find themselves in such a good position for at the com- mencement of the season their liabilities caused no small concern to some, although not to him. He understood that on the occasion of the Chester regatta in July the club was to have a day's outing and he (Mr. Speakman) would be pleased to accompany them and give them all the pleasure he could. (Applause.) In conclusien he asked them to record a vote of thanks to the members of the committees and players.-Mr.R.Hughes seconded the proposition and said the thanks of every member of i the club were due to the committees for the excellent work they had done. He was very pleased with the balance sheet which had been presented that evening, as he had not expected that it would have been so favour- able. (Applause.) The motion was carried unani- mously. '</p>
        <p>RE•ELECTION OF PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Lightburn proposed, Mr.W.Faulkner seconded, and it was carried unanimously, that Mr. P. Speakman, J.P., C.C, be re-elected president of the club.—Mr. P. Speakman thanked the members for the honour con- ferred upon him.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF OFFICIALS. The Chairman moved,and Mr. Jones seconded,that the secretary, treasurer, and assistant secretary be re-ap- pointed. The motion was carried unanimously.—Messrs. W. Faulkner and G. Robinson were ballotted for the position of captain of the first team. The result was the election of Mr. Faulkner, who received 34 votes to his opponent 20. Mr. G.Robi nson was unanimously re-elected sub-captain. Messrs. T. Antrobus and J.Lightfoot were the candidates for the position of captain of the second team, and the latter was elected by 36 votes to 17. Four persons stood for the position of sub-captain of the se- cond team, viz.. Messrs. Antrobus, Clarke, Gregory, and Woodward. Mr. Gregory was elected. Mr.J.J. Stubbs was appointed linesman for the first team, and Mr. J. Nickson for the second tram. The auditors, Messrs. Cooper and Lunt, were re-elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>FORMER PLAYER COMPLIMENTED.</head>
        <p>A FORMER PLAYER COMPLIMENTED.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he would like to state that he had heard with very great pleasure that one of their old players, Crompton, had been selected captain of the Brad- ford team. He thought this was a very great compli- ment to Runcorn,and he moved that a letter of congratu- lation be sent from that meeting to him. (Applause.)— The motion was carried with enthusiasm.</p>
        <p>THE COMMITTEE. A ballot was taken ror the purpose of electing twelve members on the committee, There were 25 persons nominated but of these nine withdrew. The result of the vote was : S. Abrams, 55 ;W. Jones, 50 ; W. Car- roll, 51; W. H. Cawley, 50; A. Lightburn, 59; T. Robinson, 40 • W. Bo.ckett, 36 ;H. Farmer, 34 ; E. Bey, 32; J. Fraser, 29. Three persons, viz. : T. Anderton, J. Goodwell, and D. Light- foot each received 25 votes and it was necessary that annther vote should be taken. This resulted as follows : —Lightfoot, 43 ; Goodwell,36 ; and Anderton, 25.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>HONORARIUM FOR THE ASSISTANT SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>HONORARIUM FOR THE ASSISTANT SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Carroll said their assistant secretary had done the work of a Trojan,ancl some of them thought that he was underpaid. He therefore proposed that a bonus of £2 be voted to Mr. Ellison.—Mr. W. Jones seconded the motion, which was unanimously carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>TIIANE9.</head>
        <p>TIIANE9.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to the scrutineers Messrs. Nieid, H. Wright, Cragg and A. Riley, on the motion of the Chairman seconded by Mr. %V. Jones.—Dr. McKenzie said he desired to move a hearty vote of thanks to the president. Last year Mr. Speakman said he would like to remain at the helm of the ship until they had got out of their difficulties. This year they had done remarkably well and the guidance of the vessel depended upon the man at the helm. If they had Mr. Speakman as presi- dent they would get on " swimmingly." (Applause.)— Mr. Lightburn seconded the motion, and it was carried unanimously.—Mr. Speakman responded and the meet- ing termined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>QUEEN'S BIRTHDAY.</head>
        <p>THE QUEEN'S BIRTHDAY.</p>
        <p>THE LIM' OF HONOURS.</p>
        <p>The following list of birthnay honours was issued at the Premier's official residence last evening ._ • .._._ • .. _ .</p>
        <p>To be sworn of her Majesty's Privy '6 ouncil—Lord Leigh, and Sir Henry Brougham Loch, G.C.8., G.C.M.G.</p>
