      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MOST IMPORTANT.</head>
        <p>MOST IMPORTANT.</p>
        <p>Every person's future is, to a certain extent, in his own band. Many a future which might have been brilliant has been darkened by lack of the spirit of self- help. Many a borne which might have been gladdened by life and health, and comfort, has been saddened by poverty, illness, and, death, in many cases wholly pre- ventible. The spring and summer months are, from a sanitary point of view, among the most important of the year. Due care may now mean a healthy and happy summer and autumn, and robust constitution to meet the tricds of next winter. Neglect may now seem a joy- less summer, a trying autumn, and a broken constitu- tion to succumb tind,,r the attacks of any exceptional trial. There are in&amp;ed few that do not feel the system needs recruiting alter the trying winter they have passed through. With seine it is "only a little cough," and others, " a tendency to bronchitis," with others, " not quite up to the mark," a sort of all over sinkingfeeling," "too weak to do anything about the house," "these recurring headaches,"" no appetite for my food,"I These and a dozen others are common expressions which all p,4int to the urgent need of some good tonic. What change of air can do for you in a few months' time—if you are no worse then than you are now—a course of some good tonic mixture, a reliable medicine of established reputation and of proved virtue will do now. There are several tonic mixtures to be had, but none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. The vegetable tonic. It has never been known to fail ; send for a pamphlet of testi- monials and read the voluntary testimony therein con- tained. Then get Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters,whic.h will prove to be your best self-help. CAUTION.—Avoid imitations. See that yo a get Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. Do not be persuaded to buy any other. See the name " Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters " on the Label, Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
        <p>Prices : Bottles, 23.9 d. ; double size, 43. sd. Sample bottles, is. lid. To be had of all Chemists, or direct from the Proprietors :</p>
        <p>QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING CO., LIMITED. LLANELLY, SOUTH WALES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>BEFORE TIM MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE TIM MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL KICKING OUTRAGE. At the Warrington Borough Court, on Thursday, be- fore the Mayor and Alderman Sutton, John 1 annion, 18 Dolman's lane, was charged with assaulting and beating his wife Annie Mannion on the 18th May.—lnspector Spinks said that on Monday the defendant's wife made application with her head bandaged up and three stitches in her jaw, for a warrant which was granted her. Wit- ness made enquiries about the defendant and found that he had left the town. He advised .the woman to go into the workhouse. At 7 o'clock on Wednesday night wit- ness received a telephonic message from Stockport say- ing that defendant had surrendered himself there. Wit- ness reported the matter to the Chief Coustable and by his instructions went the same evening to Stockport and received defendant in custody. When the warrant was read over to him the defendant said, "I should never have done it if she had not been keeping company with Mrs. Carr," a woman, witness explained, who was now in gaol. Defendant's wife had since gone to Staly- bridge not knowing her husband was arrested. They had however two witnesses present who saw the assault and the case could be gone on in the absence of prosecu- tor.—Mrs. Burke said that on thelBth of May complain- ant was in Mrs. Cain's house and defendant came in. He charged his wife with drinking with the woman pre- viously mentioned and an argument ensued ; defendant got angry, and as complainant was sitting on a stool he sifted his foot and kicked her in the face causing her jaw to be lacerated and to bleed very much.—Mrs. Cain gave corroborative evidence.—P.C. Wood said that about 8 o'clock on the night in question the woman came to him bleeding very much from the jaw and her apron was -Jr,. saturated with blowl. She was conducted to the Infir- ,-..pary where her face was stitched.—Defendant denied tr :eking complainant, and said that Ise only gave her a ..s.ddr;out with his fist and suggested that she fell against the - magistrates characterised his behaviour as ; brutal and fined him 203. and costs or one month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>MINOR CASES. -- WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. -- WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. Alderman Wright. BRAWL BETWEEN GREENGEOCERS.—CharIotte Finch, Foundry-street, was charged with using threats on the 21st May to Sarah Cliffe, Foundry-street. —Mr. Browne prosecuted and asked that the defendant should be bound over in a substantial sum.—Mrs. Cliff' e the com- plainant, stated that on Tuesday evening the defendant made use of the fearful threats which were put on paper for the information of the bench. She was afraid to go out without her husband in consequence of the defend- ant. —James Cliffe, husband of complainant, also gave corroborative evidence. Several witnesses were called for the defence who alleged that complainant had caused the bother with striking the son of defendant. The latter was bound over to keep the peace in one surety of £5 and also ordered to pay the costs. Monpay.—Before the Mayor (Councillor James Fair.. dough Mr. J. Fairclough, and Mr. S. Wright. DISORDERLY TOPERS.—John Hares, 2, Berry's Yard, was fined ss. and costs for being drunk and disorderly on Saturday night ; William Ly-hgoe, 33, Sharpe-st.,for a like offence, 103. and costa. A TRADESMAN'S GRIEVANCE.--JOSeph S. Garland, iron- monger, Bridge-at., was charged with obstructing the passage of Rylands-st., on the 21st May.—P.C. Chrimes said that on the day in question he saw a reaping machine belonging to the defendant in Rylands-at., opposite to his shop. Witness cautioned the shopman who took it In but it was afterwards brought out again and left in the street 25 minutes. Re then reported the matter.— Mr. Garland said that he himself knew absolutely noth- ing about the matter and he did not receive the sum- Mons until Friday. His premises, he added, were situ- ated in a narrow part of Bridge-st. They had a passage at one side for the delivery of goods which was in joint occupation of three people and it was a great difficulty to keep it free. All goods coming to the shop had to be delivered at the entrance. On Tuesday morning it was very likely that his men took the machine out of the narrow part of Bridge-at., and placed it out of the way in Rylands-st., which was a wide thoroughfare, and there could not possihlv be any actual obstruction. He thought it very hard that he stiouid be brought to the police court for such a charge. Be had been in Warring- ton 20 years in business and he had contributed £l,OOO in rates and this was the first time he had been sum- moned for anything of that nature.—Tbe magistrates took a lenient view of the case and fined defendant in the costs (4s. 61.) DANGEROUS GAMES IN THE STREET.—The following youths were fined is. each or in default to be locked up in the cells for the day, ter playing football in Bank. street on the 19th May to the annoyance of passengers: —John McLoughlin, (12), 6 Blaekhurst-street ; Martin Dolly, (13), 13 Town Hill : William Carey, (12) 13 Town Hill ; William Glavin, (12). 17 Bank-street ; Thomas Conway, (14i, 25 Dolman's-lane ; Martin Conway, (12), ' 25 Dolman's-lane John Durkin, (14), 35 Dolman'-lane; Patrick Caine, (11), 18 Dolman's-lane; Patrick Caine. John Maguire, (14), 10 Cloth-hall yard; George Maguire, (11), 16 Cloth-hall yard. The five bel 99v mentioned were similarly dealt with for playing cricket in Friars' Green on the 20th May. Patrick Garritty, (13), 3 Friars'- lane ; Thomas Collins, (12), 5 Stanley-street ; William Connor, (11), 8 Friars'-Green ; Andrew Mannion, (13), 3 Black's-yard ; John Jackson, (11), 9 Arpley-Walk. TuEsney.—Before Mr. R. Garnett and Dr. T. S. Smith. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE PROSECUTIONS.—A WARNING TO INCORRIGIBLES.—The following persons' were dealt with for not having sent their children regularly to school : Thomas Sloane, 23, Crossley-st., was summoned for the 18th time, and his boy being proved to be inc3rrigible, the latter was sent to the ship" Clio" until he attains the age of 16 ; William Thompson, Bay Horse-yard, was fined 2s. 6d.; James Bibby. School-brow, 5 ; William Ryder, Erwood-st., 29. 61. ; Joseph Poulter, Bewsey- road, 55. ; Peter Phillips, Lovely-lane, 24. 61.; John Dutton, Silver-square, 24. 61. ; Thomas Fearnley, Ellen- at., ss. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman), Mr. J. Geddes, and Mr.J. 11. Fleming. DRErNIENNESS.—EIIen Rainford was fined 54. and costs or seven days' imprisonment for being drunk and disorderly in Sankey-St., at midnight on Tuesday ; Frederick Lunn was charged with committing the same offence in Horsemarket-st., on Tuesday night. He pleaded guilty, anr4 his employer, Mr. Clay (Williams and Clay), gave evidence as to his previous good char- acter.—A fine of 54. was imposed. FALSE ATTE.STATION.--William Caldwell was charged with making a false answer to being attested for the Militia on the 31st May.—Sergeant Jaeger proved the case, and said the prisoner had been attested at St. Helens, where he said he bad never been in the army, whereas he had been invalided from the Warrington depst in April, last year.—The lowest penalty,£s or one month's imprisonment was impeeed. DAMAGING SHRUBS IN THE PARE.—Thomas Gibbons, last from Manchester, was charged with being drunk in Bank Park at 3.10 a.m., on Wednesday morning ; he was further charged with committing wilful damage to the trees to the amount of is. 61—P.C. Bell stated that at the time in question he was in Golborne-Bt., and from what he had heard, searched the Park in company with p.c. Evans,and they found the defendant lying on some news that he had bent down,and which were damaged</p>
        <p>in consequence. He had the flowers produced in his hand.—Defendant expressed his contrition, and was fined ss. and costs. Ors? Licit:lsm—Henry Whitfield was granted tempor- ary authority to sell at, an off-licence in Orford-lane in lieu of William Green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>TLTESDAY.—Bef ore Mr. J. C. Parr (ehairman), Mr. H. Nield,Mr. H. Talbot,and Alderman Greenelt. DRUNTLENNEES.—For this offence committed at Preston-o'th-Hill on May sth, Patrick Morris Rowe was fined 2s. 61 John Morris Rowe was also fined 53. in- cluding costs. SCHOOL CASES.—For neglecting to send their children regularly to school, Thomas Wilkes, Stockton Heath,was fined 3s. 6d.; Joseph Shaw, Grappenhall, 5i.; William Brougtiton, Stockton Heath, discharged with a caution ; George Ainsworth, Stretton, ss. THEFT OF A GOOSE.—William Getty was charged with stealing on the 10th May, at Little Leigh,a goose valued Ll, the property of William Brock. The complainant said that he was a farmer at Little Leigh. On May 10th at about five p.m., he drove his geese into the stack yard while he did some work in another part of the I farm. About an hour afterwards in consequence of what his daughter told him, he went to the flock of geese and found one missing. He went along the road and after a time, he found some feathers. A little further, he found both blood and feathers where the goose had been killed. He could not find any further trace of the goose and then went to Barnton and in- , formed the police. He saw the goose dead the same night.—P.S. Hanby said that from information re• ceived, he went to the defendant's house and saw his coat hanging on the wall with blood and feathers upon it. He also found the dead body of the goose upstairs. Defendant was sober at the time, hut witness believed he had been drinking during the day.—The defendant pleaded guilty and expressed his sorrow at committing the theft, and promised to reform in the future.—The bench inflicted a penalty of 20s. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Mompay.—Before Mr. Reynolds (chairman) Mr. F. J. Norman, and Mr. E. Imison. Licaosmo.—Richard Jones applied for permission to sell at the Bridgewater Arms, Gilbert-street, lately occu- pied by William M. Williams. The application was granted. POOR RATES.—A number of persons were summoned non-payment of poor rates. Orders were made for pay- ment to be made within fourteen days. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE CASES.—Mr. T. Bowyer, school attendanoe officer prosecuted in the following cases of parents summoned lor neglecting to cause their children to attend school regularly :—William Martin, Gilbert. street ; William Marshall, Gilbert-street ; Peter Stubbs, Stone-street ; Bernard Flaimagan, Loch-street ; Walter Rose, Mill Brow ; Luke Gardner, Heath-road ; Peter Johnson, Gilbert-street ; George Johnson, Penketh-lane, Emma Moores, Mill Brow ; Joseph Faulkner, Granville- street ; Peter 13radbury,Bridgewater-street ; and William Cooke, Surrey-street. Defendants were fined fid. and 4s. 61. costs.—Elizabeth Robinson, Mersey View, was fined a similar amount; Mr. Tomlinson prosecuted. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Mary Lyon was fined ss. and costs for making use of obscene and profane language in Mersey-street. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—EIIen Walker, Merser-st.. and George Halt, Friar-st., were fined for being drunk and disorderly.</p>
        <p>DEFRAUDING THE RAILWAY COMPANY.—WARET Min- shull,Barnton,was summoned to answer the information of John Fenna with having on the 22ad and 30th April travelled on the London and North-Western Railway from Runcorn to Acton Bridge without paying the fare with intent to avoid the payment thereof. Defendant pleaded guilty.—Mr. Fenna' solicitor, wbo prosecuted, stated that the defendant had been committing a syste- matic and deliberate fraud upon the London and North- Western Railway Company. Be had frequently been travelling between Runcorn and Greenbank, and when he got out at this latter station he had informed the ticket collector that he had only travelled from Acton Bridge and then tendered the fare from that place. He (Mr. Penne) saw no redeeming features in the case, but the defendant had expressed his contrition, and he did not press the case so much as he would have cone. The facts of the case were that the defendant was suspe:ted, and a ticket collector from Runcorn followed him in the train and watched his movements. He was seen to get in the train at Runcorn without booking and he was watched. He proceeded to Greenbank where he stated that he had not come from Runcorn, but on being con- fronted by the ticket collector from Runcorn he wanted to pay the full fare.—The defendant said he was sorry for what he bad done ; he did not know what possessed him to do it.—A fine of 10a. and costs in each case was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>SATIIIIDAY.—Before Col. McCorquodale (in the chair) and Messrs. N. McCorquodale, T. J. Gillespie, C. B. F. Borron, R. Stone and Col J L Wood ..... _•</p>
        <p>- . DRUNKENNESS.—WiIIiam Whittle, James Coadliffe, Peter Kenney, John Kerr, John Lowe, Thomas Mutroy, James Rigbey, and Samuel Webster were each fined 2s. fld. and costs for having been drunk and disorderly at divers times and places.—Peter Flynn was fined 53. and costs for a similar offence. CRUELTY TO ANlMALS.—Christopher Gibney, cattle dealer, was charged with having cruelly ill-treated a cow at Newton on the 17th inst. by nrolonging parturition.—lnspector Luckings. R.S.P.C.A., stated that the cow was in a poor condition, and was not in a fit state to ba out. He (witness) had the animal re- moved to a stable, where she calved about half-an-hour afterwards. Inspector Luff, Warrington branch, R.S.P.C.A., corroborated, and stated that the cow was totally unfit to be out. Thomas Cooney, who repre- sented Christopher Gibney, stated that the latter was on his way from Drogheda to Liverpool, but could not arrive at that court in time. He, therefore, ask&amp; for an adjournment, which was not granted. Defendant was fined £5 and costs.—James Berry was charged with having worked a horse whilst in an unfit state on the 15th May at Newton.-I'.C. Dixon stated that defendant travelled with a boxing show. On the day in question he saw the defendant in charge of a horse and Parry. Under the horse's collar was a large raw wound which was bleeding, and on the left buttock was a punctured wound which was about two inches deep. He (witness) took the horse to Mr. Hewitt, veterinary surgeon, who said it was not fit ta work. Police-Sergeant Banks cor- roborated. Defendant said the. horse had been kicked by another animal, and the whole journey it had to travel was only four miles. Defendant was fined 204. and costs. _ .</p>
        <p>A MEAN THEFT.—John Bainbridge was charged with having at Newton, on the 21st May, stolen 3. in cash from the person of John Henry Plant, aged three years, the property of Henry Plant.—Complainant stated that he gave the money to the child to take to the landlord of the house to pay the rent. Elis wife asked prisoner whom she had been keeping for about three months, to go with the child. He did so, but after a while the child returned home without the prisoner, and from what he said she told her husbind, who went in search of prisoner, and found him on a dray in Warrington-road. —Prisoner said he was a sailor and had been starving for 12 weeks, and it was a sudden temptation. He exprecsed sorrow.—Mr. Gillespie said it was a very fool- ' ish thing to send a child three years of age with mon,y, but prisoner would be sent to gaol for 14 days with hard labour. ALLEGM, THEFT FROM A STALL.—Mary Ann Bradley, was charged with having stolen a pinafore, value ls., from a stall belonging to Mr. Arthur Peacock, of 252, Winwick-road, Warrington, on the 17th May, at Earles- town.—Complainant said he noticed the woman near the stall for a considerable time, and when she left he fol- lowed her and saw her putting something under her shawl —Albert Shaw, assistant to Arthur Pencock,eated that he was on the opposite side of the stall to the prisoner. He saw her pick tip the pianafore and walk away with it. Information was then given to Detective Schofield, who brought prisoner back to the stall and took the article from under her shawl. When charged she replied "I bought it and pail the boy (Albert Shaw) 8i for it."—Eliz theth Ball and Jane Holmes stated that they had each scan prisoner give the boy Is., and she (prisoner) received some change. There being a doubt in the case prisoner was discharged. QuannEtsoma NEIGHBIIIRS.—James Unsworth was charged with having assaulting Sarah Shaughnessy on the 20th May, at Golborne.—Complainant stated that she was having a few words with defendant's sister when Unsworth come out of the house and struck her several times on the face, besides kicking her on the lower parr of her body.—Defendant said complainant had thrown bricks at him, and had also broken the windows of the house by throwing a bucket and bricks at it.—Thomas Shaughnessy, son of the complainant stated that James Uns worth kicked his mother twice and struck her in the face Fever d times.—Marjory Uns- worth stated that her brother only pushed complainant and knocked her down.—Defendant said he only knocked bar down and " hit her once in the face be- cause she threw the brick at him." Be did not kick her once.—Defendant was fined to. and costs. lINMCZZLED Doos.—John Lomas and Thonsas Ashton were charged with having allowed their dogs to run at large without muzzles on the 18th May at Newton and the 20th May at Golborne respectively.—Defendants were each fined 3s. 6d. including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>FRODSEIAM PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FRODSEIAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WsDsy.—..BeforeD; Steele and Mr. Thomas Handley. DRUNK ON LICENSED PREMISES.—Joseph Allman, Newton, was charged with being drunk on the licensed premises of Mary Buckerstaffe, Five Crosses, on April 6th. P.C. Breeze stated that on visiting the house on the date named he found three jugs, one of which was half full, and which belonged to the defendant who was drunk. Re ordered him out and the landlord asked the defendant why he came there to get people into trouble. Corroborative evidence was given by P.C. Price, and the defendant was fined 10i. including costs, or in default of payment seven days' imprisonment. FOOTBALL PLAYING IN THE STREET—Frederick Burk- hill, H. Dutton, John Wright, Frederick Miller. Joseph Davies and William Clarke, of Frndsham Bridge, were summoned for playing at football in Chester Main-road,</p>
        <p>Frodsham, to the annoyance of passengers on May 15th. Defendants admitted the offence. P.C. Price stated that he had warned the defendants some time previously as to their conduct.—A fine of 2s. 61, each was imposed. D RUNKENNESS. —R. Garner, Frodsham, was fined ss. for Leing drunk on the licensed premises of the Drovers Arms, Church-street, on May 4th. Sergeant Thomas proved the case.—Daniel Garner, Church-street, Prods- ham was also fined ss. for being drunk and disorderly in Marsh Green on the 12th May. A WOMEN'S QuAisaaL.—EIIZI Oaks, Newton, was summoned for assaultiug Ellen Joynson on the 20th May The assault complained of was that on the date named, the defendant attacked her, tore her dress, pulled out a i quantity of hair and otherwise abused her. For the de- fence it was contended that the complainant commenced the quarrel and that defendant struck her in self defence —Case dismissed. A RUNCORN FISH DEALER IN TROUBLE —Frederick Raymond Chell fish dealer, Runcorn, was summoned for being drunk whilst in charge of a pony and cart in Bridge-lane, Frodsharn, on the 28th May. The defen- dant admitted having had a drop too much. A fine rf 53. and 83. 6d. costs was imposed. OFF TO Gs.cm Aoarg.—Thomas Dolly alias Brown, of Northwich was charged under a warrant with disobed- ience to a summons for feloniously stealing thirteen duck eggs and nine hen eggs valued at 53. 6d., the property of William Sanders of Oakmere on April 25th. Sergeant Johnson said the prisoner had been convicted in differ- ent parts of the country for qarious offences, including highway robbery, and had been sentenced to five years penal servitude. He was committed to gaol for three I months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 1, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 1, 1895.</p>
        <p>WHY?</p>
        <p>Why do I have this drowsy, lifeless feeling ? Why does my back ache; my food cause distress ? Why have I frequent headaches ; and difficulty to secure rest at night ? Why do I lose flesh, or pass fine sand or gravel ? Because your blood is filled with uric acid poison, which must be eradicated before you can regain health. Your liver is too weak—run down. enervated,and torpid —to properly excrete the bile which accumulates and poisons the blood, the system, and the kidneys—these greatest blood-purifying crgans are failing to perform their normal functions. This is" Why !" Warner's Safe Cure quickly restores health. It quickens and invigorates the liver to a healthy action. Tens of thousands have witnessed to this fact. Be wise. Frederick the Great said, "Facts are Divine things. It is only the foolish who doubt facts." Mr. G. Hall, of 15, Carlton-street, West Hartlepool, says, " I suffered from biliousness and liver complaint from childhood, and had tried several kinds of medicines, but they gave me very little relief, until I was persuaded to try Warner's Safe Cure ; and I can truly say that I found relief from the first few doses."</p>
