      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>NEW WARREN(iTON STE/MSHIP COMPANY.</head>
        <p>A NEW WARREN(iTON STE/MSHIP COMPANY.</p>
        <p>LAUNCHING OF A STEAMER.</p>
        <p>For about three years or so a most successful and lucrative business has been carried on by the proprietors of the "Zillah," a steamer which has been engaged in trade between North Wales,Liver- pool, Birkenhead, Widnes, and also along the Ship Canal. The captain has been Mr. William Savage, and a great amount of the success has been due to him and the other men engaged on the steamer. The trade has developed so much that it was de- cided to purchase another steamer, and also to form a company, under the name of the Zillah Ship and Carrying Company, Limited, with a capital of £lO.OOO, consisting of 2,000 shares of £5 each. The subscribers to the memorandum were Captains George and William Savage, Mr. Walter Brough Nlr.J.W.lfayward,J.P.,Councillor H. Rob?rts,J.P.,Mr. I. Biddle, and Councillor Shaw Green. Mr. F. ft. Roberts, M.A., L.L.B.,was appointed the solicitor to the company. The shares were very quickly taken up, and in a very short time the company was formed.</p>
        <p>It was decided by the newly-formed company to purchase a steamer which was being built by Mr. Harland, ship builder, of Tranmere graving dock. This steamer is built of oak, extra strong, and will he fitted with a bridge. She is 97 feet in length, 22 feet 2 inches in breadth, and about 10 feet in hold. She will carry a cargo of from 280 to 300 tons. The engines are being made by• Messrs. Cochrane, Birkenhead,and have 14 and 23-inch cylinders,with 18-inch stroke, and will work at 115 pounds pres- sure. She will be able to go at the rate of from nine to ten knots per hour. She will also be pro- vided with a steam winch for the quick discharge of cargo The captain will be Mr. William Savage, and he will he succeeded on the "Zillah" by Captain Samuel Summerfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LAUNCH.</head>
        <p>THE LAUNCH.</p>
        <p>The steamer is not yet completely equipped for work, but will probably be so in the course of four or five weeks. It was, however, decided to launch her on Saturday. and a large nunaber of persons interested journeyed to Tranmere to witness the event. The morn- ing was fine,and at about twelve o'clock everything was In readiness. It will be interesting to state that the gentleman who " christened " the vessel, was Captain William Summerfield, wha resides and is well-known in Sankey Bridges, and who will in the course of neat month, be eighty years of age. The steamer was named the "Merlin,"and the launch was described by those who witnessed it as being one of the best they ever saw. The I large boat went as smoothly as possible down the planks 'into the water,aud if this is a fore cast to her future,she has a very successful career before her. Loud cheers were raised by the spectators while she was making her first plunge into the water. The steamer was moored to the side.and the party then adjourned to a sumptuous lunch which was provided by Mr. Harland. Among those present were : Mr. and Mrs. Harland, Messrs. Scott and Reece (representing Messrs. Knee:haw, Lupton and Co.), Messrs. Singleton, J. Adams, R. Pevey, Rowlands, and .1. E. Woodin. Mrs. Povey, and Mrs. Gorch (Fran- mere). Mr. and Mrs. J. W. Hayward, Councillor and Mrs. J. Shaw Green. Mr., Mrs. and Miss Brough,Captain and Mrs. G. Savage, Captain and Mrs. W. Savage. Cap- tain W. Summerfield, Mr. and Mrs. 8. Summerfirld,Mr. and Mrs.J.Suminerfield,Mr. and Mrs.W.Winstanley,Miss SumrnPrfield,Mr. Smallwood,Mrs.Green,Mr.F.R.Roberts, M. A., L.L.B. (Warrington),Mr .Mrs. and M isters Biddle (Southport), Mr. G. Travis ( Liverpool), Mr. and Mrs Andrews (St. Helens), Mr. F. J. Poole, Mr. and Mrs. Lock (Widnes). Councillor Roberts was unable to be present owing to other engagements. After the lunch had been done full justice to, Council- lor J. SHAW GHEE!z said that he thought it was the duty of those present to express to Mr. Harland their thanks for the way in which he had provided for them that morning. They had that day launched a ship which he (Mr Green) hoped was going to do a great deal of good. The lunch had been an excellent one, and it was only fair that they should thank Mr. Harland in the heartiest manner possible for thP kind reception and for the good ttinsfs hP had provided for them. (Applause.) Mr. W. BROVGII said that he eiteemed its privilege to second the motion which had been proposed by Mr. Green. That was the second time that he had had the privilege of being there on a similar occasion. He hoped it would not be the last time that Mr. Harland was go- ing to bnild for them. (Applause.) lie was proud of the builder, and Was also proud of the captain, and he was sure that the company would always be proud of the name of Savage. (Applause.) He was glad that he had bectme associated with them in that way. A great deal of the success of the first boat was due to them,and he was cure that Captain Savage would do much toward making the present one a success. He hoped the com- pany would go on and prosper. (Applause.) Mr. Race endorsed all the last speaker had said about Captain Savage. He hoped the Merlin would be equally as succes4ul as the Zillah. He thought she would. No man had been more energetic and indefatigable in the interests of the Zillah than Captain Savage, senior. Mr. RIDDLE said he believed that the present boat was of a very high standard, and that all had been done that was necessary toward making the company a success. They had every confidence in the captains. They would never be afraid in stormy weather of their captains' heads being muddled. They were clear headed men with good sound judgment. (Applause.) They had cause to be proud of the past, and they were very hopeful for the future, and he trusted that their hopes would be realised. (Applause.) Mr. J. W. HAYWARD, also supported the vote of thanks. The company, he said, had till best possible captains, and he hoped that the change would be equally good for the workers. He had got sympathy for the workers of the country. A great deal depended upon the workers, and he was glad that they had been So well looked after in the Zillah. He was sure they would he treated equally well in the future. The motion WAS carried with acclamation, and Mr. HAULAND in response said that he considered he had done no more than his duty in the matter. With reference to , the Merlin, he would only be too proud to hear that she had eclipsed the Zillah. They had now had the Zillah three years, ani she was a good ship, but he was sure that the Merlin would prove as good or even better than the Zillah. He wished every success to the Merlin and I to evervbody connected with her. Mr. POTttY, as a local man in Tranmere, spoke in hig,h terms of Mr. Harland, and said that he had taken &lt;treat interest in the Zillah ever since she was launched and was glad to hear how well she had silted her owners. With regard to Mr. Harland, he was sure that, aa that gentleman was a right down decent fellow, he would give them right down honest work. In conclusion, he said tha he would watch the career of the Merlin with much interest. The party then separated. It had been intended to have an excursien to Bidstnne Hill after lunch, hut owing to a severe thunderstorm, this was dispensed with.</p>
        <p>Scott's Emulsion is not a secret compound. You kry.w what you ere getting when von buy it. It i, tar more, eff-,e tive than Plain Cod-liver Oil. because it is e 51.5,1 on rho stomach and palatable. It gives real strength and fortifies the system agsinst cliseas.. (11.)</p>
      </div>
