      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>OHAIY'S CIRCUS,- WARRINU TON. MR. OHMY'S PERFORMANCE.</head>
        <p>OHAIY'S CIRCUS,- WARRINU TON. MR. OHMY'S PERFORMANCE.</p>
        <p>There has been no lack of variety and enjoyment at the circus during the present week. The performances consisted of the displays of the popular artistes of the previous week, and in addition there was an agile troupe of vaulters, to whom the clearing of six or eight horses was nothing out of the common. Migget, the comic pony, evidently recognized but one master only, that one being Clown Cartland who makes amusing use of his advantages. "The horse of 1,000," Galatea, Is worthy his name, not only being a very handsome animal but showing evidences of skilful training in the manner in which he circles about the ring. A tight-wire performance by the Air-king, a native of Japan proves to be also one of interesting dexterity, the performer's daring feat of mounting a sloping wire to the roof of the building and then sliding rapidly down, being especially applauded. Mons. D mbavand gives a clever exposition of bare-back riding and Miss Lizzie, though very young, is a remarkably grac:ful Scotch, Spanish, and Italian costume dancer. .</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, the six cup winners in the buck. jumping competition of the previous week competed for the championship and a handsonae silver watch. The race, of 18 times rsund the ring was, as usual, very ex- citing, the winner eventually being Mr. John Stockton, 20 Pierpoint-st., who was far ahead of all other rivals, doing the round in 2mlns. lOsecs. After the watch had been handed to him, Mr. Stockton was made the reci- pient of 'another appropria:e present, namely, a bottle of Mr. H. M. Ashton's Drinkstone embrocation. Tuesday evening was the most important of the week as on that night only Mr. Ohmy himself had undertaken to repeat the performance which won him his name, many years ago,under the balloon in Paris. The popular proprietor's appearance in brilliant costume was greeted with loud cheers, after which he mounted to the roof and bestrode a rope swing which stretched acros'i the building. Upon this he spun rapidly, swing by his feet and went through all manner of feats of a daring nature. One specially attractive feature was afforded by Mr. Ohmy placing upon his head,a helmet surmounted with fireworks which he lighted, and as they went off he whirled round and round, sending the sparksflying in all directions. The most daring and sensational of Mr. Olumy's numerous performances was his swinging on the rope and then plunging into space with the cry of 64 oh-my " which was echoed by the startled spectators. On reaching a certain distance however a cord was seen to be fastened to each ancle of the performer and this brought him up with a jerk. Altogether, the proprie- tor's display was the finest which has yet been seen at the circus and the skill for which he was formerly so famous does not seem to have in any degree deteriorated. After receiving a handsome bouquet Mr. Ohmy said that his reason for including that part ot the pro- gramme was that when he came to Warrington a few weeks ago It was reported that he was not the Ohms' that performed at Paris, years ago. It was thought that he had been killed while performing at Blackpool, but he was happy to assure the audience that that was not the case and that he was the only and original Ohmy. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>BURNING FATALITY AT WARRiNGTON.</head>
        <p>BURNING FATALITY AT WARRiNGTON.</p>
        <p>An inquest- WL-WeT(l-;t" the Infirmary on Mon- day, by Mr. S. Brighouse, on the body of William Lash, Factory-lane, who died on Friday night from injuries which he received on the previous night by being severely burned on the head and upper part of the body.—Francis Lash said that her hus- band was a labourer and was 31 years of age. On Thursday night he was carrying his child upstairs to bed, and be had also an oil lamp in his hand. It was probable that the child gave a jump, for her husband fell with the lamp on the stairs,and the up- per part of his clothes caught fire. Witness was able to get the child before the flames reached it, and thus save it. Her husband came rushing downstairs in flames and ran outside, when the neighbours and a volunteer put the flames out, and he was taken to the Infirmary. Witness was present when her husband died on Friday night.—James Massey also gave evidence to seeing deceased run out of the house in flames, and he was one who assisted in putting them out.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death," and wished to draw the atten- tion of the public to the danger of carrying paraffin lamps about the house.—The coroner said that he agreed with the jurymen that that was a very dangerous practice.—lnsoector Spinks said that Mr Hartshorne, to whom the house belonged, where the accident occurred, acted in a very praiseworthy manner on Thursday night, for he rushed into the house at great risk to himself, and saved the chil- dren from being burnt,and it was probable that but for his assistance, the house might have been burnt down.—The coroner said they were all very much obliged to Mr. Hartshorne for the courage he had displayed.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>P.S. A. AT THE Y. M .C.A.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the Lecture Room of the Y.M.C.A. on Sunday afternoon, when the chair at the meeting was taken by Mr.H.Nl.Ash- ton. An admirable address was given by Mr. F. H. Harrison, of Manchester, upon the subject "The Writing on the Soul," by which be meant the build- ing up of character. Mr. W. H. Wallington, in his usual taking style, sang " Christe Salvator." The meeting was also taken part in by Mr. H. Stewart (gen. sec.), and Mr. F. Hickman accommpanied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>CONCERT AT ST. ANN'S CHURCH._</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT ST. ANN'S CHURCH._</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a concert to raise funds for the purchase of materials for the sale of work was held in the schoolroom,Dannett-st., when there was a large attendance, the Rev. J. Davies occupying the chair. The following artistes assisted in the carrying out of the programme :—Mrs. Blake, the Misses Marie Randall, Kent, Rigby, Milne, and Tay- lor; and Messrs. Percy Randall,Brimelow, Woodhall, Sewell, Fred Randall, and Whittaker, all of whom acquitted themselves in an admirable manner, the pianoforte, violin, mandoline, flute and concertina solos being well rendered. The Chairman thanked those who had taken part in the programme, and renaarked that it was a great trial to them to lose the services of Mr. Randall, the organist. (Rear, hear.) The Rev. J. Byrne proposed, and Mr. Frank Taylor seconded, a vote of thanks to those whoriad decorated the schools, and the proceedings were brought to a conclusion by the singing of the National Anthem. THE STRENGTH OF THE WATER MAINS._</p>
        <p>In consequence of the severe comments which were made in various quarters as to the inadequate supply of water at the fire at Messrs. Skelton's, Limited, Horsemarket-street, the Fire Engine Com- mittee decided to test the .mains in the street, and this took place at an early hour on Tuesday morn- ing. The proposed visit was kept as much a secret as possible, and the proceedings were not witnessed by many persons. There was a good attendance of the members of the.FirtEngine Committee and the brigade, and the former are in a position to make a full explanation as to any shortcomings which may have been experienced on the night of the fire. A thorough test was made, the steam fire engine "Captain " being brought into requisition for the purpose. The brigade left the Fire Engine Station at half-past twelve, and it will be seen that the in- vestigation was a severe ene when it is stated that it was nearly three o'clock before it came to a con- clusion. Without going into details it was evident that there is not a sufficient supply of water, in Hersemarket-street at any rate, for an engine run- ning at 1001 b. pressure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WARRINGTON RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>A special meeting -0-1 the members of the War- rington Football Club was held at the Norton Arms Hotel, on Monday night, to elect an assistant secre- tary and to alter the date of the annual meeting. This latter was rendered necessary owing to the football season having been shortened. Councillor Evans occupied the chair. Messrs. S. W. Jeffery and T. Wallington were nominated for the assistant secretaryship, but the former was elected by a good majority. It was decided that the date of the annua meeting should be within 21 days of the close of the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</head>
        <p>CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Cham- ber of Commerce, held on Monday afternoon at the Market Gate Chambers, the Hon. Secretary (Coun- cillor Arthur Bennett) reported that Lord Cross had presented to the House of Lords the petition of the Chamber in favour of the nationalization of the telegraphic system ; and that Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., had presented a similar petition to the House of Commons.—A. memorial in favour of the resolu- tion passed by the recent Ottawa Conference urg- ing the importance of securing a system of inter- postal and telegraphic route was adopted and or- dered to be sent to the Postmaster-General.—Mr. Frederick Monks was appointed to attend the con- ference on railway rates, which is held this week in London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>WARRINGTON TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Teachers' Asso- ciation connected with the National Union of Teachers on Saturday held their annual picnic at Frodsham, to which place about 70 ladies and gen- tlemen were conveyed in Messrs. Maginn Bros'. 'buses. Mersey view was reached about 4.30, and at five o'clock ample justice was done to a good tea provided by Mrs Parker in the large tea room. After tea, the president of the association, Mr. Cann briefly addressed those present, and urged strongly the necessity of all teachers joining the ranks of the National Union. A hearty vote of thanks to the members of the committee who had arranged the picnic was proposed by Mr. Staustield, seconded by Mr. Amos, and carried unanimously and Mr. Edden, hon. secretary, responded.—, Rambles along the hill side, dancing, or games at bowls, &amp;c, occupied the remainder of the evening, until about 8.30, when a start for Warrington was made from the Bear's Paw Hotel, Arpley-st. being reached about 10 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>TER BLUR COAT SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>TER BLUR COAT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A new organ, the necessity for which has been felt for some time, has been placed in the chapel of the above institution. The builders are Messrs Wadsworth Bros., of Manchester, and the organ, which is in every respect an excellent one, consists of two manuals and independent pedal and couplers. The swell organ is enclosed in a box, there are double action feeding bellows, a pollished case, and gilt pipes. The tone of the instrument is very sweet, and its appearance altogether is very handsome. The specification is as follows :—Swell organ : Violo, 81t.; lieblich gedact, Bft.; salicet, Great organ: Open diapason, 81t.; dulciana, Bft.; flute, 41t. Pedal organ: Bourdon, 16ft. Couplers: Swell to great; great to pedals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EXCITING SCENE IN SANKEY-STREET.</head>
        <p>EXCITING SCENE IN SANKEY-STREET.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon a horse and cab belonging to Messrs. Maginn Bros., Sankey-street, were taken to the trill Hall, where the animal was pulled up, the driver having occasion to go inside the hall. Whilst inside the bugle call was sounded for the Volunteers to fall in. This startled the horse, which bolted down the drive through the east gate of the Park and into Sankey-street,where it dashed into the wall of the building opposite, with the result that the animal burst one of its eyes. It then made another rush across the street, but was stopped in its career tefore further damage was done. Fortunately, though there were many people passing at the time, no one was hurt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>DRAWING Room MEETING AT PADGATE.</head>
        <p>DRAWING Room MEETING AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon a drawing room meeting was held at Christ Church Vicarage, Padgate,in aid of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission and the Day School Lectures of the Diocesan C.E.T.S. The Vicar, the Rev. J. J. Langham, presided. The speakers were the Rev. Canon Penrhyn, of. Win- wick, the Rev. It. Postance, vicar of St. George's, Everton, and chairman of the society, the Rev. J. Hirst, and Mr. Henry Taylor, local missionary. The collection realised £3 16s. 6d., and several new sub- scribers were secured. Tea and coffee were after- wards provided.</p>
        <p>MILITARY ITSPECTION AT W AntalloToN. Major-General Sir Baker Russell, 8.C.8., has in- spected at Warrington the depots of the Liverpool and South Lancashire Regiments. The Major- General, who was accompanied by Captain Altbam, D.A.A.G., and Captain Christoph er, R. 8.0., after in- specting the troops on parade, visited the hospital, barrackirooms, cookhouses, and regimental institu- tions. WESLETA.N CHAPEL SCHOOL, APPLETON.</p>
        <p>On sti;cd;iii;i, services in connection with the Sunday school anniversary were held in the above place of worship,when the pulpit was occupied in the afternoon and evening by the Rev. G. D. Mason of Warrington, who delivered two very interesting sermons,which were thoroughly appreciated. Special hymns were rendered by the choir and scholars,who acquitted themselves in a very able manner. There were large congregations.</p>
        <p>BOWLING CLUB PICNIC. On Saturday afternoon about 20 memb lL ers of the Buffalo Bowling Club, held at the Hole ill -i'-th.-Wa Inn, Westleigh, had a pleasant drive to the : Shoe Inn, Croft, and played a main of bowls nn to. green for a number of prizes. Tea was afterwar 3 partaken of, and the remainder of the evening W$ spent in a pleasant manner. THE REV. F. SLATER. The Society for the Propagation 0f the .-I,,amin'st.. has made the following amongst other aPY" ments to continental chaplaincies for this men. :—The Rev. Francis Slater, vicar of Latchford to Menagio. SUDDEN DEATH OF MR. JAKE BONOStII It is with regret that we record the suddl "fl-ci y of Mr. Jake Bounell, which took place 001; :lid evening at his residence in Folly-lane. At toeitli, of the week he was apparently in his usual "'h ever, he hoeutruopntuhriesdbiac brain' gr, seized with an apopletic fit and death enaned;, be ybelloeo do n vFersisdealy 0. n th Latere n , sv° onnell was well-known in the town and r;,(7%iti.s son of the late Mr. John Bonnell H mrs art% 01 G askell, who survives him and h e e was Ye- age. WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS.—CELEBRATICh. -11 0 TO QUEEN'S BIRTHDAY. the The Queen's birthday was celebrated tae Warrington Volunteers, on Saturday, svb,o- ek. battalion paraded at the Drill Hall at faur,"'"ol°oor about 200 members being present, under te:,.B la mend of Colonel Ridgway. The other eratree• attendance were Captain and Ad • utant Silvertr," Captains Buckton, Hutchinson, jF irclough..dter ton, Edelsten, Chorley and Ridgway,a and li'°„"g al ants Fairclough and Fletcher. After tile n-IBPs inspection of arms, &amp;c., in the Dr'll Hall, the eh-fire marched to the recreation ground Drill the Parigiv"ihe a large number of spectators had assembleu,;. battalion was formed in line, and the ranks vber been opened a feu de joie was fired in honour ",:dieg Majesty's birthday, after which the Conltae"?,/0 Officer formed the men in quarter column 011,-, ibe moved to the right in fours, an