      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>k</p>
        <p>THE OPENING OF PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to be in position now to announce that the new concert hall which Warrington has acquired through the munificence of Mr. J. Charlton Parr will be formally opened on Thursday, the 26th September next. The inaugural proceedings will commence at two in the afternoon and will take the form of a grand performance of Handel's stir- ring oratorio " Judas Maccboeus," b 3, the Warring- ton Musical Society in fullest force of rank and file, and with the assistance of solo vocalists of the highest standing. There will be some short formal proceedings on the platform of the hall during which the key of the building will be presented to the Warrington Corporation and duly accepted by them. A grand orchestral and vocal arrangement of the National Anthem from the pen of Sir Michael Costa will then be given by the united musical force as part of the ceremony, the whole forming a fitting and forceful prelude to the oratorio which will immediately follow. In the evening there will be a miscellaneous concert on n., lees a magni- ficent scale in which the 51usical Society's band and chorus and the same principal vocalists are expected to further distinguish them- selves. The following circular has been ad- dressed to the members of the Musical Society by the Hon. Secretary, 51r. W. E. Atkin "The committee has much pleasure in announcing that the Warrington Corporation has invited the Musical Society to inaugurate the opening of the new Concert Hall, which event is now fixed for Thursday, Septembar 26th next. In accordance with the expressed wish of Mr. Parr, the Corpora- tion has further desired that the inauguration ceremony shall comprise two concerts by the Musi- cal Society on the date named, viz. :—An afternoon performance of Handel's oratorio "Judas Macca- bmus," and an evening programme to include 1, cantata "On Shore and Sea" (Sullivan), 2. part song " Moonlight " (Eaton Fanning)," 3, march and chorus "Hail bright abode," from " Tannhauser " (Wagner). It will be seen that in this way the opening of the new Parr Hall is arranged practi- cally to take the form of a grand musical festival and the committee feel that a very great honour is done the Musical Society by Mr. Parr and the War- rington:Corporation in their selection. It is neces- sary, owing to the magnitude of the work entailed, that the Society immediately commence ;rehearsal of the music named, and the first practice will accordingly be held in the Trinity Schools, on Ines- day next, May 28th, at 7.30 p.m. By commencing the practices in good time it is hoped to arrange a short, holiday for all concerned about the end of July or beginning of August, and to resume re- hearsal again a few weeks before the date of the performance. The conductor is particularly anxious to secure a full and efficient chorus, therefore the enlistment of new members is desirable, as also the return to the ranks of as many as possible of mem- bers who may not have attended during the last one or two sessions. The committee is also pleased to be able to announce that arrangements are pend- ing with solo vocalists of the very first rank, and that the Corporation is generously bearing the entire expense of these two concerts."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>CREATURES THAT BUILD.</head>
        <p>CREATURES THAT BUILD.</p>
        <p>The megapodes of New Guinea and Australia are the only birds that use incubators to hatch their eggs. There are now a number of species, but in general they resemble small turkeys with very large feet, and are found in the brush near the shore or beach. When the breeding season arrives both sexes select a suitable place and proceed to build a mound of grass and vegetable matter. Thii is accomplished by the birds seizing the material in their large and powerful claws ana hurling it backward. The work of perhaps a score of birds so accumulates, and a large-sized mound is the result, and which when used year after year often often assumes striking proportions. As an example, some naturalists who were travelling on the Island of Nogo, in Endeavour Straits, were at- tracted by the account of the natives of a bird that made mountains in which to hide its eggs from enemies. By offering to reward the natives the travellers were taken to the mound of a megapode, from which the guides triumphantly dug out several eggs. The mound was, if not a mountain, a small hill and measured 150 ft. in circumference, and at the end was 14ft. in height, sloping in one place 24ft. to the level of the ground, which was scraped bare in the vicinity. Another mound observed was 25ft. in length and 51t. high. The mounds are formed of vegetable matter, in- terspersed here and there with fi no gravel, decayed wood and leaves, and are artificial incubators. The birds dig a hole in the top, the eggs being deposited about six feet from the surface, then covered and left to ' batch by the heat generated in the mass, whereupon the young scramble out and as a rule look out for themselves from birth. The Nicobar megapode constructs a similar mound, while the Telegallus of Australia is equally remarkable as a mound-builder. In this instance several females use the same nest, and as many as a basketful of eggs have been taken from a single mound. This bird is nearly as large as a turkey, and resem- bles it. Among the birds there are a number of mound-builders among which the lyre-bird, so remarkable for its ven- triloquistic powers, may be included, forming a singular mound of sticks and brush. Upon one occasion several naturalists, visited the small islands on the Bahama banks, and came upon a singular settlement of mounds. The latter were about 30in. across, and from 1 to 311 high, and looked like stools or seats rising from the mud. They were the mound-like nests of the flainingo. A little bird found deep in the heart of Borneo, called the gar- dener bird, erects a perfect mound, apparently 3 or 4ft. high, which, wonderful to relate is hollow. The bird is but little larger than a robin, and builds this mound of green twigs simply as a pleasure-house. its eggs being deposited in a nest not far distant. Travellers across the prairies of Illinois have often noticed singular mounds, interspersed here an d there in what appears to be comparatively dry land. So numer- ous are these mounds that they give the surrounding country the appearance as though billows of water had passed over it. These mounds, it will be found on in- spection, have been thrown up by little crawfish, that penetrate the soil in all directions from the neighbour- ing brook. Other curious instances of mound building performed by fishes may be met with on the American rivers. A traveller relates that when rowing on the St. Lawrence he came across a mound of stones from two to three inches in diameter rising to within two feet of the surface. At first glance the mound looked like a cart- load of Stones that had been tipped over from some pass- ing steamer, or, and not the water been shallow, it might well have passed for cinders thrown from a pass- ing vessel. But the stones were all selected pebbles, and of the same general size. This mound proved to be the work of the fishes (Sono- thus). Year after year they toil at the mound, piling up the stvnes and bringing them in their moo the, in many instances from not a little distance. This mound and others of the type were often over a ton in weight, and in shape that resembled caves. The fishes evidently dropped th e stones from above so that they rolled down forming the shape desired. In succeeding seasons they had built the mounds to the surface of the water, or so near it, that during the winter months the stones were frozen in the ice, and in the spring when the ice melted many of the top-layer were carried away. The mounds were the nests of the fishes, many of them depositing their eggs in them, which were washed in the interstices of the muund, where the young, when they appeared, found shelter. Equally remarkable are the mould-like structures of lamprey eels. One observed in the Saco River was fifteen feet in length and three feet in height. It was formed of stones, but of larger size than in the case of the St. Lawrence River mounds. This accumulation was for a similar purpose, and in contemplating it one wonders how the builders could accomplish so much. In removing the stones, the eels attached their sucker- like mouths to them, and rising with a wriggling motion from the bottom allowed the current to carry them along as far as it would before they dropped ; then the upward wriggling motion was repeated, until finally the stone was placed where desired. Among the material carried down stream in this manner was a portion of a brick,and took the united efforts of two large eels,which held themselves upright in the water as they were carried on by the current. These eels also attack and adhere to fishes, eating their way into the interior of the body. The species, about- twenty in numberware mostly inhabitants or temperate regions of the northern and southern hemispheres. The wood rats of southern California and Florida are , also mound-builders. Their retreats are constructed of</p>
        <p>. . brush wood piled up about the trunks of trees to the height of three feet or so, and so interwoven and laced as to render it difficult to pull them apart, the short twigs being in some way wound in and out so closely as to render the result not only an impregnable fOrtress, but also secure from the weather. The inside of the nest is no less curious and serves the double purpose of convenience and safety. The upper portion contains a room or apartment lined with fine moss and the bark of trees, and thoroughly protected from the rain. Other apartments are stuffed with seeds, and pieces of cloth picked up in the vicinity of a neighbouring ranch-house. , The whole whole mass is honeycombed with tunnels, so that it is impossible to catch one of the inmates napping. The passages lead into the ground and radiate in vari- ous directions, and thus pruvide for their escape.</p>
      </div>
