      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>tetterss to tin &amp;Oar.</head>
        <p>tetterss to tin &amp;Oar.</p>
        <p>bIBEL UNIONISTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l maintain the name assumed by the so-called Liberal Unionists is one they do not deserve. When they formed a union with the Tory party they should have been called Tory Unionists, fc.r I consider they are quite unworthy of the name of Liberal after supporting the Tories for the last eight or nine years. If any party should be called Liberal Unionists I consider the Glad- stonian Liberals ought to be known by that name, for they have always avowed their desire to maintain the union between England and Ireland. Mr. Chamberlain and his party I suppose wanted men to think them better than other Liberals when they call themselves Liberal Unionists. I tell you what I think of them—l think they are the political Pharisees of these times.- -Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>PEACE-AND CHRISTIAN HUMBUG.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>R. M. B.</head>
        <p>R. M. B.</p>
        <p>Sir,—While being quite at one with "Peace at any price " in his denunciation of the transparent humbug of a nation like England calling itself " Christian " and still maintaining a most expensive war equipment—not as he very rightly puts it—for purely defensive pur- poses. Now if Christianity means "peace at any price," absolute non-resistence,then England, Christian England, is one of the most shameless humbugs upon the face of the world. But dees the reported dicta of the Jewish peasant, who said he came not to bring peace but a sword and whose avowed mission was to set friends and relatives by the ears, does this lead us to the belief that "Christianity "inculcates absolute national non-resist- ance to aggression ? Let the candid engineer into into these things consult Matt. 1034 and following verses,and ask himself if they are of a peaceful nature ? 'Then why was the man told to sell his garment and "buy a sword" ? Was the sword to be a plaything, a " Quacker " ? All this shows the utter futility of appeal- ing to the book of contradictions to " prove ' anything for your adversary can easily produce a " scripture" equally opposite on the other side. If the New Testa- ment advocates peace at any price it is flatly contra- dicted by the Old Testament, which is full of incentives to the most barbarous wars. From this view why should not "priests preach peace and bless war from the same pulpit" ? Why should not the book of contradic- tions have contradictory ministrations ? Your correspondent " A Christian Endeavourer," must be very young, a suckling who is still at the teat that gives the" milk for babes" of his sort. In consideration then of his yeuth and inex- perience let us " endeavour "to bring things down to the level of his very juvenile comprehension. Our Endeavourer " delivers himself thusly : " tie says that if England adopted the doctrine of non-resistance to aggression the Society of Friends would quickly lose the platform from which they preach this peculiar doc- trine. Now, in making an assertion of this kind" Pro aria et focis " shows that he does not know what is go- ing on in the world." Possibly. But I know what would be going on in the world if this " cranky "doctrine was adopted ! Not only would the Society of Friends quickly lose their platform—and we our England and our liber- ties—but a " Christian Endeavour" might as quickly become personally acquainted with the in- terior economy of the Mines of Siberia or the mildly corrective influence of the galleys. Let me advise " Endeavourer " to cease reading, think- ing, and talking humbug of this kind. Does not this non-conbatant gentlemen look un his spoons arid bar his doors against aggression at night? Does he protest against paying rates to hire men to resist such a ag,gres- skin ? No !he is wiser than his own profession. And so it is with the English people. They profess Chris- tianity but are not yet sufficiently unbued with its peculiar doctrine as to leave their spoons unlocked or their doors unbarred and unguarded. Indeed your correspondent is young and has net yet corns to real- ise that all the best part of Christianity is borrowed ; its impractical dogmas alone being its only distinctive feature. I, however, find in them nothing to induce me to be spoony enough to expose my spoons or to leave open my doors to the aggressor.—Trulv yours, PRO ARIS ET FOCIS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PEACE AT ANY PRICE.</head>
        <p>PEACE AT ANY PRICE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—After a little needed breathing time " A Reader" has returded to do battle with ttle on the war qusstion ; but his gun is so antiquated and harmless that it has not affected me at all. Still, I will oive him an exchange Shot or two lest by my refraining, to do so he might chuckle under the delusion that he had demolished me altogether. He asks what I mean by peace at any price ? My answer is, the passive suffering of the loss of friends, property, and even life itself .when called upon t 3 do so for the sake of Christ. This doctrine, lam well aware, does not find much acceptance with our modern nasters and their admiring people ; but it is neverthelrss the doctrine of Christ, and the man who has no exneri- mental knowledge of it has yet to learn what Chris- tianity is. Your correspondent appears to have executed a re- markable tack since he first addressed you—nothing IPR9 than a tremendous jump from the Armenian atree.ities to the advocacy of war for trade purposes ! This policy might possibly be advantageous to Christian army con- tractors and the like who try to it noon two stool;,that is to serve God and mammon at the same time, but the results would be terrible to the combatants. For the sake of poor suffering humanity let me respectfully sug- gest a surer and bloodless way of reviving our languish- ing trade. If our friend could only get the ear of the preachers and their devoted congregations all over the country and persuade them to destroy the liquor traffic (they could do it at the polls) he would confer an incal- culable benefit on his nation and the world at large. The hundred and fifty million; a year which that nefarious trade eharees for debauching: and impovishing the people of this country would then filo, into legitimate trade channels. With drunkenness removed from them the inhabitants would he better housed, fed and clothed ; peace and order would reign in our homes and in our streets ; prisons and workhous,s would he depopulated ; and a ar,her end intelligent people would no longer make sport and pay for political Philistines. _</p>
        <p>.... That your correspondent is a well meaning man is evident from the advice he offers me. He says—" If lenr friend ia weak let him find consolation in the Bible ; if he is strong let him find encouragement from the same source." Permit me to thank him for these pious counsels, being a very human creature I need them much—and to suggest, in the same friendly spirit, that he would con- , suit the same book for information that will have some bearing on the subjects he discusses. Bi quotation from Jeremiah is so out of place in this controversy that fancy some other matter must have been exercising his brain when he penned the words. And now, Mr. Editor, let me hid farewell to your gentle and unsophosticated contributor " Reader.' His corresponelence shows that I have nothing to learn from him except the usual devices that are retorted to to justify the slaying of our fellow-creatures. I part with him in peace, and venture to hope. that he may soon be converted from the fighting to the peace policy as the only one consistent with Christianity. And when the happy; change takes place he will be anxious to strengthen his brethren.—Yenra truly. PEACE AT ANY PRICE.</p>
        <p>TOMMY BURNS' LATFST ENTFRFRISM. The feat which Tommy Burns proposed to under- take—that of pushing a wheelbarrow l(Y) milee— bas so far been successfully accomplished. The money he has collected on the way is for the benefit of Mrs. Miller. wife of the man condemned for th Redcross-street murder. Burns started from Liver- pool on Wednesday at two o'clock, walkinq throne Old Swan, Knotty AQh, Prescot, St. TI elenP, Parr. Wigan, Bolton. Bury, Rochdale and Heywood, and he returned vid Eccles and Pendleton, errivinq at Warrington on Thursday morning at 7.55, wlien he put up at the Crown and Sceptre inn,Buttermarket- street, where he stayed fifteen minutes lie then proceeded on his way to Liverpool. Burns was dressed in a white " sweater," and looked exceed- ingly fresh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>_ At the Town Hall, Newton, on P:lonclav morning, before Colonel Wood, Margaret McLoughlin, of no fixed abode, was charged with being drunk in Queen-street. Earlestosvn, on Sunday night, and was fined 2s. 6d. and costs; in default of payment she was sent to gaol for 14 days. P.C. Schofield proved the case. VIADUCT INSTITUTE BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The first handicap of the season in connection with the above Was held on the .club green on Saturday afternoon. A heavy downpour of rain during the progress of the handicap greatly inter- fered with play, rendering the ground very heavy. There were 35 entries, and the various mains were well contested. The first prize,a copper kettle,was won by A. Gee; 2nd, F. Davies; 3rd, It Green, each half-a-dozen knives and forks. WIGAN AND NEWTON BRANCHES OF THE TYPOGRAPHICAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The members of the above branches, to the number ot 50, visitsd the lovely village of Croft, on Saturday afternoon, making the General Elliot Hotel their rendezvous. Mr. Henry Walker, the proprietor, had prepared a splendid knife-and-fork tea, and when 5.30 came all were glad to regale the inner man. Tea over, as previously arranged, a bowling contest took place between the Wigan and Newton-le-Willows contingents, and after 16 games had been played it was found that Newton were the victors by 15 points. At eight o'clock the party assembled in the large pavilion,and a convivial time was spent. Mr. C. K. Robinson (Wigan) occupied the chair, and Mr. W. Devereux (Newton) the vice- chair. "Auld Lang Syne " was sung at 9.30, thus bringing to a close a most enjoyable gathering. Before separating,the Chairman tendered to the host the hearty thanks of the party for the excellent provision he had made for their enjoyment. GOOD TEMPLADY AT EARLEBTOWN.</p>
        <p>The weakly session of the Hope of Eau lestown lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall, on Monday evening. There was a good attendance of members, Bro. W. Wilson, C. T , pre- siding. A report of the quarterly session of the District Lodge was given by Bro. G. Clarke and adopted. The item of the evening was" Impromptu speeches" for which Bro. E. Newman L.D , was re- ponsible. A number of interesting subjects were ' discussed the following members taking part: Bros. Holliday, Clarke, Barker, Willis, Blair, Halton, Hodnett, Sisters Willis and Whitley.—The usual weekly session of the Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge was held in the Co-operative Hall on Tuesday even- ing and was presided over by Bro W. J. Taylor, C.T. there being a good attendance ot members and friends. The programme of the evening was " Sur- prise Night" Bro. H. Brown being responsible and the following taking part: Sisters Mrs. Brown, Mrs. Taylor, Mrs. Foster, Mrs. Singleton, Mrs. Witty, Miss Hatton, Miss Pritchard,Miss Hayden and Bros. , W. J. Taylor, H. Brown, J. Taylor, H. Foster, T. Dean, W. Wharton,-George Wharton, A. Morris, and T. Bastien. A vote of thanks was tendered to Bro. Brown for his excellent programme which was amusing and also instructive. STRANGE DEATH OF A HAYDOCKDOLLIER.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inqnest at the Wagon and Horses Hotel, Haydock, on the body of Henry Maudesley, 60 years of age, pony tenter in the Havana Colliery of Messrs. Richard Evans and Co. Mr. John Robin- son, mining engineer, and Mr. Seddon, certificated manager, were present on behalf of the firm,and Mr J. Johnson, local secretary of the Miners' Permanent Relief Society, also attended.—On the 9th of April Maudesley was exercising one of the ponies, when the animal suddenly plunged and knocked him down. II 3 received a contused wound on the left leg, and was removed to the Cottage Hospital,where he remained until the 9th inst. On that date he left by his own desire. On the 14th inst. he was suddenly taken ill, and Dr. Hayward, who was im- mediately summoned, found him in a state of apo- plexy, and speechless and unconscious. He died on Saturday last. A post-mortem examination was made by Dr. Hayward, assisted by Dr. Rossiter,and Dr. Dowling also attended on behalf of the rela- tives of the deceased.—Dr. Hayward said there was no evidence to directly connect the injury with the death, though it was posEible the injury might tend in some degree to accelerate death in the case of a broken down constitution like that of Maudesley. —The jury expressed the opinion that death was due to apoplexy, accelerated by injury,and returned j a verdict of "Accidental death.'</p>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL SUNDAY SCHOOL NEWTON. The annual services in aid of the above school, were held in the Cnurch on Sunday. The weather was most propitious and consequently there were large congregations, a very respectable contingent coming trom Earlestown. The preacher at the morning and evening services was the Rev. E. Herber Evans, PD., principal of Bangor College, whose homely discourses,delivered in a very simple style, kept the congregation wrapt in interest. His texts were appropriately selected, and the exposi- tion was brimful of advice in the tutoring and management of children in the ways of Christianity. The choir was under the leadership of Mr. W. Thompson, and in an efficient manner sang at the morning service the anthem" Make a joyful noise," and at the evening service " Lift up your heads," Mr. G, Kidd sang the solo "Thou didst not leave." In the afternoon a musical service was given by the organist, Mr. W. 11. Thompson and choir, be- fore a very large audience. The programme was a nicely selected one and was well rendered, the items including anthem ''Ye shall go nut with joy." solo The Unseen Master" Mr. C. Pritchard. organ solo " Barcarole from fourth Concerto," Mr. W. H. Thompson, anthem " I waited for the Lord," solo "There is a green hill " Mr. W. Turner, quintet "By the dark Euphrates," chorus "0 sing unto the Lord," organ solo " The Lost Chord," Mr. W. H. Thompson, song "The Miss Wood, chorus “ The Heavens are telling." The collections during the day, in aid of the school funds amounted to £32 10s. THE INDEPENDENT LABOUR PARTY, EARLESTOWN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>BRANCH.</head>
        <p>BRANCH.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the above a lecture was delivered in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Monday evening, by Mr. Leonard Hall, a prominent member of the 1.L.P., Mr. S. Schofield presided, and there was a fair audience. The subject of the lec- ture was "The Liberal-cum-Tory reccrd." The lecturer said before they could introduce tie groat principle of truth, all superstitions would have to be cleared away, and amongst those superstitions were the Liberal and Tory parties. They said that true patriotism and sincere interest in the welfare of the country demanded complete political revolu- tion. Re argued that neither the Tory nor Liberal parties were friends of the poor. After quoting many speeches dealing with the Tory faction he condemned the action of the liouse of Lords in pro- posing the contracting out clause in the Employer's Liability Bill. He held that ever since the bill was thrown out, hundreds of deaths had occurred wh'cli would have been prevented had it been passed. Dealing with the Liberal party, he referred to the House of Commons as a place of snobberY', jobbery and robbery. In conclusion he drew attention to the unemployed question. Mr. Balfour said there was no work for the unemployed, and the Liberals had ordered a Committee of enquiry, and after compiling evidence which completely proved the cause of the 1.L.P., but in their opinion they had gained nothing and would go on enquiring. - THE LOCAL VETO BILL.—MEETING AT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the Earlestown Committee of the United Kingdom Alliance, an open air meet- ing was held on the Market Ground, Earlestown, on Wednesday evening, The Rev. F. E. Nliller pre- sided, and he was supported by Mr. Councillor Postlethwaite of Pendleburv, and Mr. B. Boreland of Liverpool (district superintendent of the U.K.A.) The Chairman said they had met there in support of the Local Veto hill, which was intended to put into the hands of the people, the power which was at present held by a few. It was in accordance with the whole drift of modern legislation, giving power to the people to do what before had been I done for them by others. The justices of the peace 1 for the most part, instead of reotricting the traffic bai encouraged it. Instead of considering the wants of the people of the district in granting licenses they had considered the interests of the trade, and licenses had been granted without any consultation of the wishes of the people, and again . . .</p>
        <p>and again, apparently opposed to their desires. From all kinds of quarters they had testimony to the fact that the traffic in intoxicating liquors was the one great curse ot this country. (Hear, hear.) Mr. W. Pearson moved "That this meeting records its thanks to Her Majesty's Government for intro- ducing into the House of Commons the Intoxicat- ing Liquor Traffic Local Control Bill, a measure the well-being of the country, and de calculated,epl y a affecting citfpgut in operation, to secure a great diminution of the evils of intemperance, and this meeting urges Her Majesty's Government to put forth its full power to passing this bill into law." Mr. E. Boreland seconded, end Mr. Postlethwaite spoke in support of the resoluti-m, which was un- animously passed.</p>
        <p>THR LIBERAL CAMPAIGN IN THE NEWTON DIVISION.</p>
        <p>It will be seen by an advertisement in anotler column that Mr. James Moomthe Liberal candidate- elect for the Newton Division,will address a public meeting in the Town Hall, Earlestowmon Wednes- day evening, J`une 12th. In addition to Mr. Moon the meeting will be addressed by Mr. H. W. Perks, M.P., and other gentlemen. WHITSUNTIDE AT EARLESTOW N.</p>
        <p>All the works in the district will close on Satur- day at noon and re-open on Tuesday morning, with the exception of the Vulcan Foundry, which will remain closed until Wednesday morning. All the tradesmen in the town will close their establish- ments on Whit-Monday, on which day the ;various Sunday schools excepting the Church of England will hold their annual demonstration and treats. It is to be hoped that for the sake of the children, the weather will be fine. BURTONWOOD MEN IN TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Town Hall, on Monday, before Alderman T. Cook (chairman) Messrs. J. Burchall, T. Glover, B. A. Dromgoole, and W. L. Pilkington, Robert Yates, Landlord of the Salisbury Hotel and Music Hall, was summoned for having his premises open during prohibited hours; and Thomas Bellfield (12, Mercer-st., Burtonwood), Harry Reyiklds (Bold), Joseph Houghton (Chapel House, Burton- wood), Francis Martin (Mercer-at., Burtonwood), arid Benjamin England (Fairclough-st., Burton- wood), were charged with being on licensed pre- mises during prohibited hours. The Town Clerk (Mr. W. J. Jeeves) appeared to prosecute, and Mr. 'if L. Riley represented be defendants. After Mr. Riley had called the landlord,Thomas Bellfield and Harry Reynolds, the Chairman said the Bench did not request any further evidence. They had de- cided to dismiss the case, though he thought the police were justified in bringing it before them. The case against the five men was withdrawn. BIIRTONWOOD SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The usial monthly meeting of the above was held in the classroom of the Board Schools, Burtonwood, on Tuesday,when there were present Messrs. James Warburton (chairman), William Fairclough, Thomas .Norcott, William Webster (clerk), 11. Gawthorpe (schoolmaster). Letters of apology for absence were read from Mr. A. Jackson and the Rev .A. M. Mit- chell, the latter not being sufficiently recovered from his serious illness to attend. The minutes of the last meeting were read and confirmed and the correspondence considered. Notice of the drawing examination on the 21st June was read. Mr. Gaw- thorpe reported that he had received 3s. for damage to the windows by stone throwing and such sum was ordered to be debited in the cash book. The treasurer's book was produced and examined and a cheque for £ll 163. Bd. for current salaries signed The Attendance Officer's report was read from which it appeared three parents had been summoned be- fore the magistrates at Warrington for the irregu- lar attendance of their children and a fine of 53. had been inflicted in each. The monthly attend- ance returns were as follows : Board school (mixed), on register 142, average attendance, 123, or 86.6 per cent. ; infants on register 57, average 52, or 91 per cent. ; St.Patirs Roman Catholic school (mixed), on register 97. average 84 or 866 per cent, ; infants on registers.B6, average 79 or 91.8.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Tl-I E IX.MINR, vrull,DAY , JUNE 1 1895</head>
        <p>Tl-I E IX.MINR, vrull,DAY , JUNE 1 1895</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR.)</p>
        <p>The hot weather has played havoc with the fields and gardens round these parts. Vegetation and verdure presents a parched appearance, and during the week a downfall of rain would have been most welcome. On Thursday the hearts of the farmers was gladdened by a thunderstorm accom- panied by a shower of rain, wtich though heavy while it lasted, did not penetrate the earth to any great depth, and within a very short time the sur- face appeared as dry as before. Still we should be thankful for small mercies, and if the appearance of the heavens goes for anything we may expect another downfall ere long.</p>
        <p>The dry weather has not only affected the fields and gardens but has had a detrimental effect on the sewers. In his report a short time back, the Medi- cal Officer of Health recommended the regular flushing of the sewers as a preventative against disease, but whether this recommendation has been acted upon or not,l cannot say. Judging, however, from the smells which hare greeted us as we pass over any of the manholes in the district, I am in- dined to think that the flushing of the sewers has been somewhat neglected. During the hot dry I weather it is most important that the sewers should receive every attention, and by this means remove to some extent one of the dangerous grounds of disease.</p>
        <p>The temperance question at the presen t time, if one might be allowed to form an opinion: from the proportions of the gathering on the Market-place the other night, does not occupy first place in the minds of the public of Earlestown. The meeting was announced as a demonstration in support of the Local Veto Bill, but about the advertised time for commenthig, there was not more than half-a- dozen people present. The numbers, however, in- creased,and as the meeting progressed,the " crowd" swelled to perhaps a couple of hundreds. Some forcible arguments were used by the speakers in favour of the bill, and if the workingman could not see that the Act if passed, would be to his advan- tage he must have been blind indeed. A resolution in support of the measure was moved, and carried, and will be forwarded to the proper quarter.</p>
        <p>In case a general election should be brought about before the passage of the Act, the temperance party will he saved the necessity of ascertaining from Mr. Legh, the member for the division, his views on this particular subject, as, in the course of the meeting, it was brought out by one speaker that Mr. Legh was distinctly opposed to it. This forms a remarkably efficient weapon in the hands of the Liberal candidate, as he can put before the constituency ira plain language the fact that Mr. Legh is no friend of the working man, for he re- fuses to be a party to putting into his hands the right of saying whether or not he requires a public.- house. But Mr. Legh never was democratic, and as such we cannot expect him to befriend the working classes. Thus the working classes should study carefully what their action should be at the ballot boxes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT, Continued from page 3. . THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT, Continued from page 3. . THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday evening, on the motion for the second reading of the Westminster (Parliament-street, Szo.) Improvement Bill. Mr. FL GLADSTONE moved the rejection of the bill, stating that the Government intended to bring forward a proposal to take the greater part of the area in question for the pur- pose of adding to the accommodation for Government buiidiruzs. Sir K. WEBSTER protested that if the bill wai, rejected the promoters of the schemes which it authorised would lose all their money. Mr. H. GLAD- STONE Said there had been no breach of faith on the part on the Government. Sir W. flApconaT said the site would give a splendid approach to Westminster Abbey and the Hs‘uses of Parliament. Mr. H. GLADSTONE'S motion for the rejection of the bill was carried by 200 votes to 78. Mr. 11. GARDNER replying to Mr. GRAPLIN, said there had been a large increase of rabies in the present year—practically con- fined, however, to Cheshire. Lancashire, and the W,-st Riding.—but with the precautionary measures which were being adopted there should be no difficulty in bring about an early and substantial improvement. In the course ot nu answer to a question about gold mining in North Wales, Sir W. FIARCOODT said he had within the last few days been taking a groat deal of pains to investigate this matter,with the desire to do all that a Government could do properly to encourage the gold mining industry of Wales, wisicla seemed to have taken a start it had not had before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>BIT OF HISTORY.</head>
        <p>A BIT OF HISTORY.</p>
        <p>Charles the Seeond was altogether in favour of extern- nore preaching. On one occasion he asked the famou- S-illingfieet how it was that he always read his si.rmon, before him, when he was informed that he always preached without a book elsewhere. Stillingfiert ans- wered something about the awe of so noble a congrega- tion, the presence of so great and wise a prince, with which the King himself was very well contented. " But pray," continued Stillingfieet, " will your majesty give me leave to ask you a question ? Why do you read your speeches when you can have nonoof the same reasons ?" 6' Why, truly, Doctor," replied the King, " your ques- tion is a very pertinent one, and so will hn my answer. I have a-ked the two Houses so often and for so much money that lam ashamed to look them in the face."</p>
        <p>WRINGING NIACRINRS. GARLAND, Ironmonrr Warrington. SEWING MACHINES, Hand £2 18s. Rd. Treqdle £4 4s. with handsom“ wilnut cover.—J. and W. Durrorl, 16 and 18, San'e'-t.. Warrington —Advt. CYCLES.—Oslttasin,Sole Agent, Bridge-at., Warring- ton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>OUR LOCA.L. CIIURCILES-WARRINGTON,</head>
        <p>OUR LOCA.L. CIIURCILES-WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The ordinary meeting of the above last Sunday was presided over by Mr. Kenworthy who gave a brief ad- dress upon " Home Life." Unfortunately the speaker, Mr. S. Heath, of Manchester, had chosen the same sub- ject for his address which necessitated the congregation listening to two dissertations upon the same theme. : Miss Pemberton sang " Wintry Echoes " with good effect, and the service was also assisted in by Mr. G. Wallington and Mr. Unsworth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The Sunday school anniversary in connection with Cairo-street Chapel was observed on Sunday, when the preacher, morning and evening, was the Rev. J. E. Manning, M.A., of Sheffield. In the morning the rev. gentleman's sermon was of special helpfulness to Sunday school teacht.r, the text being " Behold a sower went forth to sow." Matt.xiii. 2. The preacher ably deline- ated the various characters of the children with whom Sunday school teachers had to deal. Some were, he said, quick of perception, others slow, but it was not always the ready learner who afforded the best results. Various modes of procedure were pointed out, and the preacher observed that practically each character re- quired a different treatment. The evening service was I also an excellent one, and on each occasion the choir, under the superintendence of Mr. J. M. Muir, A.R.C.0., rendered special anthems, that a the morning being one of Sir Arthur Sullivan's, and in the evening one of Dr, Bile's. In the afternoon Mr. Joseph Coventry, of Liverpool, delivered an interesting address to the chil- dren on " The Importance of Little Things. Each ser- vice was well attended, and the collections were above the average, thus giving general satisfaction. BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN ;:d'fIURCEI.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services in connection with Bold- street Wesleyan Sunday School were held on Sunday, when the preacher in the morning and evening was the Rev. John Bond of London. who preached two eloquent sermons to very large congregations, the evening service being particularly well attended. In the afternoon a meeting wasalso held, the chair being taken by Mr. R. G. Ilenshall, and a very interesting and instructive ad- dress was given to the childreß bv Councillor J. Shaw Green, At the meeting, the Bold-street church band _ _ .</p>
        <p>under the conductorship of Mr. Pendlebury gave several selections. During the day, the choir and children sang special hymns, Mr. Pigot being the conductor. and Mr. P.:Flarrison officiating at the organ. At the morning services, the anthem, " Great is the Lord," (Bruce Steane), was given by the choir, and the solo was well taken by Miss Jeans. In the evening, the anthems, " Will sing of Thy power (Sir Arthur Sullivan), and" The Lord is my Shepherd "(Alfred R. Gaul), were efficiently rendered by the choir, and in the former,the solo portion was taken by Miss Gunton, and the duet in the latter was taken by Miss Gunton and Miss Rawling. A sole was also tastefully sung by Miss M. Collins. Collections in aid of the Sunday School were taken at each service and about £4O were realized. A lecture,which we report elsewhere was given on Monday evening in connection with the anniversary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>MR. SAM WOODS, M.P., AT HAYDOCK.</head>
        <p>MR. SAM WOODS, M.P., AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>TFIE TRADE DEPRESSION.-REFORM OR REVOLUTION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the miners of Haydock and district to consider grievances at the local collieries of Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., was held on the ground of the Haydock Football Club on Saturday afternoon. There was a good attendance,and Mr. J. Lawrenson, jun.,who presided, was supported by Mr. S. Woods, M.P., Messrs. James Lawrenson, sen., John Turner, and others. The Chairman said the meeting had been called to stimulate and help each other in the consideration of the ii.st of grievances under which they at present suffered. Little notice seemed to be taken by the firm of the men's complaints, and it was now high time that the men themselves took some course to bring about a different state of affairs. (Hear, hear.) Though trade was de- pressed he did not think the employers were justified in forcing upon the men unfair and unreasonable conditions of labour which meant rediactions in earnings. Mr. John rurner afterwards detailed certain griev- ances in regard to the conditions under which the miners worked at the titolborne, Princess, and Legh and Wood cits,and in conclusion moved the following resolu- tion :—" That this meeting of Haydock miners deeply regrets that there should be so much dissatisfaction at these collieries in consequence of the constant interfer- ence with the field prices' and the rel'usal to pay for proper • extras' in difficult places, with the result that the miners in this district are net getting anything like sufficient wages to keep their families. This feeling of dissatisfaction is so strong that unless something is done to remedy this state of things immediately the Miners' Federation will be compelled to adopt such extreme measures to defend the men as, in their judgment, may be necessary to secure for them their just rights." Mr. James Lawrenson, sen., seconded. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Woods, M.P., w'hyo wasreceived with applause, supported the resolution. He regretted that not only the flaydock collieries but other collieries and other branches of industries all over the country were suffer- ing under such a depression as they were at present ex- periencing. In regard to the Haydock collieries he never knew them to be in such a condition as they were at present ; a condition which was largely due, be thought, to the formation of the firm of owners into a limited liability company a year or two ago. The men complained of many grievances,and all they wanted was fair protection, fair treatment and fair wages. The depression in trade was general all over the world, and in France, Belgium, Germany, America, and Australia trade was quite as bad, time was quite as short, and 1 wages were equally low as they were in this country.He never admitted that the present state of affairs was due to bad trade, for in the coal trade of last year, notwith- standing the strike of 150,000 Scottish miners, two and a half millions of tons of coal were produced in this coun- try more than the previous year. Tbe evil, in his judg- ment, arose through laws whieh they had not yet been able to alter, but which were the basis of all the depression, short time, and low wages in this country. The state of affairs was due to the land laws under which farmers and their labourers were being- driven into their towns and centres of industry, where they were gradually swamping the local trade with their extra labour. The landowners of the country had hitherto 'thwarted the attempts of the reformers to bring about a ,!esirable change, but such a change would soon be effected. There were between twenty and thirty millicn acres of uncioltivated land in this country, and if men were set to work upon it there would be fewer complaints about had trade and about depression in industries. One million men were at pre- sent ceit of employment, and another million were on the veige of starvation, and he could not for the life of him believe that people would for any length of time continue to live under such desperate circumstances when they saw the millions of starving people and while n signs of a remedy were to be observed. The remedy was in the hands of the people, who should never return as their parliamentary re;firesentacives men con- nected with the landed interest, but those pledged to a rigid system of land reform. (Applause.) 1 Ile thought that the workingclass and the middle class dh ewi int tho ltehrea bg lree astteastte fortitudeof thngasnsdo pl oantgi e tnici ae tpbuyt Ipuepowpliethhat reasonable means or by sheer force a remedy would have to be brought about. As the Bishop of Manchester re- marked a year or two ago they must in social matters have either radical reforms or a revolution. He (Mr. /Woods) wished for the former, but if they were not effected during the next few years they would have a state of affairs in this country that they had never vet dreamed of. In concluding Mr. Woods urged upon the men the importance of a„,thorough organisation of their forces in order to more effectually deal with the griev- ances under which they at present suffered. The resolution was unanimously passed. 1 Mr. Woods afterwards briefly referred to the evils of over-production, and on his suggesstion, the following motion was unanimously adopted :—" That this meet- ing of Baydock miners hereby condemns work by any 1 man on play (lays' when the collieries are understood to be idle, believing it to be injurious to the best Inter- , eats of the workmen in general."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>STRA.NUE STORY OF A WHEAT STACK.</head>
        <p>STRA.NUE STORY OF A WHEAT STACK.</p>
        <p>- A sample of newly-threshed wheat which had been in stark nearly forty years was shown at Bridlington Quay on Tuesday, its history being somewhat remarkable. Two bachelor brothers, who were farmers in the neigh- bourhood of Harrogate, had a stack of wheat each, the proceeds of the harvest reaped the year before the close of the Crimean war. They had 975. per quarter offered for it, which one of them accepted, but the other said he would keep it till he got £5. Peace being declared the following year, the price of wheat fell,and has never since risen so high ; but the farmer kept to his word, and refused to interfere with the stack. A younger rela- tion recently came into possession of the farm, and on Monday last he had the. stack threshed. It had entirely escaped the ravages of rats or mice (being built on a standard a little from the ground). but had provided a home for thou9ands of spiders. The yield was about eighteen quarters, but in quality it was only fit for chicken corn.</p>
        <p>It is estimated that three thousand marriages are daily performed throughout the world. A number of labourers took refuge by a wheat stack at l'irton, near Flitchin, during the severe thunderstorm on Thursday. The stack was struck by lightning, and one man was killed on the spot, another so seriously injured that he is not likely to recover, and three others were slightly injured. A house was also set on fire, and it and the three adjoining houses were burned down. The result of Wednesday's polling in West hdinburgli, in fill the vacancy caused by the succession of Lord Wolmer to the peerage, was declared on Thursday as inflows :—L. M'lver (L il). 3,783 ; A. W. C. 0. Murray (L), 3,075 ; majority, 708. A house well built of first-class brick will outlast one constructed of granite.</p>
        <p>WA RHIN GTON. COMING EVENTS. To-day (Saturday) sees the opening of the Man- chester Naval Exhibition in St. James' Hall. At- tractions of wonderful variety and excellence are promised for Whit week and some of the most popular entertainers known are engaged to lend their aid to the success of the exhibition ; the director of which is Mr. John Hollingshead. On the opening night at 7 p.m. there will be a grand Concert at which there will be an orchestra of fifty performers of individual merit. Each day there is to be a series of five varied concerts,recitals,and mis- cellaneous performances, so that visitors are not likely to be troubled with ennui, and may conclude that " there's a good time coming." Music IN THE PARK. The Free Methodist Church Band,Buckley-street, will render the following programme in the Park to-morrow (Sunday) :-11vmh, " Sabbath Strains," I. 11. Wright; march, "The Royal Salute," George Hamer; anthem, "God is Great," J. A. Clarke ; overture, "Eldorado," H. Round ; air vane, " Adeste Fideles," H. Round; selection, "Marino Faliero," Domzetti ; anthem, "The earth is the Lords," R A. Smith ; God Save the Queen. Mr. J. T. Richardson will be the conductor. SEWING MACHINES, Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. w.th handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTT@N, 16 and H Sankey-. • Warrington.—Advt. A STOCKTON lIEATH BUILDER AND HIS TENANT. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, bsfore his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, William Bostock, builder, of Stockton Heath, sued John Booth, 3, Dial-street,foreman-paviour in the employ of the Warrington Corporation for £4 155., a quar- ter's rent of a shop at 4, Dial-street. Mr. A. Browne appeared for plaintiff and Mr. P. Davies for defend- ant.—For the plaintiff it was contended that the defendant had agreed to take the shop let to him at the annual rental of £l9 and at his wish certain alterations had been begun in the shop by plaintiff's workmen. Subsequently it was heard that the defendant had altered his mind, and in consequence ' of this the son of the plaintiff interviewed him and gave him the option of taking a shop at Stockton Heath in lieu of that in Dial-street. If he did not take the one in Stockton ileath he was informed that legal proceedings would be taken against him for the quarter's rent.—Defendant's case was an emphatic denial that he had agreed to take the shop —Judgment was given for the defendant. ALL HOUSEHOLD LINEN, hemmed and marked, free of charge.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-et.. Warrington.—Advt. ACTION BY A LOCAL PAWNBROKER. At Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour, Judge ,Wynne Ffoulkes, ;Edgar Valen- tine Morton, pawnbroker, of Warrington, sued Thomas Peasegood, Oakham, Rutlandshire, clothes salesman, for the sum of £6, balance of an account contracted by the defendant with the plaintiff for certain goods—forfeited pledges.—The plaintiff alleged that in January, 1889, he had sold defen- dant goods amounting to £25 15s. 5d., of which sum he arranged to allow defendant 20 per cent. The defendant had paid £l4 Gs. 6d. on account, but since June 27th, 1891, nothing had been paid. It was not until June, in fact, that the defendant had disputed the claim.—Edward Green, assistant in the employ of the plaintiff,depoAed to having given defendant credit for certain instalments to the am- ount of £l4 Gs. 6d., and that until that sum had been paid no dispute had been raised by the defen- dant.—The defendant denied that he had purchased goods amounting to £25 15s. The amount of the bargain was £l5 10s., and he had refused to exceed that sum until he had sold the goods representing that amount.—His Honour said it was useless bringing forward a defence such as that after he had been paying since 1889 on the sum of £2O odd. The defendant should have given notice before that he objected to the sum of £2O, instead of paying several instalments since 1889 as he had done. He must, therefore, give judgment for the plaintiff. , ALL HOUSEHOLD LINEN, hemmed and marked free of charge.—J. and W. DUTTON, Sankey-at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>CLAM FOIt THE VALUE OF A HORSE,</head>
        <p>CLAM FOIt THE VALUE OF A HORSE,</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court an Thursday,William Leech, Lower Whitley, farmer, sought to recover from a horse dealer named William Bailey, of 11 Portland-place Rose Vale, Liverpool, £5, being the value of a horse which he had sent to the defendant to dispose of. Mr. Percy Davies appeared for the plaintiff, and observed that his client had seen an advertisement in the local paper to the following effect :—" Knackers:s,ooo horses, knackers, fat and fleshy for export purposes, must be alive, prices from £2 to £s—Bailey, 11 Portland-place, Rose Vale, Liverpool." In consequence of seeing that advertisement, txtr. Leech, who had a very handsome cart horse which had unfortunately hurt its foot, and had become useless for farm purposes wrote to the de- fendant offering to dispose of tho horse to him, and asking for terms. The defendant wrote back saying that the matter was not worth the expense of hie travelling to Whitley to see the man but that if the plaintiff would book the horse to him and let him known when the animal would arrive at Liverpool he would meet it and try to get as much as he could for it. If the horse proved good enough for expert purposes he would give the plaintiff more for it. It would suit for one or other purposes. The horse was sent by the plain- tiff and defendant was informed of the fact on Decem- er sth, 1894. The defendanttreceived the animal but no settlement had yet been made. The horse was cer- tainly fat and flebhy. The plaintiff in reply to his Honour said that he did not name in any of his letters any sum which he wanted for the horse. The defendant said that he had only be able to get £1 for the horse at a certain bone yard, as the horse WM not fit to be sent to the con- tinent. He denied that he bad sent the horse to a wild beast show. He had had to spend 7s. out of the £1 for carriage to several places. His Honour said that he would give judgment for £2 because it was apparently en the strength of the advertisement that the horse had been sent. If the horse had not been fit, why had not the defendant told plaintiff. Defendant hereupon re- plied that the horse had been sent before the advertise- int nt had been published, and his Honour said that cot.sidering this fact, he would reduce the judgment from £2 to 13s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>PROPOSED TESTIMONIAL TO MR.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED TESTIMONIAL TO MR.</p>
        <p>W. G. GRACE.</p>
        <p>It is stated that a movement is on foot to get up a national testimonial to Mr. W. G. Grace, the present time being considered ruost appropriate, as he has so re- cently completed the extraordinary feat of making his hundredth hundred in firat-class cricket. The suggestion which comes from Bristol, and is strongly supported by the Mayor and a number ot well-known citizens,is by no means confined to those who are intimately associated with cricket. Two members of the Gloucestershire Count? Club were at Lord's, on Thursday, in communi- cation with the M.C.C., who, we are informed, quite approve of the scheine. The matter is to come before the committee of the Gloucester County Club, and no doubt some further particulars will soon be forthcoming. It has already been announced that the Gloucestershire Club propose giving a banquet to Mr. Grace at Bristol, on June 24, when the Duke of Beaufort,president of the club, will take the chair.</p>
        <p>In 1594 all the rivers of North Europe were frozen before Christmas. The roar of the lion can be heard farther than the sound of any other living creature. . _</p>
        <p>The Forth- goods stati-on, Newcastle-on-Tyne, is the largest in Great Britain. On an average two thousand five hundred trucks are loaded and unloaded there daily. In 1613 no gentleman, either in England, France, or Germany, thought for a moment of going abroad with- out his cloak, even in the hottest days of summer. Ancient" coins, many of which antedate the Chris- tian area, are made in large quantities in London, and find sale all over the world.</p>
        <p>ALL HOUSEHOLD Id/rmi hemmed and marked. free 0 charge.—J. and W. Durrox, Sankey-at.. Warrington,—</p>
        <p>IN 0 tiRS u N GAM U.S AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Warrington repeated their performance of last year at Waterloo, on Saturday, and they again had to lee satis- fied with a draw with Norther's, and a draw in which neither could claim much advantage. The thunderstorm which burst over Liverpool on Saturday a little after noon delayed the match considerably and this in a great measure contributed be the undecided result.</p>
        <p>There was some good scoring in the match, and the most noticeable feature of Northern's innings was the partnership of Henson and Tunnicliffe which produced 75 runs. Previous to that Warrington had a good chance of winning the match, but this completely altered the game, and soon after Tunnicliffe's departure the Northern captain put on the closure with 146 for five wickets. The averages of the Warrington bowlers were considerably damaged by the match for Clarke with one wicket for 26 bad the best average. The fielding of the Warrington men was very good, and only two extras were given.</p>
        <p>Warrington commenced disastrously, and had lost three wickets for 34, but Podmore and Bates then came together and when time was called, they had taken the score to 113, and were still not out, Podmore being only three off half a century, and Bates having scored 40. The two batsmen were in a very lively mood, and were well set when the time for the game to close arrived.</p>
        <p>Owing to Saturday's match there ace more presentable batting averages this week than formelly, but Smith remains indisputably at the top. The following is the list of the averages of those who have played three inn- ings and have an average of thr±e and over. times</p>
        <p>innings not out runs average R. Smith Wll Podmore ... 4 1 ..... .... 55 Bates Clarke (A) 3 Callon Ci Clarke W Wordsworth . 4 E onnor 3 Bibby</p>
        <p>Warrington second suffered a severe defeat on Satur- day at the hands of Northern second. This was the Warringtonians' first defeat. Warrington were first at the wickets and realised the respectable sum of 144, but Northern scored 161 for the loss of only eight wickets. Silcock and Lowe each contributed over 30,while Cromp- ton made 21 and Ashton 18. A feature of the match was the large number of extras scored on both sides, War- rington having 14, and Northern 15. This is in striking contrast to the seniors' match, the extras being five and two, respectively.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wesleyans proved too many for both St. Helens Congregationalists and the Warrington Co- operative Stores. Strange to say, they got each out for 16, the former on Saturday and the latter on Tuesday, and gained substantial victories in each case. In the St. Helens mateb,Watkins took six wickets for six runs, and Bailey four for the same number. Watkins,however, eclipsed this on Tuesday, for he took seven wickets for only three runs He was also the top scorer for the Wesleyans, making 15 not out. Evans also batted well for 12. After the St.Helens match a tea was held ,when the more serious matter of cricket was for a time for- gotten in the lighter enjoyment of looking after the inner man.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wire Mills were exceedingly unfortunate on Saturday, for they had been looking forward to a good game with Widnes White Rose at the ehemical town, but a thunderstorm upset all their calculations, and they had to return with the match unplayed. They have, however, a chance to show their superiority when the return fixture comes off.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Friends and Penketh Wesleyans met in friendly rivalry on Saturday, but the rain was again en evidence and spoiled the game, which resulted in a draw to the advantage of the Friends. Despite the wet wicket some good cricket was displayed, and it is a pity that the game could not be brought to a definite ending.</p>
        <p>Peter Walker and Son both first and second teams had an easy time of it on Saturday, and the Y.M.C.A. first and second got severe thrashings. the first especially as they only scored 18 against their opponents 63. The Castle Rubber Company came a cropper against Hale- bank,but they console themselves with the fact of hav- ing a rather weak team. The two church teams, Trinity and St. Ann's, had a very stiff fight, and Trinity only won by eight runs.</p>
        <p>Coming to first class cricket, we find Grace again to the fore, and not content with completing his hundredth century, he has made still another double century, scor- ing 258 against Kent, and thus contributing largely to the defeat of the hop ceunty. Gloucester are going strong this year, and for this they have to thank W. G. for he appears to have recovered all his best form again, and has already scored over 800 runs in first class cricket, although the season is scarcely a mouth old.</p>
        <p>The score of W. G. Grace against Kent seems to have affected even the churches, for a cricket eathusiast who is a supporter of Kent, complains that when he went to church on Sunday, one of the hymns chosen contained the line " And grace the victory won." . The hymn was, however, rather one-sided, for Grace's big score would not have won the match had not Painter and Roberts tumbled out Kent for the small sum of 76.</p>
        <p>Surrey have challenged England,and though the Eng- lish team was by no means the best which could be had, they have badly beaten the challenger. Surrey, how- ever, will not be very down-hearted about it, for they have had a good financial result, and W. W. Read will have a handsome " testimonial." By the way, what is the difference between a testimonial and a benefit match. Why a benefit to an amateur should be politely termed a testimonial, and a testimonial to a professional should be dubbed a benefit is beyond most people's apprehen- sion.</p>
        <p>Yorkshire refused to allow Brown and Peel to take part in the match between England and Surrey because they had such an important fixture as Oxford Univer- sity. This has not, however, given them victory,for the University have won the match, and have thus had the pleasure of beating almost the strongest team Yorkshire could put on the field.</p>
        <p>Ward has at length scored 163, and is thus the first of the " Australians " to score a century since their return to England.</p>
        <p>The following is the position of the first class counties up to Wednesday night : Leicestershire</p>
        <p>Gloucestershirs 2 Notts kaneashire Yorkshi - 1 Somerset</p>
        <p>l'orkshire Middlesex Surrey 1 Sussex 2 Derbyshire 0 Warwickshire</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>S W 1111 MING.</head>
        <p>S W 1111 MING.</p>
        <p>The St. elens water polo team, the winners of the last year's championship, de not appear to have deteri- orated during the winter's vacation. On Saturday night they met the Southport team at the Boundary-road baths and inflicted the crushing defeat of 13 goals to nil. This was not due to exceptional play of either description at a certain end of the baths because 6 goals were notched in the first half and 7in the second. An average of almost two goals for each player is a very creditable result, especially when it is considered that Sharrocks was not playing. Any deficiency which might have been expected from his absence was fully made up for by Ripley who actually shot 8 of the goals himself. What the Southport goal-keeper can have been engaged in is a matter for speculation, for it is very un- usual for so many poiots to be scored against a team in a polo match. All the same it looks as if Warrington, the runners up of last year, will have to " put it all in " to enable them to overcome the bottle-makers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>Chester was intended to be the destination of the club run on Saturday,but the elements proved unfavourable, and when Frodsham was reached the 30 members pre- sent decided to stay there. The clouds and rain disap- peared after tea,the weather then being beautifully fine, and a stroll round the hills was evidently enjoyed. The summerlike weather of Tuesday had the effect of bring- ing out a larger muster for the run to Present, and quite 40 members took part, the ride being very enjoyable. The membership of the club is rapidly increasing, over 30 new members having joined during the present season.</p>
        <p>HEROISM OF A WIDNES FOOTBALLER.</p>
        <p>--- On Wednesday afternoon, James Clarke, a prominent member of the Widnes Football Club, was the means of saving the life of a boy named George Cank, five years of age, son of Thomas Cank, a labourer. of 29, Beamont- st., Widnes, from drowning in the river Mersey. It ap- pears that about 330 the lad was playing with a num- ber of other children on the promenade at West Bank, When he accidentally fell over into the water. There was a strong ebb tide running at the time, and the child was quickly being carried down the river when Clarke came upon the scene. Without any hesitation he threw off his coat, and with his other clothes and boots on, jumped into the water. Before he could reach the lad, however, the latter had sunk twice, but when he appeared again Clarke managed to grasp him, and brought him to the shore, where they were assisted out of the water by P.C. Layland and others. Clarke was in a very exhausted condition, while the lad was in a semi.conscious state. The constable applied restoratives, and the youngster was soon brought round and conveyed home. Clarke suffered from swallowing a qtruatity of the water, which is not of the purest about the Widnes Chemical Works.</p>
